window.CSA_DATA = {"domains":[{"id":"case-management","title":"Case Management","weight":25,"intro":"The largest exam domain: how a case type is modeled as a lifecycle and then made to move by itself through routing, SLAs, approvals, conditions, child cases, waits and automations. Expect scenario questions that ask which App Studio option (or which Dev Studio rule) satisfies a business requirement.","objectives":[{"objective":"Case lifecycle: stages, statuses, and instructions","modules":["Defining a Case Lifecycle","User guidance"],"summary":["A case type is the reusable model of a repeatable business transaction, and a case is one running instance of it with its own ID. The lifecycle is organized as **Stages > Processes > Steps**. A stage is a business milestone named with a noun or gerund of no more than two words (it should read naturally in the sentence 'This case is in <Stage>'). Processes and steps are named verb + noun. Every step is either an **assignment** (a person does the work) or an **automation** (the system does the work). Stages can be primary (the happy path) or alternate (exceptions such as cancellation or investigation).","Stage behavior is set in the stage properties: whether the case advances automatically when the stage finishes or waits, and whether finishing the stage resolves the case. You cannot configure transitions on the Create stage. To jump out of sequence (for example into or out of an alternate stage) use a Change Stage automation. The default Create process is a multi-step form: one assignment, one user, several views, with Next/Previous navigation and Submit on the last view. The process option 'Allow users to go back to previous step' adds a Previous button. New processes start in **draft mode**, which lets them run while incomplete; draft mode must be off before production. App Studio clears it automatically when the case type saves without errors, and turning it off by hand is a Dev Studio task.","The case status tells users where a case stands. Standard values are New, Open, Pending-Approval, Pending-Fulfillment, Resolved-Completed, Resolved-Cancelled and Resolved-Rejected. Set a status on a stage (applied on entry; the final stage holds the resolution status) or on an individual step when you need more detail. Reuse existing statuses and keep to about ten or fewer per case type. Instructions belong on steps that need user input. The form shows the step's instructions by default, and you can replace them with custom text or hide them."],"keyFacts":["Hierarchy: Case Type > Stages > Processes > Steps; a step is an Assignment (human) or an Automation (system).","Stage names: noun or gerund, at most 2 words. Process and step names: verb + noun.","Transitions cannot be configured on the **Create** stage.","The default Create process is a multi-step form: single assignment, single user, multiple views; navigation styles are horizontal, vertical or standard.","Processes are created in draft mode by default. Leaving draft mode on in production is a guardrail violation. App Studio clears it automatically on an error-free save; turning it off manually happens only in Dev Studio.","Standard statuses: New, Open, Pending-Approval, Pending-Fulfillment, Resolved-Completed, Resolved-Cancelled, Resolved-Rejected.","Best practice: no more than 10 statuses per case type, and reuse existing ones.","Status can be set on a Stage (on entry) or on a Step (more specific).","Form views show step instructions by default; override them with custom text or hide them.","In Dev Studio a new case type maps to a Work class and a pyDefault Case Type rule, and each process maps to a Flow rule."],"table":{"headers":["Element","Naming","Purpose"],"rows":[["Stage","Noun/gerund, at most 2 words","Business milestone; primary or alternate"],["Process","Verb + noun","Group of steps; Flow rule in Dev Studio"],["Step","Verb + noun","Assignment (user) or Automation (system)"],["Status","Standard values such as Pending-Approval","Where the case stands; set on stage or step"]]},"examTraps":["Stage names that are actions ('Review application') are wrong; actions belong to processes and steps.","You cannot set transitions, SLAs or escalations on the Create stage.","Draft mode is ON by default, not off. App Studio turns it off automatically; only Dev Studio turns it off manually.","Creating a new custom status when a standard one fits goes against best practice."]},{"objective":"Service level agreements: urgency, goal, deadline, and passed deadline","modules":["Completing work on time","Escalating late work"],"summary":["An SLA (called 'Goal & deadline' in App Studio) sets time milestones and raises urgency as each one passes. The **goal** is when the work should ideally be done, the **deadline** is when it becomes late, and the **passed deadline** repeats after the deadline, either a fixed number of times or until the work completes. Goal and deadline are measured from the start of the work and fire once each. Passed deadline can only be configured in Dev Studio on the SLA rule. Urgency ranges from 0 to 100. Each milestone can add 1 to 100. Once urgency reaches 100, further increments are ignored but escalation actions still run.","SLAs can sit at the case type level (from creation or reopening until resolution), the stage level (entry to exit), the process level (start until the last step finishes), and on assignment (Collect Information) and approval steps. You cannot put an SLA or escalation on the Create stage. In the case type Settings tab > Goal & deadline you choose Use SLA: Custom, Existing or Never, and Calculate time from start of: This Case, Parent Case or Top-level Case, with durations in days plus HH:MM:SS. App Studio creates a Dev Studio SLA rule behind the scenes. An existing SLA rule shows in the App Studio list only if it is marked as a relevant record.","Case urgency and assignment urgency are different values. A new assignment starts at the case urgency, and the initial case urgency is set on the case type Settings tab (default 10, set by the Work- pyDefault data transform). Stage and process SLAs change case urgency. Changing case urgency affects only assignments created afterward, not existing ones. Worklists and work queues are ordered by urgency, and Get Next Work picks the most urgent assignment. App Studio escalations can notify the case creator or participants. Dev Studio adds advanced options: initial urgency, delay timers, variable interval length, a business-day calendar, and escalation actions beyond notification such as reassigning the work or resolving the case."],"keyFacts":["Urgency range is 0 to 100. Default initial case urgency is 10. Each milestone adds 1 to 100.","At urgency 100, further increments are ignored but escalation actions still run.","Goal and deadline fire once each. Passed deadline repeats a fixed number of times or until the work completes.","Passed deadline is configured **only in Dev Studio** on the SLA rule.","Example: start 10, goal +10 gives 20, deadline +10 gives 30.","SLA levels: case type, stage, process, assignment step, approval step. Not allowed on the Create stage.","Use SLA: Custom / Existing / Never. Calculate from: This Case / Parent Case / Top-level Case.","Changing case urgency affects only new assignments, not existing ones.","Get Next Work returns the highest-urgency assignment first.","Reassigning or resolving the case as an escalation action is a Dev Studio SLA option; App Studio escalations notify (creator or participants)."],"table":{"headers":["Milestone","Meaning","Fires","Where configured"],"rows":[["Goal","Desired completion time","Once, measured from start","App Studio or Dev Studio"],["Deadline","Work is late after this","Once, measured from start","App Studio or Dev Studio"],["Passed deadline","Continued lateness after the deadline","Repeats N times or until done","Dev Studio SLA rule only"]]},"examTraps":["Urgency cannot go above 100, but escalations keep running after it gets there.","Raising case urgency does not re-rank assignments that already exist.","Passed deadline is not in the App Studio Goal & deadline panel.","'Calculate time from Parent/Top-level case' is how a child case SLA can be measured from an ancestor's start."]},{"objective":"Routing assignments to users, work groups, work queues, and by business logic","modules":["Routing Assignments to users","Creating and managing teams of users"],"summary":["Each assignment must be routed to someone. The options are the **current user**, a **specific user** (the assignment goes to that person's worklist), a **work queue** (a shared list for a team), or **business logic** (conditions pick the destination, for example sending recreational-vehicle loans to an RV underwriters queue). Worklists and work queues both list open assignments in urgency order. An assignment in a work queue stays there until a team member pulls it or a manager sends it to someone.","In App Studio a team is a work group, and in Dev Studio a user is an operator. A work group is a cross-functional team with a manager and exactly one work queue. A work queue needs a work group and a work group needs a work queue. The default queue is default@<org>. Every operator must belong to at least one work group and can belong to several. The work group manager is used for notifications, approvals, routing and escalations, and works from the Web Portal. **Authorized managers** can help transfer assignments and do not have to be work group members. They cannot perform approvals, and if they are outside the group they cannot complete its work-queue assignments. In Dev Studio a decision table can act as a router, and routing to a queue updates the Assign-Workbasket assignment."],"keyFacts":["Four routing options: current user, specific user (worklist), work queue, business logic (conditions).","Worklist = an operator's own assignments. Work queue = shared assignments for a team.","Each work group has a manager and **exactly one** work queue; neither can exist without the other.","Default work queue: default@<org>.","An operator must belong to at least one work group and can belong to many.","Work-queue items stay until a member pulls them or a manager sends them.","Authorized managers can transfer assignments, need not be members, cannot approve, and cannot complete work-queue assignments if outside the group.","Terminology: Team = Work Group, User = Operator.","Best practice is to configure people in App Studio; use Dev Studio for advanced items such as permissions."],"table":{"headers":["Routing option","Use when"],"rows":[["Current user","The person doing the work continues"],["Specific user (worklist)","One named person must do it"],["Work queue","Any qualified team member can pick it up"],["Business logic","The destination depends on case data"]]},"examTraps":["Authorized managers are not approvers.","A work group has one work queue, not several.","Routing to a work queue does not put the work on anyone's worklist until it is pulled or sent."]},{"objective":"Approvals, including cascading approvals with a reporting structure or an authority matrix","modules":["Designing an approval Process","Cascading approvals","Decision tables and trees"],"summary":["The Approve/Reject step routes like any assignment (user, work queue or business logic) and supports email approval and mobile approval, so approvers can respond without opening the application. On **approve**, the case continues or changes to a specific stage, and you can optionally set a status. On **reject**, the case resolves (default status Resolved-Rejected), continues, or changes stage.","Choosing the Cascading option creates a chain of approvals. A **reporting structure** cascade reads the operator ID record and uses either the Reporting Manager or the Workgroup Manager. The number of approvers can be One, All (the whole hierarchy), or Custom (levels decided by When rules, for example amount thresholds). An **authority matrix** is more flexible and is the right choice when at least one approver is outside the reporting structure. Its approver list is filled by a decision table, data page, activity or data transform, and approvals run in list order. If you use a decision table it must be set to **Evaluate all rows**, otherwise it returns only one result. Among decision rules, only decision tables (not decision trees) can drive an authority matrix."],"keyFacts":["Approve: continue, or change to a specific stage; optionally set a status.","Reject: resolve (default Resolved-Rejected), continue, or change stage.","Email approval and mobile approval let approvers act outside the application.","Reporting structure uses the operator record: Reporting Manager or Workgroup Manager.","Reporting structure approver count: One, All, or Custom (When rules and thresholds).","Authority matrix: use when at least one approver is outside the reporting chain.","Authority matrix sources: Decision table, Data Page, Activity, or Data Transform.","A decision table feeding an authority matrix must use Evaluate all rows; approvals happen in list order.","Decision trees cannot drive an authority matrix."],"table":{"headers":["","Reporting structure","Authority matrix"],"rows":[["Approvers come from","Operator record (Reporting Manager or Workgroup Manager)","A list built by a DT, data page, activity or data transform"],["Best for","Approvals that go up the management chain","Approvers outside the chain (e.g. Legal, Finance)"],["How many","One / All / Custom (When rules)","Every entry in the list, in order"],["Key setting","Number of approvers","DT must Evaluate all rows"]]},"examTraps":["Choosing authority matrix when every approver is in the manager chain; reporting structure is simpler.","Forgetting Evaluate all rows, so only one approver is returned.","Picking a decision tree for an authority matrix.","Assuming rejection always resolves the case; it can also continue or change stage."]},{"objective":"Identifying and handling duplicate cases","modules":["Identifying duplicate Cases"],"summary":["The **Search duplicate Cases** automation step compares the current case with existing cases in two passes. First, **basic conditions** filter candidates: a case must match ALL of them to be considered, and you can cap how many candidates are returned. Next, each candidate is scored with **weighted conditions**, each worth 1 to 100. If the total meets or exceeds the threshold, the case is a potential duplicate.","If potential duplicates are found, a user reviews them and either resolves the current case as a duplicate (status Resolved-Duplicate) or ignores the matches and continues. If nothing matches, the case continues automatically. For example: date of birth as a basic condition, four weighted conditions of 25 each, and a threshold of 50, so any two weighted matches on a same-birthdate case flag it."],"keyFacts":["Implemented with the Search duplicate Cases automation step.","Basic conditions: ALL must match for a case to be a candidate; you can limit the number of candidates.","Weighted conditions: each weight is 1 to 100; candidate is a potential duplicate when the sum is at least the threshold.","Resolving as a duplicate sets the status to Resolved-Duplicate.","The user can also ignore the matches and continue.","No matches: the case simply continues."],"table":{"headers":["Condition type","Role","Rule"],"rows":[["Basic","Filter candidates","All must be true"],["Weighted","Score candidates","Weights 1 to 100; sum compared with threshold"]]},"examTraps":["A case that matches every weighted condition but fails a basic condition is never a candidate.","The threshold check is 'at least' the threshold, so equal counts as a match.","Duplicates are not resolved automatically; a user decides."]},{"objective":"Optional actions in the case lifecycle and for data objects","modules":["Adding optional Actions to a workflow"],"summary":["Optional actions are work users can start whenever they need to, outside the planned steps. A **user action** is a single step or screen, and an **optional process** is several steps. Either can be **case-wide** (available in every stage) or limited to one **stage**. Edit details and Change Stage are built-in case-wide actions. Configure them with the Optional Actions button in the upper-right of the Workflow tab; at runtime users open them from the Actions menu. Data objects also support an Update Details action so users can change only the information they need, with validation and pre/post-processing.","The **promoted action** appears as a button (on the case header, in the summary panel) so users do not have to open the Actions menu. It is the first stage-wide action other than Edit details, or if there is none, the first case-wide action. Only user actions can be promoted, not optional processes."],"keyFacts":["User action = single step/screen. Optional process = multi-step.","Scope: case-wide or stage-only.","Built-in case-wide actions: Edit details and Change Stage.","Configured with the Optional Actions button (upper-right of the Workflow tab); run from the Actions menu.","Promoted action = first stage-wide action other than Edit details; otherwise the first case-wide action.","Only user actions can be promoted; optional processes cannot.","Data objects offer an Update Details action for targeted updates.","Change Stage is useful for moving into or out of alternate stages."],"table":{"headers":["","User action","Optional process"],"rows":[["Size","One screen","Multiple steps"],["Can be promoted","Yes","No"],["Scope","Case-wide or stage","Case-wide or stage"]]},"examTraps":["Adding the same step to every stage when a case-wide optional action does the job.","Expecting an optional process to show as the promoted button.","Edit details is skipped when the promoted action is chosen from the stage actions."]},{"objective":"Using conditions to automate workflow decisions","modules":["Automating workflow decisions","Skipping a Process or a Stage"],"summary":["Conditions let the case decide its own path from data. A condition is a field, a comparator and a value, and several conditions combine with AND. They are used in decision shapes (the orange diamond in the Process Modeler) to choose a branch, which can also leave the stage through Change Stage. They are also used in business-logic routing, custom approval levels, and stage and process skip rules. A When rule answers a single yes/no question. When several properties together determine an outcome, use a decision table. A decision shape that references a decision table automatically gets one connector per possible result.","Values that change often should not be hard-coded. Configuration settings (which replace Dynamic System Settings) can hold parameters such as an approval monetary limit and are referenced through the Condition Builder. Delegated decision tables let process owners make small production changes, such as lowering a threshold, without a developer."],"keyFacts":["Condition = field + comparator + value; multiple conditions are ANDed.","Decision shape (orange diamond) creates conditional paths and can go to another stage via Change Stage.","When rule = yes/no; decision table = outcome depends on several properties.","A decision shape referencing a DT auto-adds one connector per result.","Configuration settings replace DSS and are referenced through the Condition Builder (for example an approval monetary limit).","Delegated decision tables let business owners change values in production (e.g. a threshold from $500 to $400).","Custom levels in reporting-structure approvals are decided with When rules."],"examTraps":["Hard-coding a business threshold that the business wants to change itself.","Using a When rule where the result depends on many inputs and needs more than yes/no.","Choosing Dynamic System Settings; configuration settings are their replacement."]},{"objective":"Purpose of Flow Actions","modules":["Automating workflow decisions"],"summary":["A flow action defines what a user can do at a step. In App Studio flow actions are called user actions. A flow action is attached either to a connector or to an assignment shape. A **connector action** completes the assignment and moves the flow forward. A **local action** updates the assignment without advancing it, and is configured in the assignment properties.","Flow actions are also where step-level logic hooks in. Validate rules are assigned to flow actions. Pre- and post-processing can run data transforms (for example copying a shipping address to billing) or save a savable data page. A Validate rule can be qualified by flow action, so the same field can have different validation on different forms. Flow actions can be marked as relevant records so App Studio can reuse them."],"keyFacts":["Flow action = what a user can do at an assignment; called a User action in App Studio.","Connector action: completes the assignment and advances the flow.","Local action: updates the assignment without advancing; configured in assignment properties.","Validate rules are assigned to flow actions.","Pre/post-processing on a flow action can run a data transform or trigger a savable data page.","Flow actions (user actions) are a relevant-record rule type."],"table":{"headers":["","Connector action","Local action"],"rows":[["Completes assignment","Yes","No"],["Advances flow","Yes","No"],["Configured on","Connector leaving the assignment","Assignment properties"]]},"examTraps":["A local action never moves the case forward.","Validation for a single screen belongs to the flow action, not the property definition."]},{"objective":"Pausing and resuming processing with wait steps","modules":["Pausing and resuming Case processing"],"summary":["A Wait automation step pauses a process until something happens. A **Timer** wait resumes after a set date/time interval or at a date/time stored in a property. A **Case Dependency** wait makes a parent case wait until all or any child cases of a chosen type reach a status, or reach 'To be resolved' (any status starting with Resolved).","Case dependency options include whether to consider the child's current status or only changes made after the parent reaches the wait, and whether a user may cancel the wait. The child cases must already exist when the parent reaches the wait step; otherwise the wait is skipped. A wait step is different from a case simply sitting at an assignment until a user acts."],"keyFacts":["Two wait types: Timer and Case Dependency (case status).","Timer: set a date/time interval, or reference a date/time property.","Case Dependency: all or any child cases of a type reach a status, or 'To be resolved'.","'To be resolved' = any status beginning with Resolved.","Options: consider current status vs only changes after reaching the wait; allow user to cancel the wait.","Children must be created before the wait step, otherwise the wait is skipped.","A wait step is not the same as waiting on a user assignment."],"table":{"headers":["Wait type","Resumes when"],"rows":[["Timer (interval)","A fixed time has passed"],["Timer (reference property)","The date/time in a field is reached"],["Case dependency","All/any child cases of a type reach a status or are resolved"]]},"examTraps":["If the children already resolved before the parent arrives and you chose only changes after reaching the wait, the parent may never resume.","Creating child cases after the wait step means the wait is skipped.","Using an SLA goal as a pause; SLAs raise urgency and escalate, they do not hold the flow."]},{"objective":"Skipping a stage or process based on conditions","modules":["Skipping a Process or a Stage"],"summary":["Stages and processes can be conditional, and their defaults are opposite. A **stage** is never skipped unless you add a 'Skip stage when' condition. A **process** always starts unless you add a 'Start process when' condition. Each condition is a field, comparator and value, and multiple conditions are combined with AND, so all must be true.","Use a skip rule when whole parts of the lifecycle do not apply to some cases, such as skipping an inspection stage for low-value claims or running a reference-check process only for senior hires. If you instead need to jump to a specific stage from the middle of a flow, use a Change Stage automation or a decision shape."],"keyFacts":["Stage default: never skipped. Configure 'Skip stage when'.","Process default: always starts. Configure 'Start process when'.","Condition = field + comparator + value; multiple conditions are ANDed.","For a stage you describe when to SKIP; for a process you describe when to RUN.","Change Stage automation moves the case to a named stage out of sequence."],"table":{"headers":["","Stage","Process"],"rows":[["Default","Never skipped","Always starts"],["Condition label","Skip stage when","Start process when"],["Condition is true means","Stage is skipped","Process runs"]]},"examTraps":["The logic is inverted between stage (skip when) and process (start when).","Multiple conditions are AND, not OR."]},{"objective":"Child cases","modules":["Creating a child Case"],"summary":["A top-level case has no parent. A child case is work that must be completed to resolve the parent, and it lets work run in parallel. The parent cannot resolve until its children do. Use child cases when work is done by different parties, needs separate reporting, runs on a different time frame, or is common to several case types (for example a reusable Body Injury case). In the class hierarchy a child case type sits under the parent's work class.","Create children with the Create Case automation step. Dev Studio adds options such as creating a top-level case instead of a child and creating multiple child cases. When passing data, decide between a **snapshot copy** at creation (fine for data that won't change, such as accident details) and **referencing the parent's data** when it must stay in sync (such as a travel itinerary). Referencing is an advanced Dev Studio configuration. Related tools: the Wait step (case dependency), Update a Case (current case or all child/descendant cases), SLAs calculated from the Parent or Top-level case, and the Related Cases widget."],"keyFacts":["Top-level case = no parent. A parent waits for its children before it resolves.","Reasons: different parties, separate reporting, different time frames, reuse across case types, parallel processing.","Created with the Create Case automation step.","Dev Studio options: top-level vs child, and multiple child cases.","Data propagation copies a snapshot at creation; referencing parent data keeps it synchronized (Dev Studio advanced).","The child case type class sits under the parent's class (e.g. Laptop Request under IT Ticket).","Update a Case automation can update the current case or all child/descendant cases."],"table":{"headers":["","Copy (propagation)","Reference parent data"],"rows":[["Data","Snapshot taken at child creation","Always reads the parent's current values"],["Use for","Data that won't change (accident details)","Data that must stay synchronized (itinerary)"],["Where","Create Case step","Dev Studio (advanced)"]]},"examTraps":["Propagated data does not update in the child when the parent changes.","Child cases are for separate units of work, not just for grouping screens.","Parallel processing for a distinct unit of work suggests a child case, not more steps in the parent."]},{"objective":"Automation shapes","modules":["Automation shapes in the Case Life Cycle","Sending emails during Case processing"],"summary":["Automation steps let the system do work without a person, which saves time and reduces errors. Add them from a step with More > Automations, or in the Process Modeler with Add > Automations. App Studio automations include: Attach content, Change to a Stage, Change to next Stage, Change to previous Stage, Connect Generative AI, Create Case, Create PDF (needs a workaround in Constellation), Generate document, Persist Case (makes a temporary case permanent), Post to Pulse, Push notification, Run Data Transform, Send email, Send notification (multiple channels), Update a Case (current or all child/descendant cases), Search duplicate Cases, Wait (timer or case status), and Questionnaire. A Save Data Page step writes data back to a system of record.","In the Process Modeler, Start is a green circle, End a red circle, an assignment a green rectangle, a subprocess such as Approve/Reject a blue rectangle, an automation a yellow rectangle, and a decision an orange diamond. Automation steps do not get a form view; one is generated automatically only for user steps."],"keyFacts":["Add via Step > More > Automations, or Process Modeler Add > Automations.","Persist Case turns a temporary case into a permanent one.","Run Data Transform copies or sets data (e.g. shipping address to billing).","Send notification targets multiple channels; Send email sends email only.","Update a Case works on the current case or all child/descendant cases.","Wait pauses on a timer or a case status.","Create PDF needs a workaround in Constellation.","Shapes: Start green circle, End red circle, Assignment green rectangle, Subprocess blue rectangle, Automation yellow rectangle, Decision orange diamond.","No form view is generated for automation steps."],"examTraps":["Create Case makes a new (child) case; Persist Case keeps an existing temporary case.","Send email vs Send notification: only Send notification covers several channels.","Change to a Stage goes to a named stage; Change to next/previous Stage is relative."]},{"objective":"Decision tables and decision trees","modules":["Calculating fields using Decision tables","Decision tables and trees"],"summary":["A **decision table** checks the same set of properties across many combinations. Each column is a property or expression, and rows are evaluated top to bottom; the first row whose conditions are all true returns its result. An Otherwise row at the bottom guarantees a result. Setting **Evaluate all rows** (Results tab) returns every matching row instead, which an authority matrix needs, and disables 'Allowed to return values'. A table built in App Studio becomes a DT rule in the Work- class. A Dev Studio table must be marked as a relevant record before App Studio users can use it. Delegation lets process owners make small production changes, and delegation options on the Results tab control what they can change (for example add columns but not rows). Decision tables can also drive flow decision shapes and assignment routing.","A **decision tree** is a set of branches, each with a comparison value, operator and action (return, continue, or evaluate a nested branch), checked top-down with an otherwise value. Use a tree when different properties matter on different branches or when conditions depend on each other. Use a table when the same properties apply everywhere. The wrong choice shows up as a table full of empty cells or a tree full of repeated conditions. **Show conflicts** flags rows or branches that can never be reached (for example 'Score > 900' above 'Score > 1000'); conflicts appear as warnings on save and on the Guardrails landing page, and none are shown when Evaluate all rows is on. **Show completeness** adds rows for missing combinations, which can produce many rows that you decide whether to keep."],"keyFacts":["DT rows are evaluated top to bottom; the first row with all conditions true wins.","An Otherwise result guarantees a return value.","Evaluate all rows returns all matches, disables 'Allowed to return values', and is required for an authority matrix.","Only decision tables (not trees) support authority-matrix cascading approvals.","Table = same properties in many combinations. Tree = different properties or nested, dependent conditions.","Show conflicts finds unreachable rows/branches; warnings appear on save and on the Guardrails landing page.","Show completeness adds rows for missing conditions.","App Studio DTs are created in the Work- class; Dev Studio DTs need relevant-record marking for App Studio use.","Delegation lets business owners change values in production; Results tab controls what they can change.","Trees can be referenced from flows, Declare Expressions, activities and routers."],"table":{"headers":["","Decision table","Decision tree"],"rows":[["Best for","Same properties, many combinations","Different properties per branch, nested logic"],["Evaluation","Rows top to bottom, first full match","Branches top-down, nested"],["Symptom of wrong choice","Many empty cells","Duplicated conditions"],["Authority matrix","Supported (Evaluate all rows)","Not supported"]]},"examTraps":["A broad condition placed above a narrower one hides it; Show conflicts flags the narrower row.","Show completeness adds rows; it does not remove conflicts.","Turning on Evaluate all rows suppresses conflict reporting.","A Dev Studio DT is invisible in App Studio until it is marked as a relevant record."]},{"objective":"Personas","modules":["Inviting users to an application","Pega GenAI in Pega Platform"],"summary":["A persona is the business view of a type of user, managed in App Studio under Users > User Management > Personas tab. The default persona is 'Users'. Creating a persona also creates default permissions, a work queue and an access group (creating an access group does not create a persona). Each persona maps to a role (configured in Dev Studio), but not every role has a persona. Users are invited by email and given a persona or a developer role, and generic shared accounts such as 'admin' should not be used. Autopilot can suggest personas.","In the lifecycle's Personas & Channels section you can draft which personas use which channels in each stage and plan them into releases. Drafting does not configure access: by default a persona can reach all pages. To restrict a portal, assign the persona when you configure the portal (for example a Manager Portal). Several personas can share one channel interface."],"keyFacts":["Managed in App Studio: Users > User Management > Personas tab. Default persona: 'Users'.","Creating a persona creates default permissions, a work queue and an access group; not the reverse.","Each persona has a role, but not every role has a persona.","Persona and channel access can be set per stage; personas can share channels.","Drafting persona/channel mapping in the lifecycle does NOT restrict access; personas see all pages by default.","Restrict a portal by assigning personas when configuring the portal.","Users are invited by email with a persona or developer role; avoid generic accounts like admin."],"examTraps":["Assuming the lifecycle Personas & Channels draft enforces security.","Thinking an access group created in Dev Studio creates a persona.","Persona = business alignment; role = specific permissions. They are not the same object."]}],"questions":[{"id":"cm-001","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"In a Loan Application case type, applicants flagged during intake must move straight to a Fraud Review alternate stage. The architect opens the Create stage's properties to set up this transition. Which statement about this plan is correct?","options":["A Change to a Stage step cannot target an alternate stage.","The Create stage transition must be set to resolve on stage completion.","Stage transitions cannot be configured on the Create stage.","Alternate stages can be entered only through a Create stage transition."],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Pega does not let you configure a transition on the Create stage, so this plan fails; the usual way to reach an alternate stage is a Change to a Stage automation, which works to and from alternate stages. Resolving on stage completion is a setting for other stages, not something the Create stage requires. Alternate stages are not tied to the Create stage. Saying Change to a Stage cannot target an alternate stage reverses the fact.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-002","objective":0,"type":"multi","stem":"In a Warranty Claim case type, the Repair stage shows Open. While the 'Await parts' step is active, the case must show Pending-Fulfillment. Which two statements about this design are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Set the Pending-Fulfillment status on the Await parts step.","A status set on a Stage is applied when the case enters the stage.","Create a custom status, because Pending statuses are reserved for approvals.","A status set on a Stage is applied when the stage's last step completes."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Status can be set on a Stage or on a Step, and the Step setting is the more specific one, so the Await parts step should set Pending-Fulfillment. A Stage status takes effect when the case enters the stage. Pending-Fulfillment is a standard status, and best practice is to reuse existing statuses rather than create new ones. The claim that a Stage status applies when the last step completes has the timing wrong: it applies on stage entry, and only the final stage supplies a resolution status.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-003","objective":0,"type":"multi","stem":"Several processes in the Onboarding case type still have unconfigured steps, and go-live is two weeks away. Which two statements about draft mode are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["App Studio turns off draft mode when the case type saves without errors.","Draft mode must be off before production, or a guardrail violation results.","Draft mode stops a process from running until its configuration is complete.","Draft mode can be turned off manually only in App Studio."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Processes are created in draft mode, and leaving it on in production is a guardrail violation. App Studio turns draft mode off by itself once the case type saves with no errors. Draft processes still run even when their configuration is incomplete, which is the point of draft mode. Turning draft mode off by hand is done in Dev Studio, not App Studio.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-004","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"A Permit case type starts at urgency 10. Its SLA adds 10 at the goal and 20 at the deadline. A passed deadline in Dev Studio adds 15 and repeats 5 times. Which statement describes an unresolved case after the SLA finishes?","options":["Urgency is 115, because SLA increments are not capped.","Urgency is 100, and escalation actions stopped once urgency hit 100.","Urgency is 100, and escalation actions still ran at every interval.","Urgency is 55, because the passed deadline adds its value only once."],"answer":[2],"explanation":"10 + 10 + 20 + (15 x 5) = 115, but urgency cannot go above 100. Once urgency reaches 100, further increments are ignored, but escalation actions still run at each interval. The 115 answer ignores the cap. Saying escalations stop at 100 confuses the urgency cap with escalation behavior. A passed deadline repeats a fixed number of times or until the work is complete, so it does not add its value only once.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-005","objective":1,"type":"multi","stem":"A Permit review must warn reviewers at 2 days, be treated as late at 4 days, and then escalate daily until the review is completed. Which two statements about configuring this are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["The goal and the deadline are both measured from the start of the SLA.","The passed deadline is measured from the goal and occurs only once.","The daily escalation is a passed deadline, set on the SLA rule in Dev Studio.","The deadline can be set to repeat a fixed number of times in App Studio."],"answer":[0,2],"explanation":"A passed deadline repeats, either a fixed number of times or until the work completes, and you configure it only on the SLA rule in Dev Studio. The goal (2 days) and the deadline (4 days) are each measured from the start and each happens once. The passed deadline follows the deadline, not the goal, and it repeats. The deadline does not repeat, and App Studio cannot configure repeating escalation.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-006","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"Case CL-88 has an open Review assignment with urgency 10. A stage SLA then reaches its goal and raises the case urgency to 30. Which statement is correct?","options":["Stage SLAs change assignment urgency only, not case urgency.","The Review assignment is raised to 30 immediately.","New assignments always start at the case type's initial urgency.","The Review assignment stays at 10; new assignments start at 30."],"answer":[3],"explanation":"An assignment's initial urgency is copied from the case urgency, and a change to case urgency affects only assignments created afterward. So the open Review assignment keeps 10 and new ones start at 30. Existing assignments are not updated. Stage and Process SLA adjustments change the case urgency, so the claim that they change only assignment urgency is backwards. New assignments take the current case urgency, not the initial value from the Settings tab.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-007","objective":1,"type":"multi","stem":"An architect is adding SLAs to a Travel Request case type. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["A case-level SLA runs from creation or reopening until resolution.","An SLA cannot be set on the Create stage.","A process SLA runs from stage entry until the case leaves the stage.","Escalation can be set on the Create stage even though an SLA cannot."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"The case type SLA runs from when the case is created or reopened until it is resolved. The Create stage cannot have an SLA. Stage entry to stage exit describes a Stage SLA; a Process SLA runs from when the process is called until its last step is done. The Create stage cannot have escalation either, so the option that allows escalation there is only half right.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-008","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"Policy case POL-12 creates Claim child cases, and each Claim creates Payment child cases. Every Payment SLA must be measured from when POL-12 started. Which Goal & deadline setting should the Payment case type use?","options":["Calculate time from start of: This Case","Use SLA: Existing, pointing to the Policy SLA","Calculate time from start of: Parent Case","Calculate time from start of: Top-level Case"],"answer":[3],"explanation":"POL-12 has no parent, so it is the top-level case, and Top-level Case measures each Payment from POL-12's start. Parent Case would use the Claim, which is the Payment's direct parent. This Case would start timing when each Payment begins. Use SLA: Existing picks which SLA rule to reuse; it does not change the point that time is measured from.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-009","objective":1,"type":"multi","stem":"When the Underwriting assignment misses its deadline, it must be reassigned to a supervisor and the case participants must be notified. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["App Studio can notify the case Participants when the milestone is reached.","Escalation actions stop running once case urgency reaches 100.","App Studio escalation can reassign the work or resolve the case directly.","Reassigning the work is an extra escalation action configured in Dev Studio."],"answer":[0,3],"explanation":"In App Studio, escalation notifies the Creator or the Participants. Reassigning work and resolving the case are extra escalation actions that are only available in Dev Studio. Escalation actions keep running after urgency hits 100; only the urgency increments stop.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-010","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"Six appraisers share the 'Inspect property' step. Any of them may do it, and each one pulls the next item when free. How should the step be routed?","options":["To the current user","To the work group manager's worklist","To the Appraisal team's work queue","To a specific user chosen by business logic"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"A work queue is shared by a work group, and an assignment stays there until a member pulls it, which is exactly this requirement. Routing to the current user keeps the work with whoever is processing the case. The manager's worklist is personal to the manager. Business logic is for routing based on conditions, and sending to one specific user does not let the whole team pull from a shared pool.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-011","objective":2,"type":"multi","stem":"An architect is setting up teams for a Claims application. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Each work group has a manager and exactly one work queue.","An operator must belong to at least one work group.","The default work queue can exist without any work group.","An operator can belong to only one work group at a time."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"A work group is a cross-functional team with a manager and exactly one work queue. Every operator must belong to at least one work group, and can belong to several. A work queue always needs a work group, including the default queue.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-012","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"Lena is an authorized manager of the Billing work group but is not a member of it. Which action can Lena perform?","options":["Transfer Billing assignments to other users","Approve Billing cases routed to the work group manager","Complete assignments taken from the Billing work queue","Act as a cascading approver for Billing members"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Authorized managers help transfer assignments and do not have to be work group members. They cannot perform approvals, which rules out both approval options. Because Lena is outside the work group, she cannot complete work queue assignments either.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-013","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"A work queue holds ASG-1 (urgency 45) and ASG-2 (urgency 70). A stage SLA then raises the case urgency of ASG-1's case to 80. A user clicks Get Next Work. Which assignment is returned, and why?","options":["ASG-1, because it entered the work queue first","Either one, because Get Next Work ignores urgency","ASG-1, because its case urgency is now 80","ASG-2, because ASG-1's assignment urgency is still 45"],"answer":[3],"explanation":"Get Next Work returns the assignment with the highest urgency. A change to case urgency affects only new assignments, so ASG-1 stays at 45 and ASG-2 (70) comes first. Reading the case urgency instead of the assignment urgency is the trap. Get Next Work orders by urgency, not by time in the queue, and it does not ignore urgency.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-014","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"Expense reports under $5,000 need the requester's manager. From $5,000, the manager's manager must also approve. Above $25,000, one more level is needed. All approvers are in the reporting line. Which configuration fits?","options":["Three single-level Approve/Reject steps routed to a work queue","Cascading, reporting structure, number of approvers Custom with When rules","Cascading, reporting structure, number of approvers All","Cascading, authority matrix populated by a decision tree"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"All approvers are in the reporting line, so use a reporting structure. Custom lets When rules and thresholds decide how many levels approve. All would send every report up the whole hierarchy. An authority matrix is for approvers outside the reporting structure, and it does not support decision trees. Approve/Reject steps routed to a work queue ignore the reporting line.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-015","objective":3,"type":"multi","stem":"Contract CT-310 must be approved by Legal counsel and then by the Finance controller. Neither is in the requester's reporting line. Which two statements about the configuration are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["The approvers act in parallel, whatever their order in the list.","A decision table that populates the matrix must evaluate all rows.","Use a cascading approval based on an authority matrix.","A decision tree can populate the matrix if it returns a list."],"answer":[1,2],"explanation":"An authority matrix handles approvers outside the reporting structure. If a decision table builds the matrix, it must evaluate all rows; otherwise it returns only one approver. Approvals happen in list order, not in parallel. Authority matrix approvals support only decision tables, so a decision tree is not an option.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-016","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"An architect adds an Approve/Reject step to a Purchase Request case type and keeps the default rejection behavior. What happens when the approver rejects the request?","options":["The case returns to the previous stage for rework.","The case is resolved with the status Resolved-Cancelled.","The case is resolved with the status Resolved-Rejected.","The case continues to the next step with a Pending status."],"answer":[2],"explanation":"By default, rejection resolves the case as Resolved-Rejected. Continuing or changing to another stage are options you must configure; they are not the default. Resolved-Cancelled is a standard status, but rejection does not use it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-017","objective":3,"type":"multi","stem":"In Purchase Request PR-310, a manager approval step is being configured. Which two configurations are available? (Choose Two)","options":["Route the approval to a specific user, but not to a work queue.","On approval, change to a specific stage and set a status.","Let the approver approve from an email outside the application.","On rejection, the case must always be resolved."],"answer":[1,2],"explanation":"On approval, the case can continue or change to a specific stage, optionally setting a status. Email and mobile approval let the approver act from outside the app. On rejection, the case can be resolved, continue or change stage, so it does not always resolve. Approval routing supports a user, a work queue or business logic.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-018","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"A reporting-structure cascading approval is set to use the Workgroup Manager. Where does Pega find the first approver?","options":["From a decision table set to evaluate all rows","From the requester's operator ID record and work group","From the work queue that the approval step is routed to","From the case's Interested participants"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Reporting-structure approvals read the operator ID record, using either the Reporting Manager or the Workgroup Manager. Routing to a work queue is a different routing choice. Decision tables that evaluate all rows belong to authority matrices. Interested participants follow a case but do not process it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-019","objective":4,"type":"single","stem":"A Search duplicate Cases step uses Date of birth as its basic condition. Weighted conditions: last name 30, phone 20, email 25, postal code 25. The threshold is 50. Case A has the same DOB and matches phone and postal code. Case B has a different DOB and matches all four. Case C has the same DOB and matches last name and phone. Which cases are flagged?","options":["Cases A, B and C","Case C only","Cases A and C","Cases B and C"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A case must meet every basic condition before its weights count, so Case B is out despite matching all four weighted conditions. Case A scores 20 + 25 = 45, which is below 50. Case C scores 30 + 20 = 50, which meets the threshold.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-020","objective":4,"type":"multi","stem":"A Search duplicate Cases step in claim CL-204 finds a potential duplicate. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["The user can ignore the match and continue processing CL-204.","The earlier matching case is resolved as Resolved-Duplicate.","The user can resolve CL-204 as a duplicate, giving it Resolved-Duplicate.","CL-204 is resolved automatically once the threshold is met."],"answer":[0,2],"explanation":"Once a potential duplicate is found, the user reviews it and either resolves the current case as Resolved-Duplicate or ignores the match and continues. The step does not resolve the case on its own. The resolution applies to the current case, CL-204, not to the earlier case it matched.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-021","objective":4,"type":"single","stem":"Claims with different policy numbers must never be treated as duplicates, whatever else matches. How should the policy number be used in the Search duplicate Cases step?","options":["As a Skip stage when condition on the stage","As a basic condition","As a weighted condition worth 100","As the duplicate threshold value"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Every basic condition must match before a case can be a candidate, so a basic condition on policy number excludes all other policies. A weighted condition only adds to a score, so other matches can still reach the threshold without it. The threshold is a number to compare scores against, not a field. Skipping the stage turns off the whole check instead of excluding mismatched cases.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-022","objective":5,"type":"single","stem":"In the Inspection stage, the stage-only actions are, in order: optional process 'Request reinspection', user action 'Log photo', user action 'Update contact'. Case-wide actions are Edit details and Change Stage. Which is the promoted action in Inspection?","options":["Edit details","Log photo","Change Stage","Request reinspection"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The promoted action is the first stage action other than Edit details, but only user actions can be promoted, not optional processes. Request reinspection is an optional process, so Log photo is the first eligible action. Case-wide actions are used only when no stage action qualifies.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-023","objective":5,"type":"multi","stem":"Agents must update a customer's phone number on one screen at any point in a Service case. During the Investigation stage only, they must be able to run a multi-step escalation review. Which two configurations meet this? (Choose Two)","options":["A stage-only optional process for the escalation review","A case-wide optional process for the phone update","A case-wide user action for the phone update","A stage-only user action for the escalation review"],"answer":[0,2],"explanation":"A single-screen action is a user action, and it is case-wide because it is needed at any point. A multi-step action is an optional process, and it is stage-only because it applies only to Investigation. An optional process is for multi-step work, not a single screen. A user action covers one screen, so it cannot hold a multi-step review.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-024","objective":5,"type":"single","stem":"An architect adds a case-wide optional action in App Studio. Where is it configured, and where do users run it?","options":["Optional Actions button on the Workflow tab; the Actions menu at runtime","Process Modeler of each process; the user's worklist at runtime","Settings tab of the case type; the Utilities pane at runtime","UX tab of the case type; a button added to every View"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"You configure optional actions with the Optional Actions button in the upper right of the Workflow tab, and users run them from the Actions menu. The Settings tab holds settings such as goals and deadlines, and the Utilities pane holds widgets. The Process Modeler edits a process diagram. A promoted action appears as a header button without a button component being added, so optional actions are not placed on Views.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-025","objective":5,"type":"multi","stem":"An architect is reviewing optional actions for a Claim case type. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Only user actions, not optional processes, can be promoted.","An optional process can be promoted if it is listed first in the stage.","Edit details and Change Stage are built-in case-wide actions.","Stage-only actions appear in the Actions menu throughout the case."],"answer":[0,2],"explanation":"Edit details and Change Stage are the built-in case-wide actions, and only user actions can become the promoted action. Being listed first does not let an optional process be promoted. Stage-only actions are available only in their own stage, not throughout the case.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-026","objective":6,"type":"single","stem":"After the 'Review claim' step, suspicious claims must go to an Investigation alternate stage, and all other claims continue in the current process. How should the architect model this?","options":["A Decision shape whose suspicious path uses a Change to a Stage step","A Start process when condition on the Review process","A stage transition on the Create stage that checks the flag","A Skip stage when condition on the Investigation stage"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A Decision shape creates conditional paths, and a Change to a Stage step on one path sends the case to another stage, including an alternate stage. Skip stage when only decides whether the case skips a stage; it does not send a case to an alternate stage. Start process when decides whether a whole process runs, not which way to go after a step. Transitions cannot be configured on the Create stage.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-027","objective":6,"type":"multi","stem":"The business wants to change the auto-approval monetary limit several times a year without changing the process. Which two designs meet this? (Choose Two)","options":["Store the limit in a Dynamic System Setting that configuration sets extend.","Hold the limit in a decision table delegated to the process owner.","Enter the limit directly in the Decision shape's condition.","Reference a configuration setting for the limit in the Condition Builder."],"answer":[1,3],"explanation":"Configuration settings parameterize process paths, such as an approval limit, and the Condition Builder can reference them. A delegated decision table lets a process owner make small changes in production, such as a threshold. Configuration sets replace Dynamic System Settings rather than extend them. A value typed into the condition needs a process change every time it changes.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-028","objective":7,"type":"single","stem":"While working on the 'Prepare quote' assignment, agents sometimes record a callback note. This must not complete the assignment or move the case forward. How should the note action be configured?","options":["As a new Collect information step after Prepare quote","As a connector action on the outgoing connector","As a Wait step with a timer on the assignment","As a local action in the assignment's properties"],"answer":[3],"explanation":"A local action updates the assignment without moving it forward, and you configure it in the assignment's properties. A connector action completes the assignment and advances the flow. A new step would make the note a required, separate step. A Wait step pauses the case and does not give the user an action to take.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-029","objective":7,"type":"multi","stem":"The 'Review order' assignment has two outgoing connectors, Approve and Request changes, and a local action, Add note. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Add note advances the flow once the user submits it.","Each connector action completes the assignment and advances the flow.","A flow action defines what a user can do at a step.","Connector actions are configured in the assignment's properties."],"answer":[1,2],"explanation":"Flow actions define what a user can do at a step. A connector action completes the assignment and follows its connector. A local action such as Add note updates the assignment without advancing it. You configure local actions, not connector actions, in the assignment's properties; connector actions belong to the connectors.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-030","objective":8,"type":"single","stem":"Purchase case PP-55 creates Inspection child cases in stage 2. In stage 4 it must pause until every Inspection is resolved, however it was resolved. Some may already be Resolved-Completed. Which Wait step configuration fits?","options":["Case dependency on any Inspection reaching Resolved-Completed","Timer referencing the date of the last inspection","Case dependency on all Inspections, To be resolved, checking current status","Case dependency on all Inspections, Resolved-Completed, checking only later changes"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"To be resolved matches any status that starts with Resolved, and All waits for every child. Checking the current status counts children that were resolved before the case reached the wait. The any option resumes after one child. Checking only later changes would miss children that were already resolved, and it also leaves out other Resolved statuses. A timer has nothing to do with the children's status.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-031","objective":8,"type":"single","stem":"In case ORD-77, a Wait step for Shipment child cases runs, but the Create Case step that creates the Shipments comes later in the process. What happens at the Wait step?","options":["The Wait step switches to a timer wait.","The case waits until Shipment child cases are created.","The Wait step is skipped because no child cases exist yet.","The case waits until a user cancels the wait."],"answer":[2],"explanation":"A case dependency wait needs the child cases to exist before it runs; if they do not, the wait is skipped. It does not wait for future children, does not change into a timer, and does not hold the case waiting for someone to cancel.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-032","objective":8,"type":"multi","stem":"An architect is choosing between Wait step types. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["A Wait step is needed to pause until a user completes an assignment.","A case dependency wait can let a user cancel the wait.","A timer wait ends early when its children reach To be resolved.","A timer wait can reference a date/time property on the case."],"answer":[1,3],"explanation":"A timer can use a set interval or a referenced date/time property. A case dependency can let a user cancel the wait. To be resolved is a case dependency setting, not a timer setting. A case already waits for a user to act on an assignment, so that delay is not a Wait step.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-033","objective":8,"type":"single","stem":"A Recall case creates three Supplier Notice child cases. The parent may continue as soon as any supplier confirms, which resolves that child as Resolved-Completed. Which Wait step configuration fits?","options":["Case dependency on any Supplier Notice reaching To be resolved","Timer set to an interval long enough for one reply","Case dependency on any Supplier Notice reaching Resolved-Completed","Case dependency on all Supplier Notices reaching Resolved-Completed"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Any with Resolved-Completed resumes the parent as soon as one supplier confirms. All would wait for every supplier. To be resolved matches every status that starts with Resolved, so a rejected or cancelled notice would also resume the parent. A timer cannot tell whether anyone replied.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-034","objective":9,"type":"single","stem":"A Review stage has two 'Skip stage when' conditions: Order total < 100 and Customer tier = Gold. ORD-1 is $80 and Silver, ORD-2 is $150 and Gold, and ORD-3 is $60 and Gold. Which orders skip the stage?","options":["All three orders","ORD-2 and ORD-3","ORD-3 only","ORD-1 and ORD-3"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"When several conditions are listed, all of them must be true (AND). Only ORD-3 is both under 100 and Gold. ORD-1 meets only the total condition, and ORD-2 meets only the tier condition. Treating the conditions as OR would wrongly skip all three.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-035","objective":9,"type":"multi","stem":"In a Hiring case, the Background Check stage applies only to external candidates. In the Review stage, a Reference Check process runs only for senior roles. Which two configurations are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Nothing on Background Check, because stages are skipped by default","A Skip stage when condition on Background Check for internal candidates","A Start process when condition on Reference Check for senior roles","A Skip process when condition on Reference Check for senior roles"],"answer":[1,2],"explanation":"A stage is never skipped by default, so Background Check needs a Skip stage when condition for the cases that should not enter it. A process always starts by default, so Reference Check needs a Start process when condition for the cases that should run it. Processes use Start process when, and skipping for senior roles would reverse the requirement anyway.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-036","objective":9,"type":"single","stem":"An Audit process has the condition 'Start process when Claim amount > 5000'. Claim CL-9 is for 3000. What happens to the Audit process for CL-9?","options":["Audit starts anyway, because processes always start by default.","Audit starts and is then resolved automatically.","The Audit process does not start for CL-9.","The whole stage containing Audit is skipped for CL-9."],"answer":[2],"explanation":"A process always starts unless it has a condition. Here the condition is false, so Audit does not start. A process condition affects only that process, not its stage. The default to always start applies only when no condition is set. No automatic resolution is involved.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-037","objective":10,"type":"multi","stem":"An architect is designing an Auto Claim case type. In which two situations is a child case the right choice? (Choose Two)","options":["Body Injury work that other claim case types also need","A single review screen the adjuster completes before payment","A form section that collects the driver's details","Repair work done by another party on a different time frame"],"answer":[0,3],"explanation":"Child cases suit work that several case types share, work done by different parties or on different time frames, separate reporting, and parallel processing. A single review screen is an assignment step. Collecting driver details is a form, not a separate piece of work.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-038","objective":10,"type":"single","stem":"A Business Trip parent case creates Hotel Booking child cases. The trip itinerary on the parent changes often, and every child must always show the current itinerary. Which approach should the architect use?","options":["Have each child reference the parent's itinerary data","Copy the itinerary into each child when it is created","Add a Wait step to each child until the itinerary is final","Create each Hotel Booking as a top-level case"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"When data must stay in sync, the child should reference the parent's data, which is an advanced Dev Studio setup. Copying gives a snapshot from creation time that goes stale when the itinerary changes. A Wait step only delays the child and does not keep data in sync. A top-level case has no parent to read from.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-039","objective":10,"type":"single","stem":"An Accident Claim parent creates a Vehicle Damage child that copies the accident details when it is created. The parent's accident description is corrected later. What does the child show?","options":["No description, because children cannot use parent data","Both descriptions, kept in the child's case history","The original description copied when the child was created","The corrected description, because children always read parent data"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Copying data at creation takes a snapshot, so later changes on the parent do not reach the child. Children read live parent data only when they are set to reference it. The child does not keep both values. Children can receive parent data, by copy or by reference.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-040","objective":10,"type":"multi","stem":"An architect is configuring child cases for a Mortgage case type. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Choosing a top-level case instead of a child is configured in Dev Studio.","A top-level case becomes the parent of the case that created it.","Creating several child cases in one step is an App Studio-only option.","The parent case cannot be resolved until its child cases are resolved."],"answer":[0,3],"explanation":"Dev Studio offers the choice between a top-level case and a child case, and the option to create multiple child cases. The parent must wait for its children to be resolved before it can be resolved. Creating multiple child cases is configured in Dev Studio, not App Studio. A top-level case has no parent and is not a child of the case that created it.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-041","objective":11,"type":"multi","stem":"After 'Capture shipping', the system must copy the shipping address to billing, alert the customer by email and push notification in one step, and move the case into the Fulfillment stage. Which three automation steps are needed? (Choose Three)","options":["Run Data Transform","Send notification","Change to a Stage","Send email"],"answer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"Run Data Transform copies the shipping address into the billing fields. Send notification reaches several channels, such as email and push, in one step. Change to a Stage moves the case directly into Fulfillment. Send email covers only email, so the push notification would be missed.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-042","objective":11,"type":"single","stem":"A quick-quote case is created as a temporary case so that abandoned quotes are not stored. If the customer accepts, the case must be kept permanently. Which automation step does this?","options":["Persist Case","Update a Case","Create Case","Change to next Stage"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Persist Case turns a temporary case into a permanent one. Create Case starts a new case. Update a Case sets values on the current case or on its child and descendant cases. Change to next Stage moves the case forward but does not make it permanent.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-043","objective":11,"type":"single","stem":"When a Recall case is cancelled, a field on all of its child and descendant cases must be set to 'Recalled'. Which automation step does this?","options":["Create Case with the field prefilled","Update a Case, targeting all child and descendant cases","Run Data Transform on the Recall case","Post to Pulse on each child case"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Update a Case can update the current case or all of its child and descendant cases. A Run Data Transform on the Recall case changes only that case. Create Case would make new cases instead of updating the existing ones. Post to Pulse sends a message and does not change field values.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-044","objective":12,"type":"single","stem":"A pricing decision table has, in order: Row 1, UnitPrice < 50 returns Standard. Row 2, UnitPrice < 25 returns Budget. Otherwise returns Premium. The architect clicks Show conflicts. What is reported?","options":["Row 2 is unreachable, because Row 1 catches every price under 25.","Nothing, because the most specific row is evaluated first.","Row 1 is unreachable, because Row 2 is more specific.","The table is incomplete, because it has no Otherwise result."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Rows are evaluated from top to bottom, and the first row whose conditions are all true wins. Every price under 25 is also under 50, so Row 1 always matches first and Row 2 can never be reached. Pega does not reorder rows by how specific they are. The table does have an Otherwise result. Row 1 is reachable for prices from 25 to 49.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-045","objective":12,"type":"single","stem":"Gold customers' discount depends on region, Silver customers' discount depends on the number of past orders, and Bronze customers always get 0%. Which rule type fits best, and why?","options":["A decision table, because it handles nested conditions best","A When rule, because each result is a yes or no","A decision table with Evaluate all rows to fill empty cells","A decision tree, because conditions use different properties"],"answer":[3],"explanation":"A tree suits nested conditions that depend on different properties, and a table built for this logic would be full of empty cells. Tables suit the same set of properties evaluated across many combinations. A When rule returns only true or false, not a discount. Evaluate all rows returns every matching row; it does not fill empty cells.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-046","objective":12,"type":"multi","stem":"A Shipping case type uses a decision table to choose the shipping method. The process owner must adjust thresholds in production. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Turning on Evaluate all rows keeps Allowed to return values enabled.","Delegation options on the Results tab control what the owner can change.","Show conflicts still reports unreachable rows with Evaluate all rows on.","Rows are evaluated top to bottom, and the first fully true row returns."],"answer":[1,3],"explanation":"A table returns the first row from the top whose conditions are all true. You set delegation options on the Results tab, for example allowing the owner to add columns but not rows. Evaluate all rows disables Allowed to return values. With Evaluate all rows on, no conflicts are reported because every row is checked.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-047","objective":12,"type":"multi","stem":"An architect is choosing between a decision table and a decision tree. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["A tree is preferred when every condition uses the same properties.","Show completeness adds rows for missing cases, and the user decides which to keep.","An authority matrix cascading approval supports only decision tables.","Using a tree for table-shaped logic leaves many empty cells."],"answer":[1,2],"explanation":"Authority matrix approvals work only with decision tables. Show completeness adds rows for missing conditions, which can be many, and the user decides what to keep. When the conditions use the same properties, a table is the better choice. Empty cells come from forcing tree-shaped logic into a table; forcing table-shaped logic into a tree causes repeated conditions instead.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-048","objective":12,"type":"single","stem":"Citizen developers in App Studio cannot select a decision table built in Dev Studio when they configure a calculated field. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Calculated fields support only functions and expressions.","The table must first be saved into the Work- class.","The decision table is not marked as a relevant record.","Decision tables can be referenced only from Dev Studio."],"answer":[2],"explanation":"A Dev Studio decision table appears in App Studio only after it is marked as a relevant record. App Studio calculated fields can use decision tables, along with functions and expressions. Tables created in App Studio are stored in a Work- class, but the class is not what controls visibility; the relevant record mark does.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-049","objective":13,"type":"single","stem":"In the lifecycle's Personas & Channels section, the architect drafted that the Manager persona uses only the Manager Portal. In testing, managers can still open every page of the Web Portal. What is the fix?","options":["Assign the Manager persona when configuring the Manager Portal.","Create a new Access Role for the persona in App Studio.","Remove the Web Portal, because personas cannot share channels.","Set persona and channel access on the Create stage only."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Drafting personas and channels in the lifecycle does not set channel access, and by default a persona can reach all pages. You restrict a portal by assigning the persona when you configure that portal. Access Roles are a Dev Studio concept and do not fix the drafting gap. Persona and channel access can be set per stage, not only on the Create stage. Personas can share channels.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-050","objective":13,"type":"multi","stem":"An architect creates a new 'Claims Supervisor' persona in App Studio. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["An Access Group is created automatically for the persona.","Every Access Role in Dev Studio has a matching persona.","Creating an Access Group in Dev Studio creates a matching persona.","Default permissions and a work queue are created for the persona."],"answer":[0,3],"explanation":"Creating a persona creates default permissions, a work queue and an Access Group. It does not work the other way: creating an Access Group does not create a persona. Each persona is linked to a role, but not every role has a persona.","difficulty":"hard"}]},{"id":"data-integration","title":"Data and Integration","weight":20,"intro":"The second-largest domain: how Pega models data (fields, data objects, relationships), keeps it correct (calculations, validation, defaults), and connects it to systems of record through data pages, refresh strategies, savable data pages and simulation. Expect scenario questions that hinge on one configuration word such as Refer vs Copy, Thread vs Node, or Lookup vs Data Transform.","objectives":[{"objective":"Explain the data model and its levels (conceptual, logical, physical)","modules":["The Data Model"],"summary":["A data model is a picture of the data an application needs and how the pieces connect. It pays off in reuse, easier maintenance and the ability to adapt and scale; in practice the Lead System Architect reviews it before any data objects are created. Pega describes three levels of refinement. The conceptual model is drafted by the Business Architect with stakeholders and names entities, their attributes and the relationships between them; it is a living document whose purpose is to cut rework later. The logical model translates that picture into Pega terms: entities become data objects and attributes become fields. The physical model decides how data is stored and accessed, which is where integration settings live.","In App Studio the Integration Designer is the single place to see data objects, data pages, their dependencies and the external systems behind them, with an optional Integration Map diagram on top. The physical level is visible there. Each data object also has a visual Data Model diagram where fields can be added, changed or removed. All of this supports the center-out idea: the data access layer is insulated from business logic, so moving a system of record (for example from on-premises to the cloud) does not force changes to the logic or the views."],"keyFacts":["Conceptual model: owned by the Business Architect and stakeholders; entities, attributes, relationships; living document; reduces rework.","Logical model: Pega-specific refinement; entity maps to a data object, attribute maps to a field.","Physical model: storage and access details plus integration settings; surfaced in the Integration Designer.","The LSA verifies the data model before data objects are created.","Integration Designer (App Studio) = one place for data objects, data pages, dependencies and external systems; Integration Map is optional.","Data pages decouple integration from how the application uses data, so a source can change without changing views.","Center-out: data access layer insulated; changing the system of record does not require logic changes.","Benefits named for a data model: reuse, maintenance, adaptability/scalability."],"table":{"headers":["Level","Who / focus","Pega artifact"],"rows":[["Conceptual","Business Architect + stakeholders; entities, attributes, relationships","Living design document (no rules yet)"],["Logical","Refined for Pega","Data objects (entities) and fields (attributes)"],["Physical","How data is stored and accessed","Integration settings, data pages, external systems in Integration Designer"]]},"examTraps":["Mapping entities to data objects is the logical level, not the conceptual one; the conceptual level is still tool-neutral.","Integration settings and storage choices belong to the physical model; do not pick 'logical' just because data objects are mentioned.","The conceptual model is not frozen after sign-off; it is described as a living document."]},{"objective":"Describe data types, data objects, relationships, data records and field types","modules":["Capturing and presenting data","The Data Model","Creating a data relationship"],"summary":["A field is a named, reusable element that holds one value per case (Dev Studio calls it a property). App Studio guesses the type from the name you type (default Text (single line); a name containing 'date' becomes Date only), so always check before submitting. Type choice matters: Integer drops leading zeros, so IDs and postal codes that are never used in arithmetic should be Text; money should be Currency (codes and separators) rather than Decimal; Date & time values are normalized to UTC/GMT; Email, Phone and URL render as action links and Email/URL include validation; Percentage shows .5 as 50%; Address adapts its fields to the selected country; User reference points at operators mapped to the app. Fancy types include Attachment and Location.","A data object is a template that groups related fields together with their views, rules and calculations, and can be reused across case types and even several times in one case (source and target account). Objects can inherit (Person-Customer) and can be sourced from a local Pega system of record, an external SOR, or nothing at all when the case captures the data. Best practice: use a standard object such as Address-Postal or Address-Email, extend one that partially fits (Person-Employee), and only then create a new one. A data object added from the workflow starts as a draft and must be made permanent on the Data objects and integrations page. A data record is one instance of a data object; locally it is a row in a database table shown on the Records tab. Changing records is a data change, while changing the fields is a process change. Records from an external source are not stored in Pega.","Relationships are containers that relate data without storing it themselves. Embedded data keeps user-entered data inside the case in a consistent format. Data reference reuses a data object that lives outside the case (local records or external SOR). Case reference pulls data from another case. Query is a read-only, parameterized lookup of external data. Each can be a single record or a list. Data reference supports Single, List and Many to Many modes."],"keyFacts":["Default inferred field type is Text (single line); names containing 'date' become Date only.","Integer strips leading zeros; use Text (single line) for IDs and zip codes without arithmetic.","Use Currency, not Decimal, for money; Date & time is stored normalized to UTC/GMT.","Email, Phone and URL show as action links; Email and URL include built-in validation.","Data object sourcing: local Pega SOR, external SOR, or none (entered in the case).","Reuse order: standard object (Address-Postal, Address-Email) -> extend a partial fit (Person-Employee) -> create new.","Draft data objects created from the workflow must be made permanent on the Data objects and integrations page.","Editing records = data change; adding/changing fields = process change.","Embedded list UI: Table or Field Group; Case reference UI: Autocomplete, Dropdown, Table; Data reference list UI: Table, Simple table, Combobox, Checkbox group.","Query fields need a list or lookup data page, use external parameters, and do not store the key."],"table":{"headers":["Relationship field type","Where the data lives","Typical example"],"rows":[["Embedded data","Inside the case (entered by the user)","Payment information (single); Ordered items (list)"],["Data reference","Outside the case: local records or external SOR","Ordering customer; Product selected in a line item"],["Case reference","Another case","Related policy case shown in a claim"],["Query","External, read-only, parameterized via a data page","Lookup that is displayed but not stored by key"]]},"examTraps":["Numeric-looking identifiers (badge numbers, zip codes) are a trap for Integer: leading zeros are lost.","Embedded data copies values into the case; for shared catalog-style data that lives elsewhere, pick Data reference.","Query is read-only and does not store the key, so it is wrong when the user's selection must be saved.","Draft data objects are not automatically permanent."]},{"objective":"Configure calculated values","modules":["Capturing and presenting data"],"summary":["App Studio lets a field be calculated instead of entered, in either a case type's or a data object's data model. There are three calculation kinds. Functions iterate over a list and return the Sum, Average, Maximum or Minimum of a field; they only produce Decimal, Currency or Integer results. Expressions combine fields with the dot operator, arithmetic (+ - * /), parentheses and AND/OR, and can concatenate text with quoted literals; they can return any simple type except Email, Phone and Picklist. Decision tables map conditions to a result.","Calculated fields form a calculation network: when an input changes, every dependent field recalculates automatically. Creating a custom decision table from App Studio is only available in Constellation applications; a decision table built in Dev Studio can be selected in App Studio only after it is marked as a relevant record. Decision tables evaluate rows top to bottom, return the first row whose conditions are all true, and use an 'otherwise' result as a guaranteed fallback."],"keyFacts":["Three calculation types in App Studio: Functions, Expressions, Decision tables.","Functions iterate a list: Sum of, Average of, Maximum of, Minimum of.","Function results limited to Decimal, Currency and Integer.","Expressions: dot operator, + - * /, parentheses, AND/OR, text concatenation with quotes.","Expressions can return any simple type except Email, Phone and Picklist.","Custom decision tables in App Studio: Constellation apps only.","Dev Studio decision tables must be marked relevant records to be used in App Studio.","The calculation network recalculates dependent fields automatically when inputs change.","Decision table: first matching row wins (top to bottom); 'otherwise' guarantees a result."],"table":{"headers":["Calculation type","Use it for","Restriction"],"rows":[["Function","Aggregate a list (sum, average, max, min)","Result must be Decimal, Currency or Integer"],["Expression","Arithmetic/logic on fields; concatenation","Not for Email, Phone, Picklist results"],["Decision table","Condition combinations mapped to a result","Custom DT in App Studio only for Constellation; Dev Studio DT must be relevant"]]},"examTraps":["Totaling a field across an embedded list is a Function (Sum of), not an Expression.","A Picklist result cannot come from an Expression even though Text can.","In a Traditional UI app, a missing App Studio option to build a decision table is expected behaviour, not a permissions problem."]},{"objective":"Apply validation rules and business logic to data","modules":["Validating data against business logic","Validating data in Dev Studio"],"summary":["Validation answers three questions: is the value the right type (handled by field type and control), is it one of the allowed values (lists/picklists), and does it make business sense (conditions). A calendar control guarantees a real date, but not that the date is in the past; that needs a business condition. In App Studio, a validation condition describes the invalid case and produces an error that blocks progress until fixed. The comparison can be against a constant or another field. Validations attach to a specific step instance, so the same field can carry different rules on different forms (a previous job's start date must be past; a new hire's start date must be future).","App Studio offers two trigger points: on form submit, where the user can correct the value immediately, and on stage entry, configured in the case type's Data model, which stops the case from entering the stage. With stage-entry validation the developer must give the user a way out, such as going back to a previous stage or closing the case. In Dev Studio, Validate rules (Process category) are attached to flow actions and extend App Studio validations; their Input tab can qualify when they run by input property, proposed work status, flow action or stage (stage-based equals App Studio stage-entry validation). Edit Validate rules use Java pattern checks on a property and are a security risk because of custom Java; set on the property's Advanced tab they run client-side as soon as the value changes, or they can be called from a Validate rule to run on submit. A library of validation functions covers checks like 'date within the past N weeks' or 'attachment of a category is present'."],"keyFacts":["Three validation needs: correct type, allowed values, business logic.","Calendar control ensures a valid date, not a past/future date.","App Studio validation conditions define the INVALID values.","Validations are tied to process step instances, so one field can have different validation per form.","Two App Studio trigger points: form submit and stage entry (stage entry configured in the case type Data model).","Stage-entry failures block entry; the designer must provide a recovery path.","Validate rules: Dev Studio, Process category, attached to flow actions; Input tab qualifies by property, proposed work status, flow action, stage.","Edit Validate rules: custom Java pattern checks (security risk); client-side immediately when set via 'Use Validate' on the property Advanced tab.","Validation function library: e.g., date within past N weeks, attachment of a category attached."],"table":{"headers":["Mechanism","When it fires","Configured in"],"rows":[["Form-submit validation","User submits the step's form","App Studio (step/view)"],["Stage-entry validation","Case tries to enter the stage","App Studio case type Data model (stage-based Validate rule)"],["Validate rule","Flow action submit, qualified on Input tab","Dev Studio"],["Edit Validate on property","Client-side on value change","Dev Studio property Advanced tab"]]},"examTraps":["Required = Always forces a value but does not check business meaning.","Stage-entry validation is not 'immediate feedback on the form' — that is form-submit validation.","Edit Validate rules are not best practice for general logic: custom Java weakens security."]},{"objective":"Use data transforms and set default values (pyDefault, Data initialization, superclass)","modules":["Application data manipulation","Application data manipulation in Dev Studio"],"summary":["Data transforms copy and convert data (for example a text 'December 4, 2021' into a date), move it, calculate values, format it, and set defaults. In App Studio they run from a Run Data Transform automation step or as pre- or post-processing on a step; a classic case is post-processing on a shipping-information step that copies the shipping address to billing. In Dev Studio a flow connector can apply a data transform (Set properties > Apply Data Transform), and transforms can loop through lists, for example copying only child items with quantity above zero to the parent.","Defaults are best set on the Data initialization page of the case type Settings tab, which writes into the pyDefault data transform; use pyDefault directly for complex logic. pyDefault runs when the case is created and calls pySetFieldDefaults. pySetFieldDefaults runs when a view loads and is maintained by the system, so do not edit it. Both are generated the first time you create a view. To read operator details such as pyFirstName in a Dev Studio transform, the OperatorID page must be declared on the Pages & Classes tab. Functions such as @CurrentDateTime() and @addCalendar(...) help compute values.","The superclass feature chains same-named transforms through the class hierarchy. When 'Call superclass data transform' is checked, the runtime finds each parent transform up to the highest class and runs them top-down, so the most specific class runs last and its values win. It is turned on automatically when App Studio created default fields. Work- pyDefault sets status, urgency 10 and organization info; a case type pyDefault can override, for example urgency 40."],"keyFacts":["Data transform uses: copy/convert types, move data, calculate, format, set defaults.","App Studio entry points: Run Data Transform automation, step pre-/post-processing.","Dev Studio: apply a transform on a flow connector (Set properties > Apply Data Transform).","Best practice for simple defaults: Data initialization page (Settings tab) which updates pyDefault.","pyDefault runs at case creation and calls pySetFieldDefaults.","pySetFieldDefaults runs on view load, is system-maintained and should not be modified.","Both pyDefault and pySetFieldDefaults are created the first time a view is created.","OperatorID page must be on the Pages & Classes tab to reference pyFirstName/pyLastName.","Superclass chaining: parents run top-down, subclass values override; Work- pyDefault sets urgency 10.","For parent-child data, referencing avoids the synchronization issues of copying."],"examTraps":["Do not customize pySetFieldDefaults for complex logic; use pyDefault.","With superclass enabled the subclass runs last, so its value (e.g., urgency 40) wins over Work- (10).","A reference to OperatorID failing in a transform is usually a missing Pages & Classes entry, not a relevant-record issue."]},{"objective":"Configure sourced data: data pages, scope, sources, parameters, refresh strategies, refer vs copy, and populating UI controls","modules":["Accessing sourced data in a Case"],"summary":["Data pages (prefix D_) retrieve and cache data and keep integration details out of the process; they also support persistence and services. They load on demand when referenced: the system checks the cache, queries the source if the page is missing or stale, and returns the result. A data object with a system of record gets three default pages: a list (D_xxxList), a single-record lookup (D_xxx) and a savable page (D_xxxSavable). The definition sets the structure (Page or List, with list items in pxResults), the object type (class), the edit mode (Read-Only, Editable, or Savable with a save plan) and the scope: Thread (per case/transaction), Requestor (per user session) or Node (shared by all users).","Sources include Data Transform, Activity, Connector, Report definition, Database lookup, Robotic Automation, Robotic desktop Automation, Lookup and Aggregate sources (several sources run in listed order). With multiple conditional sources the first true condition wins and the last is the Otherwise. Parameters narrow the content (lookup key, report filter) using D_Name[p1:v1,p2:v2]; each distinct parameter combination is its own instance unless 'Limit to a single data page' is set on the Load Management tab. Parameters have a name, data type, required flag and In/Out direction.","Refresh strategies are optional but strongly recommended; the page reloads if any configured condition is met, and never more than once per interaction. 'Reload once per interaction' works only for Thread or Requestor scope and cannot be combined with other options. 'Do not reload when' treats the page as current while its When condition is true. 'Reload if older than' marks the page stale after an interval, and it reloads on the next access. A property can Refer to a data page (always current, no copy held by the case, a parameter change points it at a new instance) or Copy data from it (a point-in-time snapshot stored in the case, re-retrieved only when parameters change). For list controls such as dropdowns, only the selected value is copied to the case, and it can differ from the displayed value."],"keyFacts":["Default data pages for a data object with a SOR: D_xxxList, D_xxx (lookup), D_xxxSavable.","List structure stores rows in pxResults, e.g., D_X.pxResults(n).","Scope: Thread = per case/transaction; Requestor = per user session; Node = shared by all users.","Multiple sources: conditions evaluated in order, first true wins, last is Otherwise; Aggregate sources run in listed order.","Each parameter combination creates a separate instance unless 'Limit to a single data page' (Load Management tab).","Refresh: reload if ANY condition is met, never more than once per interaction.","'Reload once per interaction' is Thread/Requestor only and cannot be combined with other options.","'Reload if older than' does not reload in the background; it reloads on the next access after going stale.","Refer = always current, nothing stored in case; Copy = snapshot stored, refreshed only when parameters change.","List controls copy only the selected value; Constellation cascading dropdowns use two Picklist fields and a parameterized data page."],"table":{"headers":["Setting","Option","Behavior / example"],"rows":[["Scope","Thread","One per case/transaction (stock price for this transaction)"],["Scope","Requestor","One per user session (this customer's shipping addresses)"],["Scope","Node","Shared by all users (vehicle makes and models)"],["Refresh","Reload once per interaction","Thread/Requestor only; cannot combine (flight seating)"],["Refresh","Do not reload when","When true, page is current (stocks after market close)"],["Refresh","Reload if older than","Stale after interval; reloads on next access"],["Autopopulate","Refer to a data page","Always current; no copy in case"],["Autopopulate","Copy data from a data page","Snapshot stored in case; re-fetch only when parameters change"]]},"examTraps":["'Reload once per interaction' on a Node-scoped page is invalid.","Refresh strategies are optional (though strongly recommended); a missing strategy is not a save error.","If the case must remember values as they were at a point in time, choose Copy, not Refer.","Parameterized pages create one instance per parameter set; this is expected, not a leak."]},{"objective":"Save data to a system of record (savable data pages, save plans, Save Data Page automation)","modules":["Saving data to a system of record"],"summary":["A savable data page writes a page or page list back to a system of record, inside Pega or external, in real time with transaction handling, so a failure rolls back both the case and the data save. The save plan lists save options, each with a When condition and an Otherwise: Database save (one page to one row, page structure only, transactional, mapped with a data transform), Database update, Database delete, Connector (REST only, not SOAP), Robotic automation (unattended RPA), Robotic desktop automation (RDA) and Activity (custom code; can call any connector including SOAP; not inherently transactional, so use sparingly).","The save is triggered by a Save Data Page automation step placed after the assignment, by post-processing on a flow action, or by the Save-DataPage method in an activity (which can choose the same or a separate transaction). In the Save Data Page step you either name the data page and its parameters explicitly or pick 'Use associated property' to take the page and parameters from an autopopulated property; one step per data source, and you can add an audit note. If a property is autopopulated from a savable data page, use Copy, not Refer, to avoid overwriting. To support both create and update, make the key parameter optional: when a key is supplied a Lookup source loads the existing record; when it is not, a Data Transform source initializes a new one. Best practice is to give savable pages used for creation an Index parameter (unless autopopulating by copy), which avoids a guardrail warning and allows multiple uses on one screen. Data objects also offer an Update Details action for changing only the needed information."],"keyFacts":["Savable data pages save in real time with transaction handling; failures roll back case and data together.","Database save: single page to single row; page structure only; transactional.","Connector save option supports REST only; SOAP requires the Activity option.","Activity save option is not inherently transactional; limit its use.","Triggers: Save Data Page step (after the assignment), flow action post-processing, Save-DataPage activity method.","Save Data Page step: explicit page + parameters, or 'Use associated property'; one step per source; optional audit note.","Autopopulated property backed by a savable page: use Copy, not Refer.","Update existing: key present -> Lookup source. Create new: no key -> Data Transform source. Key optional for both.","Best practice: Index parameter on savable pages that create records (avoids guardrail, multiple uses on a screen)."],"table":{"headers":["Save option","Notes"],"rows":[["Database save","One page -> one row; page structure only; transactional; data transform mapping"],["Database update / delete","Update or remove existing rows"],["Connector","REST only"],["Robotic automation / RDA","Unattended RPA / desktop automation"],["Activity","Custom; any connector incl. SOAP; not inherently transactional"]]},"examTraps":["Connector save option does not support SOAP.","Database save does not handle page lists into multiple rows; it is one page to one row.","Using Refer with a savable page risks overwriting user edits; Copy also helps avoid stale page errors.","Multiple target systems mean multiple Save Data Page steps, not one step with several sources."]},{"objective":"Simulate data for development and testing","modules":["Accessing sourced data in a Case","The Data Model","Pega GenAI in Pega Platform"],"summary":["When an external system is not available yet, has no data, or its API is still being built, a data page can use a simulated data source as a temporary stand-in. You need to know the expected request and response so the simulation matches reality. Simulation lets the UI team keep working and is useful for unit testing because you control the response. It must be replaced by the real source before production.","For local data, Autopilot can fill a data object with realistic sample records from the Records tab ('Generate sample records with Autopilot', choosing how many); values match the field types (an Email field gets email-shaped values). This is a good substitute for a system of record that is not integrated yet during testing."],"keyFacts":["Simulate when the external system is unavailable, has no data, or the API is still in development.","Expected request and response structures must be known.","Simulation dictates responses, which helps unit testing; the UI team is not blocked.","Simulated data pages are temporary: configure the real source before production.","Autopilot generates sample records on a data object's Records tab; you choose the count and values match field types."],"examTraps":["Simulation is never acceptable as the production source, even if responses look correct.","Autopilot sample records do not sync to the external system later; they are test data only."]}],"questions":[{"id":"di-001","objective":0,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"Stakeholder workshops produced a list of entities (Policy, Claimant, Vehicle) and their attributes. The LSA now maps each entity to a data object and each attribute to a field. Which data model level is the LSA producing?","options":["Conceptual data model","Logical data model","Physical data model","Integration Map"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Mapping entities to data objects and attributes to fields is the logical level, the Pega-specific refinement. The conceptual model is the tool-neutral stakeholder view that already exists here. The physical model covers storage, access and integration settings, which have not been decided yet. The Integration Map is an optional diagram in the Integration Designer, not a level of the data model."},{"id":"di-002","objective":0,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"An insurer moves its policy system of record from an on-premises database to a cloud REST API. The case types read policy data through data pages. Which two statements about this change are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Views that display policy data do not need to change","The change belongs to the physical level of the data model","The conceptual model must be signed off again before the change","Case business logic must be rewritten to call the new API"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Data pages separate integration from how the application uses data, and the center-out approach insulates the data access layer, so views and logic stay as they are. Storage and access decisions such as the source system are physical-level concerns. The conceptual model describes entities and relationships, which have not changed, and it is a living document anyway, not something that blocks integration work. Rewriting business logic is exactly what the insulated data access layer avoids."},{"id":"di-003","objective":1,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"An onboarding case captures a six-digit Badge number that can start with 0 and a Signing bonus in the employee's local currency. Which two field type choices are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Badge number as Text (single line)","Signing bonus as Currency","Signing bonus as Decimal, which supports currency codes","Badge number as Integer, because it holds only digits"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Integer strips leading zeros, so an identifier that is never used in arithmetic belongs in Text (single line). Money belongs in Currency, which handles currency codes and separators. Decimal is a real numeric type but it is the currency type, not Decimal, that supports codes and separators. Integer would turn a badge such as 012345 into 12345."},{"id":"di-004","objective":1,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"In an Order case, each order line holds a Quantity entered by the user and one Product chosen from a catalog stored as local Pega records. How should the Ordered items field be modeled?","options":["Embedded data list whose items include a Product data reference","Data reference list pointing to the Product data object","Query field that returns the product catalog","Case reference list pointing to Product cases"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Quantity is user-entered data that lives in the case, so the line items are an embedded data list, and each line reuses the shared catalog through a single Product data reference. A data reference list alone has nowhere to store the per-line Quantity. A Query field is read-only and does not store the key, so the chosen product would not be saved. Products are data records, not cases, so a case reference does not fit."},{"id":"di-005","objective":1,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Claim CL-88 shows details from its related Policy case. It also shows a read-only list of repair shops from an external API, filtered by postal code; the user's view of the list is not saved. Which two relationship field types fit? (Choose Two)","options":["Case reference for the related Policy","Query for the repair shop list","Embedded data for the repair shop list","Data reference for the related Policy, since it lives outside the claim"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Data that comes from another case uses a Case reference. A read-only, parameterized lookup of external data that is not stored by key is a Query. Embedded data is for user-entered data stored inside the case, which the shop list is not. Data reference does reuse data from outside the case, but it points at data objects, not at another case, so the qualifier makes it wrong."},{"id":"di-006","objective":1,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"On the same day, an analyst adds 40 rows on the Records tab of the Product data object, and a developer adds a Warranty period field to the same data object. Which statement about these changes is correct?","options":["The new rows are a data change; the new field is a process change","Both are data changes because only a data object was touched","Both are process changes because the data model was edited","The new rows are a process change; the new field is a data change"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Editing records changes data only, while adding or changing fields changes the application's structure and counts as a process change. Treating both as one kind ignores that distinction, whether because only a data object was touched or because the data model was edited. Swapping the two labels gets both of them wrong."},{"id":"di-007","objective":1,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"While configuring a step in a case lifecycle, a developer adds a new Vendor data object from the workflow. What must happen for Vendor to become a permanent part of the application?","options":["Make it permanent on the Data objects and integrations page","Mark it as a relevant record in Dev Studio","Add it to the case type's Data initialization page","Nothing; objects added from the workflow are permanent already"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A data object added from the workflow starts as a draft and must be made permanent on the Data objects and integrations page. Relevant records control what App Studio users can pick from Dev Studio rules, not draft status. The Data initialization page sets default values through pyDefault. Draft objects are not automatically permanent."},{"id":"di-008","objective":1,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"An HR application needs an employee's postal address and an Employee data object. The standard Person data object covers most employee fields but not all of them. Which two choices follow data object best practice? (Choose Two)","options":["Reuse the standard Address-Postal object for the address","Extend Person as Person-Employee","Create a new Employee Address object to keep HR data separate","Create a standalone Employee object because Person fits only partly"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"The recommended order is to use a standard object first, extend one that partially fits, and create a new object only as a last resort. Address-Postal is a standard object for this need, and Person-Employee extends a partial fit. A separate Employee Address object duplicates a standard one. A partial fit is exactly the case for extending, not for building a standalone object."},{"id":"di-009","objective":2,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"An Expense report case has an embedded list of Expense lines, each with an Amount of type Currency. The case needs a Total that updates as lines change. Which configuration is correct?","options":["A Function calculation using Sum of on Amount","An Expression that adds each line's Amount with +","A Decision table that returns the total","A default set on the Data initialization page"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Functions iterate over a list and return Sum, Average, Maximum or Minimum, and the calculation network recalculates the total when a line changes. An Expression combines individual fields with operators; it does not iterate a list of unknown length. A Decision table maps conditions to a result, not an aggregate. The Data initialization page sets a value once at case creation, so it would not track changes."},{"id":"di-010","objective":2,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A developer is adding calculated fields in App Studio. Which two configurations are valid? (Choose Two)","options":["An Expression returning Text that joins First and Last name with \" \"","A Function returning the Maximum of a Currency field across a list","An Expression returning a Picklist value based on Amount","A Function returning the Average of a list into a Text field"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Expressions can return any simple type except Email, Phone and Picklist, and they can concatenate text with quoted literals, so a Text full name is valid. Functions can return Maximum of a list into Currency, one of the three allowed result types. A Picklist result is one of the exclusions for Expressions. Average of is a real Function, but Function results are limited to Decimal, Currency and Integer, so a Text target is invalid."},{"id":"di-011","objective":2,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"In a Traditional UI application, a citizen developer cannot find the option to create a custom decision table for a calculated field. A table that an SA built in Dev Studio for this purpose is also missing from the list. Which statement explains both problems?","options":["Custom tables need Constellation, and the Dev Studio table is not a relevant record","The citizen developer's access role lacks permission to create decision tables","Calculated fields can use When rules but not decision tables","The Dev Studio table must first be copied into the Data- class"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Creating a custom decision table from App Studio is available only in Constellation applications, and a Dev Studio decision table appears in App Studio only after it is marked as a relevant record. This is expected behavior, not a permissions gap. Decision tables are one of the three App Studio calculation types. Class placement is not the condition for visibility; relevant-record status is."},{"id":"di-012","objective":3,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The Start date field appears on the Prior employment step, where it must be in the past, and on the New hire step, where it must be in the future. Both steps use a calendar control. Which approach is correct?","options":["Add a separate validation to each step","Rely on the calendar control to reject the wrong dates","Add one validation to the field so it applies everywhere","Create two Start date fields, since a field holds one validation"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Validations are tied to a specific step instance, so the same field can carry a past-date rule on one form and a future-date rule on another. A calendar control only guarantees a real date, not one in the past or future. A single rule cannot express two opposite conditions. Duplicating the field is unnecessary because one field can carry different validations on different forms."},{"id":"di-013","objective":3,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Loan case LN-204 must not enter the Underwriting stage unless Collateral value is at least the Loan amount. Which two statements about configuring this in App Studio are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Configure it as a stage-entry validation in the case type Data model","Provide a way out, such as returning to a previous stage","The user sees the error immediately on the form that captured the value","The condition describes the valid values that permit entry"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Stage-entry validation is configured in the case type's Data model and stops the case from entering the stage, so the designer must give the user a recovery path such as going back a stage or closing the case. Immediate feedback on the form is form-submit validation, the other trigger point. App Studio validation conditions describe the invalid values that raise an error, not the valid ones."},{"id":"di-014","objective":3,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"In Dev Studio, a Validate rule must run when the proposed work status is Pending-Qualification but not when it is Approved. Where is this configured?","options":["On the Validate rule's Input tab","On the property's Advanced tab with Use Validate","In an Edit Validate rule's Java pattern","On the case type's Data initialization page"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The Input tab of a Validate rule qualifies when it runs by input property, proposed work status, flow action or stage. The Advanced tab's Use Validate setting attaches an Edit Validate rule to a property and runs client-side, with no status condition. An Edit Validate rule checks a value's pattern, not case status. The Data initialization page sets defaults, not validation conditions."},{"id":"di-015","objective":3,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"An SA considers Edit Validate rules to check the format of a Policy number. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Set through the property's Advanced tab, it runs client-side on value change","It is a security risk because it uses custom Java","Called from a Validate rule, it still runs client-side on value change","It is the preferred place for general business logic"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"An Edit Validate rule set with Use Validate on the property's Advanced tab runs client-side as soon as the value changes, and its custom Java pattern checks are a security risk. When a Validate rule calls it, it runs on submit, not on value change, so that qualifier is wrong. Because of the custom Java, Edit Validate is not best practice for general logic."},{"id":"di-016","objective":4,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Work- pyDefault sets urgency 10. The Claim case type's pyDefault sets urgency 40 and has Call superclass data transform checked. What is the urgency of a new claim, and why?","options":["40, because parent transforms run first and the subclass runs last","10, because Work- runs last and overrides the subclass","10, because superclass values take priority over subclasses","40, because Work- is skipped when the subclass has its own transform"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"With the superclass option checked, the runtime finds each same-named parent transform and runs them top-down, so the most specific class runs last and its value wins. Work- runs first, not last, so it cannot override the subclass. Superclass values do not take priority; they are the starting point. Work- is not skipped; it still sets status, urgency and organization details before the subclass overrides urgency."},{"id":"di-017","objective":4,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A Permit case needs Priority defaulted to Normal and a Review date computed with @addCalendar from several conditions. Which two choices follow best practice? (Choose Two)","options":["Set Priority on the Data initialization page of the Settings tab","Put the Review date logic in pyDefault","Put the Review date logic in pySetFieldDefaults, which runs on view load","Set Priority by editing pySetFieldDefaults directly"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Simple defaults belong on the Data initialization page, which writes into pyDefault, and complex logic goes in pyDefault directly. pySetFieldDefaults does run when a view loads, but it is system-maintained and should not be modified, so neither the Review date logic nor the Priority default belongs there."},{"id":"di-018","objective":4,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A Dev Studio data transform sets Requester name from pyFirstName on the OperatorID page, but the value never resolves. Which change most likely fixes it?","options":["Declare OperatorID on the Pages & Classes tab","Mark the data transform as a relevant record","Check Call superclass data transform","Move the step into pySetFieldDefaults"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"To reference operator details such as pyFirstName in a Dev Studio transform, the OperatorID page must be declared on the Pages & Classes tab. Relevant-record status affects visibility in App Studio, not runtime page references. The superclass option chains parent transforms and does nothing for page declarations. pySetFieldDefaults is system-maintained and should not be edited."},{"id":"di-019","objective":4,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"On the Shipping information step, the user enters a shipping address. The billing address must match it once the user submits the step. Which configuration is correct?","options":["A data transform as post-processing on the Shipping information step","A data transform as pre-processing on the Shipping information step","A form-submit validation on the Shipping information step","An Expression calculation on the billing address fields"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Post-processing runs after the user submits, when the shipping address exists, so a data transform there can copy it to billing. Pre-processing runs before the form is shown, when there is nothing to copy yet. A validation checks values and raises errors; it does not copy data. A calculated Expression cannot return an Address, which is not a simple type, and copying is the job of a data transform."},{"id":"di-020","objective":5,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A list of vehicle makes and models is used by every user of the application and changes only every 30 to 60 days. Which data page configuration fits best?","options":["Node scope with Reload if older than","Node scope with Reload once per interaction","Thread scope with Reload if older than","Requestor scope with Reload once per interaction"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Data shared by all users belongs in Node scope, and a long Reload if older than interval balances performance with occasional changes. Reload once per interaction is not valid for Node scope. Thread scope would load a copy per case or transaction, wasting the shared nature of the data. Requestor scope with a reload on every interaction loads a per-user copy far more often than data that changes monthly needs."},{"id":"di-021","objective":5,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"D_StockQuote supplies prices to a trading case. Which two statements about its refresh strategy are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Do not reload when can keep the page current after market close","The page reloads when any one configured condition is met","Reload once per interaction can be combined with Reload if older than","The data page cannot be saved without a refresh strategy"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Do not reload when treats the page as current while its When condition is true, which suits prices after market close, and the page reloads if any configured condition is met. Reload once per interaction is a real option but it cannot be combined with other options. Refresh strategies are optional, though strongly recommended, so a page without one can still be saved."},{"id":"di-022","objective":5,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"D_ExchangeRates uses Reload if older than 10 minutes. It is first accessed at 12:00 and next accessed at 13:00. Which statement is correct?","options":["It becomes stale at 12:10 and reloads when accessed at 13:00","It reloads automatically in the background at 12:10","It reloads every 10 minutes from 12:10 onwards","It reloads only when the requestor session ends"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Reload if older than marks the page stale after the interval and reloads it on the next access, here 13:00. It does not reload in the background at 12:10 or on a repeating timer. Session end has nothing to do with this strategy."},{"id":"di-023","objective":5,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"Credit case CR-310 must keep the applicant's credit score as it was at application time, even if the bureau later updates it. How should the Credit score property be populated?","options":["Copy data from a data page","Refer to a data page","Refer to a data page with Reload once per interaction","Refer to a Node-scoped data page"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Copy stores a point-in-time snapshot in the case that is re-retrieved only if the parameters change. Refer keeps no copy in the case and always reflects the current data page, so the score would change when the bureau updates it. Adding a refresh strategy or a wider scope to a Refer makes the value even more current, not frozen."},{"id":"di-024","objective":5,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Property .Customer refers to D_Customer[CustomerID:.CustID]. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Changing .CustID points .Customer to a different data page instance","The case holds no copy of the customer data","The case keeps a snapshot refreshed only when .CustID changes","A new .CustID overwrites the one existing D_Customer instance"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"With Refer, the case stores no copy, and a parameter change points the property at a new data page instance. A stored snapshot that refreshes only on parameter change describes Copy, not Refer. Each distinct parameter value creates its own instance unless Limit to a single data page is set, so values are not overwritten in one instance by default."},{"id":"di-025","objective":5,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"D_Product[ProductID] now holds instances for 500 different product IDs in one session. The team wants only one instance kept at a time. Which change achieves this?","options":["Select Limit to a single data page on the Load Management tab","Change the scope from Thread to Node","Add Reload once per interaction as the refresh strategy","Change the structure from Page to List"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Each parameter combination creates its own instance unless Limit to a single data page is selected on the Load Management tab. Node scope shares instances across users but still keeps one per parameter value. A refresh strategy controls when a page reloads, not how many instances exist. Changing to a List structure changes the shape of the result, not the per-parameter instances."},{"id":"di-026","objective":5,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"D_Customer has three conditional sources in this order: a REST connector when Region is EU, a report definition when Region is US, and a data transform as Otherwise. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Source conditions are evaluated in the listed order","The first source whose condition is true is used","Every source whose condition is true runs in sequence","The Otherwise source can be placed anywhere in the list"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"With multiple conditional sources, conditions are checked in order and the first true one supplies the data. Running several sources in listed order is the Aggregate sources option, not conditional sources. The Otherwise source is always the last one, so it cannot be placed anywhere."},{"id":"di-027","objective":5,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A flight seating data page must show the latest seat availability on every user interaction. Which configuration is valid?","options":["Thread scope with Reload once per interaction","Node scope with Reload once per interaction","Thread scope with Reload once per interaction and Reload if older than","Node scope with Do not reload when"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Reload once per interaction refreshes on each interaction and is valid for Thread or Requestor scope. It is not allowed in Node scope. It cannot be combined with other options such as Reload if older than. Do not reload when keeps the page unchanged while its condition is true, which does not guarantee fresh data on each interaction."},{"id":"di-028","objective":5,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"A Country dropdown is sourced from a list data page. Users see country names, but the case must store the two-letter country code. Which statement is correct?","options":["Only the selected value is copied, and it can differ from the displayed text","The full country list is copied into the case when a value is chosen","The displayed name is stored, so a data transform must convert it","The dropdown needs a savable data page to store the code"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"List controls copy only the selected value into the case, and that value can be different from what is displayed, such as a code instead of a name. The list itself stays on the data page. No conversion transform is needed because the stored value can be configured directly. Savable data pages write data back to a system of record; they are not required to store a selection."},{"id":"di-029","objective":5,"type":"multi","difficulty":"medium","stem":"A Customer data object is connected to an external system of record. Which two data pages does Pega create for it by default? (Choose Two)","options":["D_CustomerList, a list data page","D_CustomerSavable, a savable data page","D_CustomerNode, a Node-scoped cache page","Declare_Customer, a declarative lookup page"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"A data object with a system of record gets three default pages: D_CustomerList, the lookup D_Customer and D_CustomerSavable. There is no default page named for its scope. Declare_ was the prefix before version 7.1; current data pages use D_."},{"id":"di-030","objective":6,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"A Vendor case must save new vendors to an external SOAP service through a savable data page. Which save option should be used?","options":["Activity","Connector","Database save","Robotic desktop automation"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The Activity save option can call any connector, including SOAP. The Connector save option supports REST only. Database save writes a page to a row in a database table, not to a service. Robotic desktop automation drives desktop applications, not a SOAP service."},{"id":"di-031","objective":6,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Case DP-55 collects a list of Dependents and writes them to a local table through a savable data page. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["A Database save maps one page to one row","If the save fails, the case and the data save roll back together","A Database save writes a page list as multiple rows in one save","The Activity save option is transactional by default"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Database save handles a single page to a single row with the page structure only, and savable data pages use transaction handling so a failure rolls back both the case and the data. Database save does not write a page list into multiple rows in one save. The Activity option is not inherently transactional, which is why its use should be limited."},{"id":"di-032","objective":6,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"One savable data page, D_SupplierSavable, must both create new suppliers and update existing ones. How should it be configured?","options":["Optional key; Lookup source when a key is given, Data Transform source when not","Required key; Data Transform source when a key is given, Lookup source when not","Two separate savable data pages, one for create and one for update","Optional key; Lookup source when no key is given"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Making the key optional lets one page do both: with a key, a Lookup source loads the existing record to update; without one, a Data Transform source initializes a new record. A required key prevents creation, and the sources are swapped in that option. Two pages are unnecessary. A Lookup with no key has no record to load."},{"id":"di-033","objective":6,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"After an assignment, case CU-19 must update a customer record in a Pega table and also send it to an external REST billing system. Which two statements about the Save Data Page steps are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Add one Save Data Page step for each data source","Place the steps after the assignment that collects the data","Use one step that lists both sources and saves them in order","Place the steps before the assignment so data saves as entered"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"A Save Data Page step handles one data source, so two targets need two steps, and the steps go after the assignment that gathers the data. A single step cannot list both sources. Placing the steps before the assignment would save before the user has entered anything."},{"id":"di-034","objective":6,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Property .Customer is autopopulated from D_CustomerSavable by Refer. Users edit the address on a form, but their edits are overwritten before the save. Which change fixes this?","options":["Change .Customer to Copy data from the data page","Change the data page scope from Thread to Node","Add Reload once per interaction to the data page","Move the save into flow action pre-processing"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"With a savable data page, the autopopulated property should use Copy, not Refer: Refer always shows the data page, so a reload can overwrite user edits, and Copy also helps avoid stale page errors. Node scope would share one page across all users, which makes the problem worse. Reloading every interaction overwrites edits more often. Saves are triggered by a step or post-processing; pre-processing runs before the user even edits."},{"id":"di-035","objective":6,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A developer needs a savable data page save to run in a transaction separate from the case. Which trigger allows this choice?","options":["The Save-DataPage method in an activity","A Save Data Page automation step with an audit note","Post-processing on the flow action","Use associated property in the Save Data Page step"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"When the Save-DataPage method is called from an activity, the activity can choose the same transaction or a separate one. The Save Data Page step can add an audit note, flow action post-processing is another valid trigger, and Use associated property takes the page and parameters from an autopopulated property, but none of them offers a separate-transaction choice."},{"id":"di-036","objective":6,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A form lets a user add two new Contact records on one screen, both through D_ContactSavable. The property is not autopopulated by Copy, and a guardrail warning appears. Which change follows best practice?","options":["Add an Index parameter to the savable data page","Select Limit to a single data page on the Load Management tab","Change the save option from Database save to Activity","Change the scope to Node so both forms share one page"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Best practice is to give a savable data page that creates records an Index parameter, unless it is autopopulated by Copy. This avoids the guardrail warning and allows multiple uses on one screen. Limiting to a single data page would make both contacts share one instance. The save option does not affect the guardrail. Node scope would share one page with every user."},{"id":"di-037","objective":7,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The billing REST API is still being built. The UI team needs to keep working, and testers want predictable responses for unit tests. Which two statements about a simulated data source are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["The expected request and response structures must be known","It lets testers dictate the response the data page returns","It may stay as the production source if responses match","It needs the external system online to record responses"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"A simulation must match the expected request and response, and because it dictates the response it is useful for unit testing while the UI team keeps moving. A simulation is always temporary and must be replaced by the real source before production, however good the responses look. It exists precisely because the external system is unavailable, so it does not need that system online."},{"id":"di-038","objective":7,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"A local Supplier data object has no records yet. Testers need about 50 realistic suppliers before the system of record is integrated. Which approach fits?","options":["Generate sample records with Autopilot on the Records tab","Configure a simulated source on D_SupplierSavable","Import sample suppliers through a Blueprint into a Branch","Add a Data Transform source on D_SupplierList that builds 50 pages"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Autopilot can generate a chosen number of sample records on a data object's Records tab, with values that match each field type, and it is a good stand-in for an unintegrated system of record during testing. Simulation applies to a data page source for an external system, and a savable page writes data rather than supplying it. Blueprint builds the application design, not test records. A hand-built transform source is extra code that does not create real records."}]},{"id":"security","title":"Security","weight":10,"intro":"Covers who a user is (authentication), what they may do (authorization), the Security Checklist and security policies that harden an application before go-live, and the operator / access group / access role / persona chain behind role-based access control. Questions usually turn on precise distinctions: ARO 'No Access' vs Access Deny, most-permissive role wins, and production-level comparisons.","objectives":[{"objective":"Differentiate between authentication and authorization","modules":["Application security"],"summary":["Authentication establishes who the user is and is the first line of defense. In Pega the operator ID (usually an email address) plus a password is the basic form, and enterprises add single sign-on with SAML 2.0, OpenID Connect or tokens, configured through an authentication service rule. Authorization decides what an authenticated user can do: which data and actions are available at runtime, which rules can be created, read, updated or deleted at design time, and which developer tools such as Tracer are usable.","Pega also separates application-level security, which keeps outsiders out (password rules, authentication, MFA, third-party tools), from feature security, which controls what authorized users can reach (roles for personas, RBAC, ABAC, CBAC). A payroll manager who can see salary data that other HR staff cannot is a feature-security concern. On the record side, the Operator ID record supports authentication, while the access group together with the application supports authorization."],"keyFacts":["Authentication = who you are; authorization = what you can do.","Authentication is the first line of defense.","SSO options: SAML 2.0, OpenID Connect, tokens; configured via an authentication service rule.","Authorization covers runtime data/actions, design-time rule create/read/update/delete, and developer tools such as Tracer.","Application-level security protects against outsiders (passwords, authentication, MFA, third-party tools).","Feature security governs what authorized users can access (persona roles, RBAC, ABAC, CBAC).","Operator ID record -> authentication; access group + application -> authorization."],"table":{"headers":["Concern","Question answered","Examples"],"rows":[["Authentication","Who is this user?","Password login, SAML 2.0, OpenID Connect, MFA, authentication service rule"],["Authorization","What may this user do?","Access roles, ARO, Access Deny, ABAC/CBAC, permission to use Tracer or edit rules"]]},"examTraps":["MFA and password policies strengthen authentication; they do not grant or restrict features.","Permission to use developer tools or edit rules is authorization, even though it applies to developers.","The Operator ID record alone does not determine what a user can do; the access group does."]},{"objective":"Explain the benefits of the Security Checklist and security policies","modules":["Application security"],"summary":["The Security Checklist guides a team through hardening an application and is installed as an application guideline rule for each application version. Tasks are grouped by timing (at the start, during development, before deployment) and by area (monitoring, authentication, authorization, auditing, production testing), and the checklist tracks status and completion so security is built in early instead of reworked late. Deployment Manager blocks a production deployment until the checklist is complete, which also supports IT security sign-off. Its four focus areas are data encryption (passwords, properties, BLOBs; sensitive values like SSNs and card numbers), authentication, authorization/access control (RBAC, ABAC, CBAC) and auditing/accountability (failed logins, password changes, rule and data changes). The goals map to the AIC triad: availability, integrity and confidentiality. Security is a shared responsibility of Pega and the client; not every task applies, and Pega Cloud completes some automatically. The most important practices are staying guardrail-compliant and avoiding custom code, which may bypass platform security.","Security policies are configurable controls. Password policy sets length, complexity and predictability rules. CAPTCHA can be the default or custom, shown on initial login or made more likely after failed logins. Lockout, when enabled, uses a failure threshold, an initial penalty in seconds that grows, and a failure log window in minutes; when disabled, a simpler attempts-before-lock and lock-minutes setting applies. Audit policy controls detail captured for login attempts. MFA sends a one-time password by email or SMS that must be used within an allowed time. Operator disablement disables accounts inactive for a set number of days, with an exclusion list. MFA factors are knowledge (password), possession (mobile number) and being (fingerprint); two-factor authentication is a subset of MFA."],"keyFacts":["Checklist = application guideline rule per application version.","Deployment Manager blocks production deployment until the checklist is complete.","Four areas: data encryption, authentication, authorization/access control, auditing/accountability.","Tasks grouped by timing (start, during, before deployment) and area; status tracked to prevent rework.","AIC triad: Availability, Integrity, Confidentiality.","Shared responsibility; not all tasks required; Pega Cloud handles some automatically.","Top practices: guardrail compliance and avoiding custom code.","Lockout (enabled): threshold, increasing initial penalty in seconds, failure log window in minutes.","Operator disablement: inactive N days, with an exclusion list.","MFA: one-time password by email/SMS within an allowed time; factors knowledge, possession, being; 2FA is a subset."],"table":{"headers":["Policy","What it controls"],"rows":[["Password","Length, complexity, predictability"],["CAPTCHA","Default or custom; on initial login; likelihood after failed logins"],["Lockout","Enabled: threshold, growing penalty seconds, failure log minutes. Disabled: attempts before lock, lock minutes"],["Audit","Level of detail recorded for login attempts"],["MFA","One-time password via email or SMS within an allowed time"],["Operator disablement","Disable after N inactive days; exclusion list"]]},"examTraps":["The checklist is not 'all tasks mandatory'; some do not apply and Pega Cloud covers some.","Security is not solely Pega's job on Pega Cloud; it is shared with the client.","Growing penalties after failed logins are Lockout, not CAPTCHA; CAPTCHA only becomes more likely.","Automatically disabling dormant accounts is Operator disablement, not Lockout."]},{"objective":"Describe Operators, Access groups, Access roles and Personas","modules":["Managing application access","Inviting users to an application"],"summary":["A persona is the business-facing description of a type of user, managed in App Studio under Users > User management > Personas (the default persona is 'Users'). An access role describes a job function and holds the permissions to perform specific tasks; every persona has an associated role, but not every role has a persona. Creating a persona automatically creates default permissions, a work queue and an access group; creating an access group does not create a persona. Each role has a default channel interface and is associated with studio channels (App, Admin, Dev Studio) and channel interfaces such as a Work Portal.","The operator ID record identifies a person and drives authentication; the access group, together with the application it references, drives authorization. An access group bundles privileges by referencing one or more access roles, which keeps security modular, and several access groups can point at the same application. If an operator's roles conflict, the most permissive permission wins. Users are invited by email and assigned a persona or developer role; generic shared accounts such as 'admin' should not be used. Drafting persona-to-channel associations in the lifecycle does not configure access; by default a persona can reach all pages, and a portal is restricted by assigning personas when the portal is configured."],"keyFacts":["Persona = business representation; access role = job function with permissions.","Every persona has a role; not every role has a persona.","Creating a persona auto-creates default permissions, a work queue and an access group (not the reverse).","Operator ID -> authentication; access group + application -> authorization.","An access group references one or more roles; several access groups can reference one application.","Conflicting roles: the most permissive permission wins.","Default persona is 'Users'; avoid generic accounts such as admin.","Drafting personas against channels does not restrict access; restrict a portal by assigning personas on it.","Prediction Studio requires the pxPredictionStudio portal in the user's access group."],"table":{"headers":["Record","Purpose"],"rows":[["Operator ID","Identifies the user; supports authentication"],["Access group","Links user to an application and one or more roles; supports authorization"],["Access role","Job function; class-level permissions"],["Persona","Business view of a user type in App Studio; linked to a role"]]},"examTraps":["Most permissive wins, not most restrictive, when an operator has multiple roles.","Creating an access group in Dev Studio does not create a persona.","Listing a persona for a channel in the lifecycle draft does not limit portal access."]},{"objective":"Configure role-based access control (RBAC) including ARO, Access Deny, Access Manager and production levels","modules":["Managing application access"],"summary":["RBAC grants permissions through roles. Pega also supports attribute-based access control (ABAC), which compares attributes of a case, field or document with attributes of the user, and client-based access control (CBAC), which tracks and processes requests to view, update or remove a customer's personal data, as regulations such as GDPR require. Within RBAC, an Access of Role to Object (ARO) holds a role's permissions on a class; 'No Access' denies that permission for that role. An Access Deny rule is an explicit extra layer that blocks access regardless of what other roles grant, which is useful for regulatory requirements.","App Studio's User management landing page (select a persona) is the preferred place to manage access. Dev Studio's Access Manager (Configure > Org & Security > Access Manager) is needed for some permissions, such as deleting a case instance. Best practice is to use these tools instead of editing ARO or Access Deny forms directly. Permissions can be tied to production levels: 5 Production, 4 Staging, 3 QA, 2 Development, 1 Sandbox, 0 deny. Access is granted when the permission's value is greater than or equal to the system's production level. Access Manager only writes 0 or 5, so set other levels by opening the record. For example, a delete permission set to 2 gives full access on a development system (level 2) and no access in production (level 5). Access When rules can add conditions."],"keyFacts":["ARO = a role's permissions on a class; 'No Access' denies for that role.","Access Deny = explicit deny that overrides grants; extra layer for regulations.","Prefer App Studio User management; Access Manager (Dev Studio) for advanced permissions such as deleting case instances.","Best practice: do not edit ARO/Access Deny forms directly; use the landing page or Access Manager.","Production levels: 5 Production, 4 Staging, 3 QA, 2 Development, 1 Sandbox, 0 deny.","Access granted when permission level >= system production level.","Access Manager writes only 0 or 5; open the record to set intermediate levels.","Access When rules provide conditional access.","ABAC compares object attributes (case, field, document) with user attributes.","CBAC handles requests about customers' personal data (view, update, remove), e.g., GDPR."],"table":{"headers":["Permission level set","Sandbox (1)","Development (2)","QA (3)","Staging (4)","Production (5)"],"rows":[["0","No","No","No","No","No"],["2","Yes","Yes","No","No","No"],["4","Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes","No"],["5","Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes"]]},"examTraps":["'No Access' on one role's ARO can be outweighed by another role (most permissive wins); Access Deny cannot.","The comparison is permission level >= system level; a lower number means fewer environments.","Access Manager is in Dev Studio, not App Studio; it only writes 0 or 5.","Region- or attribute-matching rules are ABAC; personal-data requests are CBAC."]}],"questions":[{"id":"sec-001","objective":0,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"A bank enables a one-time password sent by SMS that users must enter after their password. Which concern does this control address?","options":["Authentication, because it confirms who the user is","Authorization, because it limits which features open","Feature security, because it applies to signed-in users","Auditing, because it records each login attempt"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A one-time password is a form of multi-factor authentication, which strengthens proof of identity. Authorization decides what an identified user may do, which an OTP does not change. Feature security governs what authorized users can reach, while MFA is listed under application-level security. The Audit policy records login detail; it does not verify identity."},{"id":"sec-002","objective":0,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A security review lists four controls for an HR application. Which two are authorization controls? (Choose Two)","options":["Whether a developer may run Tracer in the application","Whether a payroll manager can see salary fields","Requiring sign-in through OpenID Connect","Enforcing password complexity on operator IDs"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Authorization covers what an identified user can do, including use of developer tools such as Tracer and access to specific data such as salaries. OpenID Connect sign-in and password complexity both concern proving who the user is, so they are authentication controls."},{"id":"sec-003","objective":0,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"Operator jdoe@acme.com signs in successfully but lands in the wrong application with none of the expected case types. Which record should be checked first?","options":["The access group and the application it references","The password settings on the operator ID","The authentication service rule","The Lockout security policy"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The sign-in worked, so authentication is fine; what the user can reach is authorization, which comes from the access group together with its application. Password settings, the authentication service rule and the Lockout policy all affect whether a user can sign in, which already succeeded."},{"id":"sec-004","objective":0,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A CISO wants MFA and a third-party threat tool to keep outsiders out. HR wants only payroll managers to see salary data. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["MFA and third-party tools are application-level security","Limiting salary data to payroll managers is feature security","ABAC is application-level security since it checks user attributes","Password policies are feature security since only users have passwords"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Application-level security keeps outsiders out with passwords, authentication, MFA and third-party tools. Feature security controls what authorized users can reach, as in the payroll example. ABAC does compare user attributes, but it decides what authorized users can access, so it is feature security. Password policies protect sign-in and are application-level security."},{"id":"sec-005","objective":1,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"A Deployment Manager pipeline for version 02.01 of an application stops before the production stage. Which is the most likely security-related cause?","options":["The Security Checklist for that version is not complete","Some checklist tasks are marked as not applicable","The Lockout policy is disabled","Operator disablement has no exclusion list"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Deployment Manager blocks a production deployment until the Security Checklist is complete. Not every checklist task applies, so tasks that do not apply are expected and do not block anything. The Lockout and Operator disablement settings are security policies, not deployment gates."},{"id":"sec-006","objective":1,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A team on Pega Cloud starts using the Security Checklist. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["It is installed as an application guideline rule per application version","Pega Cloud completes some of its tasks automatically","Every task must be completed whatever the deployment platform","On Pega Cloud, security is Pega's responsibility alone"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"The checklist is an application guideline rule installed for each application version, and Pega Cloud completes some tasks automatically. Not all tasks are required, so a rule that every task must be done is wrong. Security is shared between Pega and the client, even on Pega Cloud."},{"id":"sec-007","objective":1,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"After each failed login, users must wait longer before they can try again. Which policy setting meets this?","options":["Lockout enabled, with an initial penalty in seconds","CAPTCHA set to appear after failed logins","Lockout disabled, with attempts before lock and lock minutes","Audit policy set to record login attempt detail"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"With Lockout enabled, a failure threshold triggers an initial penalty in seconds that grows with further failures. CAPTCHA only becomes more likely after failed logins; it adds no waiting time. The disabled Lockout setting uses a fixed lock time after a number of attempts, not a growing penalty. The Audit policy records detail but does not slow anyone down."},{"id":"sec-008","objective":1,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Contractor accounts that have not been used for 90 days must be disabled automatically, except one integration account. Which policy meets this?","options":["Operator disablement with an exclusion list","Lockout with a 90-minute failure log window","Password policy with predictability rules","An Access Deny rule on the contractor role"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Operator disablement disables accounts after a set number of inactive days and supports an exclusion list. Lockout reacts to failed logins, not to inactivity. Password predictability rules govern password content. An Access Deny rule blocks permissions for a role; it does not disable accounts, and it cannot exclude a single account."},{"id":"sec-009","objective":1,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"An architect explains the MFA policy to a client. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["A fingerprint is a 'being' factor","Two-factor authentication is a subset of MFA","A registered mobile number is a knowledge factor","A one-time password stays valid until the next login"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"MFA factors are knowledge (a password), possession (a mobile number) and being (a fingerprint), and two-factor authentication is a subset of MFA. A mobile number is a possession factor, not knowledge. The one-time password sent by email or SMS must be used within an allowed time, not at any time before the next login."},{"id":"sec-010","objective":2,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A developer creates a new Field Adjuster persona in App Studio. Which two are created automatically? (Choose Two)","options":["Default permissions for the persona","A work queue for the persona","A portal that only Field Adjusters can open","A shared generic operator account for testing"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Creating a persona automatically creates default permissions and a work queue. A portal is restricted only when personas are assigned while configuring it, and nothing creates one automatically. Generic shared accounts such as admin are against best practice and are never generated."},{"id":"sec-011","objective":2,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"An operator's access group lists two access roles. One role's ARO grants Read on the Claim class; the other sets Read to No Access. Can the operator read claims?","options":["Yes, because the most permissive permission wins","No, because No Access overrides any grant","No, because the last role listed takes priority","Only if an Access When rule is also added"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"When an operator's roles conflict, the most permissive permission wins. No Access on an ARO denies only for that role, which another role can outweigh; an override that blocks regardless of other roles is an Access Deny rule. Role order does not decide the outcome, and no Access When rule is needed because Read is already granted."},{"id":"sec-012","objective":2,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"During lifecycle drafting, the Manager persona was associated with the Manager Portal. A tester signed in as a Customer can still open the Manager Portal. Which statement explains this?","options":["Drafting does not restrict access; assign personas when configuring the portal","The Customer role needs an Access Deny rule on the portal","Manager must be made the application's default persona","Portal limits apply only after the Security Checklist is complete"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Associating personas with channels during drafting does not configure access; by default a persona can reach all pages, and a portal is restricted by assigning personas when it is configured. Access Deny rules block permissions on a class, not portal assignment. The default persona is 'Users' and is unrelated to portal limits. The Security Checklist tracks hardening tasks; it does not turn portal restrictions on."},{"id":"sec-013","objective":2,"type":"multi","difficulty":"medium","stem":"An SA reviews how personas, roles and access groups relate. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Every persona has an associated access role","Several access groups can reference the same application","Every access role has an associated persona","Creating an access group in Dev Studio creates a persona"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Each persona is linked to an access role, and multiple access groups can point at one application. The reverse of the first statement is false: not every role has a persona. Creating an access group does not create a persona; it is creating a persona that creates related records."},{"id":"sec-014","objective":3,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A role's permission to delete Claim cases is set to 2. The systems are Development (2), QA (3) and Production (5). Where can users with this role delete claims?","options":["Development only","QA and Production only","Development, QA and Production","Nowhere, because only 0 or 5 are valid"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Access is granted when the permission value is greater than or equal to the system's production level. A value of 2 meets Development (2) but not QA (3) or Production (5). Reversing the comparison would wrongly grant access in higher environments. Values other than 0 and 5 are valid; Access Manager writes only 0 or 5, but the record can be opened to set another level."},{"id":"sec-015","objective":3,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Compliance requires that interns can never update Payment cases, even if another role in their access group grants it. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Use an Access Deny rule, which blocks access whatever other roles grant","Manage it through Access Manager or the landing page, not the rule forms","Set No Access on the intern role's ARO, which overrides other roles","Edit the Access Deny rule form directly as the preferred practice"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"An Access Deny rule is an explicit extra layer that blocks access regardless of other grants, which suits regulatory requirements, and best practice is to manage access through the App Studio landing page or Access Manager. No Access on an ARO does deny for that role, but another role can outweigh it because the most permissive wins. Editing ARO or Access Deny forms directly is not best practice."},{"id":"sec-016","objective":3,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A developer wants a role to delete case instances on Staging (4) but not Production (5). Access Manager offers only full access or no access. What should the developer do?","options":["Set it in Access Manager, then open the record and set the level to 4","Set level 4 on the App Studio User management landing page","Set the level to 5, which excludes Production","Set the level to 3, since access needs a level at or below the system's"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Deleting case instances is managed in Access Manager, which writes only 0 or 5, so the record must be opened to set an intermediate value; 4 meets Staging (4) but not Production (5). The App Studio landing page is not where this permission is set. A value of 5 grants access in Production too. A value of 3 is below Staging's level, so it would deny Staging, and access requires the permission to be at or above the system level, not below."},{"id":"sec-017","objective":3,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"A business manager asks that Supervisors be able to permanently delete case instances. Where is this permission configured?","options":["Access Manager in Dev Studio","Persona access on App Studio User management","The Security Checklist","The case type's Settings tab"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Some permissions, such as deleting a case instance, can be set only in Dev Studio's Access Manager (Configure > Org & Security > Access Manager). The App Studio User management landing page is preferred for most access but does not cover this permission. The Security Checklist tracks tasks and grants nothing. The case type Settings tab does not hold role permissions."},{"id":"sec-018","objective":3,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A lender has two needs: loan officers may open only cases whose Region matches their own Region, and customers can ask for their personal data to be removed under GDPR. Which two access models fit? (Choose Two)","options":["ABAC for the Region match","CBAC for the personal data removal requests","CBAC for the Region match, since it compares client attributes","ABAC for personal data requests, since it tracks data subjects"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"ABAC compares attributes of a case, field or document with attributes of the user, which is the Region match. CBAC tracks and processes requests to view, update or remove a customer's personal data, as GDPR requires. Swapping the two models gives each need the wrong one: CBAC handles personal-data requests, not attribute matching, and ABAC does not track data requests."}]},{"id":"devops","title":"DevOps","weight":5,"intro":"A small but precise domain: how Pega applications move through a CI/CD pipeline with Deployment Manager, and which automated tests (unit tests, test cases, assertions, coverage) give confidence to deploy. Questions usually test pipeline stage names, assertion types and coverage roles.","objectives":[{"objective":"Explain Pega’s process of continuous development, integration, delivery, and deployment (CI/CD)","modules":["Application development management"],"summary":["DevOps combines people, process and technology so that development, QA and operations work as one flow, covering development, testing and post-go-live support. **Continuous integration** means frequent check-ins with automated builds and tests: changes become ready to share, are integrated, and are published to a development repository. **Continuous delivery** adds automated regression and staging, then moves through ready to accept (acceptance criteria), ready to deploy (performance, scale and compatibility), and deployment (validate in pre-production, deploy to production, run post-deployment tests, roll back if needed). A typical setup pairs a Pega repository with an automation server such as Jenkins.","**Deployment Manager** is Pega's own CI/CD pipeline tool: it merges branches, packages and promotes applications. It is accessed in Admin Studio and integrates with App Studio, where Settings > Versions lets you publish to higher environments. Third-party tools such as Jenkins and Azure DevOps can also be used. Deployment Manager blocks a production deployment if the Security Checklist has not been completed.","CI best practices: test early and often (run Pega unit tests and critical integration tests before packaging and stop the pipeline on failure), automate merges and builds, keep the product rule (Rule-Admin-Product) current, and publish archives to a repository for version history. CD best practices: use test environments (for example Docker) for UAT and exploratory testing, run regression through UI and service layers, target specific ruleset versions for production, roll back the latest import on failure, and annotate a package as ready to deploy before manual or automatic deployment."],"keyFacts":["CI stages: ready to share, integrate changes, publish to development repository.","CD stages: ready to accept (acceptance criteria), ready to deploy (performance, scale, compatibility), deploy.","Deploy includes pre-production validation, production deployment, post-deployment tests and rollback.","Deployment Manager = Pega's CI/CD tool for branch merge, packaging and promotion; opened in Admin Studio.","From App Studio, Settings > Versions publishes to higher environments through Deployment Manager.","Deployment Manager blocks production deployment when the Security Checklist is incomplete.","Stop the pipeline when unit or critical integration tests fail.","Keep Rule-Admin-Product up to date and publish archives to a repository.","On a failed production import, roll back the latest import.","Testing cycles: Pega unit, regression, performance, load."],"table":{"headers":["Pipeline checkpoint","Question it answers"],"rows":[["Ready to share","Is my change ready for others (CI)?"],["Integrate changes","Does it merge and build cleanly with everyone else's work?"],["Ready to accept","Does it meet acceptance criteria?"],["Ready to deploy","Does it meet performance, scale and compatibility needs?"],["Deploy","Validate in pre-production, deploy, post-deploy test, roll back if needed"]]},"examTraps":["Performance, scale and compatibility belong to ready to deploy, not ready to accept.","Deployment Manager lives in Admin Studio, not Dev Studio.","Skipping tests to speed up the pipeline contradicts CI best practice.","A blocked production deployment can come from an incomplete Security Checklist even when tests pass."]},{"objective":"Describe the tests required for successful deployment: unit tests, test cases, and assertions","modules":["DevOps in Pega Platform"],"summary":["The smallest testable unit is an individual rule, so unit tests isolate where an error comes from (for example whether the decision tree or the data page is wrong). Rule resolution applies during tests. A unit test run can be converted into a reusable **test case** with assertions: Property (for example `pxUrgencyWork` equals 10), Decision result, Expected run time (for example within 3 seconds) and Page (for example a list data page has no errors). Test cases can be grouped into test suites and should run at least on every merge and check-in.","Automate first where results are predictable, tests run often, manual effort on complex logic is high, or the rule is widely used. Low-priority candidates are rules that change frequently, are easy to test manually, are too complex, or depend on database persistence. Good tests cover positive and negative paths and are maintainable (clear names, comments, modular test data).","Test case details: the Disable checkbox excludes a test from suites and REST runs so it can only be run manually; the Setup & Cleanup tab prepares and removes data with data transforms, activities and clipboard pages; the label becomes the immutable identifier. Flows and case types are recorded with Actions > Record test case; captured inputs go into a data transform, and the `pxCapturePropertyIgnore` data transform excludes properties from capture. Since 8.5, a process containing the Create stage of a new case type cannot be recorded. Strategy tests use componentName and pzRandomSeed parameters."],"keyFacts":["Unit = individual rule; rule resolution still applies in tests.","Assertion types: Property, Decision result, Expected run time, Page.","Group test cases into test suites; run at least on every merge and check-in.","High automation priority: predictable, frequently run, complex manual effort, widely used.","Low automation priority: frequently changing, easy manually, too complex, database persistence.","Disabled test cases are excluded from suites and REST runs; manual run only.","Setup & Cleanup tab handles test data with data transforms, activities and clipboard pages.","Recorded flow inputs are stored in a data transform; exclude properties with pxCapturePropertyIgnore.","A test case label becomes its identifier, which cannot be changed."],"table":{"headers":["Assertion","Example check"],"rows":[["Property","pxUrgencyWork equals 10"],["Decision result","A decision rule returns the expected result"],["Expected run time","Rule completes within 3 seconds"],["Page","A data page has no errors"]]},"examTraps":["A rule that changes frequently is a poor automation candidate, even if it is important.","Disabled does not mean deleted; the test can still be run manually.","Excluding a volatile property from a recording uses pxCapturePropertyIgnore, not the cleanup tab."]},{"objective":"Describe the tests required for successful deployment: the test pyramid and test coverage","modules":["DevOps in Pega Platform"],"summary":["The **test pyramid** puts unit tests at the base because they are cheap, fast and should be the most numerous; end-to-end tests sit at the top because they are expensive and should be few.","The **test coverage** tool reports which rules were exercised; an uncovered rule is one that was not tested. User-level reports cover one user's session and keep a coverage history. Application-level reports combine data from multiple users, appear in the Application Quality Dashboard, and can be merged. Running test suites requires SysAdm4; only operators with SysAdm4 or User4 contribute coverage data; starting application-level coverage needs the `pzStartOrStopMasterAppRuleCoverage` privilege. Coverage and pass rate also feed branch quality metrics."],"keyFacts":["Pyramid: many unit tests at the base, few end-to-end tests at the top.","Uncovered rule = a rule not executed by any test.","User-level coverage: single user, coverage history.","Application-level coverage: multiple users, Application Quality Dashboard, reports can be merged.","SysAdm4 is required to run test suites.","Only SysAdm4 or User4 operators contribute coverage data.","Starting application-level coverage requires pzStartOrStopMasterAppRuleCoverage."],"table":{"headers":["Coverage report","Scope","Where/notes"],"rows":[["User-level","One user","Coverage history for that user"],["Application-level","Multiple users","Application Quality Dashboard; reports can be merged"]]},"examTraps":["An uncovered rule is untested, not a rule whose test failed.","Application-level coverage cannot be started by any operator; it needs a specific privilege.","An inverted pyramid (mostly end-to-end tests) is slow and costly."]}],"questions":[{"id":"do-001","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"Release 3.2 of a claims application has passed its acceptance criteria. Before production, operations must confirm that it performs under expected load, scales and is compatible with the target environment. Which pipeline checkpoint is this?","options":["Ready to share","Integrate changes","Ready to accept","Ready to deploy"],"answer":[3],"explanation":"In continuous delivery, \"ready to deploy\" covers performance, scale and compatibility checks before production. \"Ready to accept\" is the earlier checkpoint where acceptance criteria are verified, and this release has already passed it. \"Ready to share\" and \"integrate changes\" are continuous integration steps that publish developer changes to the development repository.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"do-002","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"Every automated test passes and the package is marked ready to deploy, but Deployment Manager blocks the deployment to production. Which cause is most likely?","options":["The Security Checklist for the application has not been completed.","The pipeline does not use Jenkins as its automation server.","The application's product rule includes ruleset versions that are locked.","The release was not started from App Studio Settings > Versions."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Deployment Manager blocks a production deployment until the Security Checklist is completed and IT security approves it. Jenkins is an optional third-party tool, and Deployment Manager is Pega's own CI/CD tool. Locking ruleset versions is a recommended practice and does not block deployment. Settings > Versions is one way to publish from App Studio, not a requirement for production.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"do-003","objective":0,"type":"multi","stem":"A team wants its continuous integration process to catch problems earlier. Which two practices follow Pega CI guidance? (Choose Two)","options":["Run Pega unit tests and critical integration tests before packaging, and stop the pipeline on a failure.","Keep the product rule (Rule-Admin-Product) up to date.","Run unit tests after packaging so the archive is built sooner.","Keep archives out of the repository so that only the latest build exists."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Pega CI guidance is to test early and often: run Pega unit tests and critical integration tests before packaging, and stop the pipeline when one fails. Teams should also automate merges and builds, keep the product rule up to date, and publish archives to a repository to keep version history. Running tests after packaging delays feedback, which is the wrong order. Keeping archives out of the repository loses the version history that the repository exists to keep.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"do-004","objective":0,"type":"multi","stem":"An operations lead is reviewing the team's continuous delivery practices. Which two practices match Pega CD guidance? (Choose Two)","options":["Roll back the latest import when a deployment fails.","Use test environments such as Docker for UAT and exploratory testing.","Run regression tests only through the UI layer.","Allow only manual deployment to production because automatic deployment is not supported."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Pega CD guidance includes rolling back the latest import when a deployment fails, and using test environments such as Docker for UAT and exploratory testing. Regression testing should run through both the UI layer and the service layer, not the UI alone. Deployment to production can be manual or automatic, so automatic deployment is supported.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"do-005","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"A system architect working in App Studio wants to promote the current application version to higher environments through the company's Deployment Manager pipeline. Where does she start?","options":["Settings > Versions","Library > Reuse Library","Users > User management","The Case Types explorer"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Deployment Manager is accessed in Admin Studio. When it is integrated with App Studio, a developer publishes to higher environments from Settings > Versions. The Reuse Library is a catalog of reusable assets. User management handles personas and access. The Case Types explorer is for designing cases. None of these three starts a pipeline.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"do-006","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"A QA lead has budget to automate unit tests for one more rule. Which candidate is the lowest priority for automation?","options":["A widely used Decision table with predictable results","A pricing rule whose logic changes nearly every sprint","A complex calculation that runs many times a day","A When rule called by dozens of flows"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Rules whose logic changes often are a low priority for automation because the tests need constant upkeep. Other low-priority candidates are rules that are easy to test by hand, rules too complex to test reliably, and database persistence. High-priority candidates have predictable results, run often, are widely used, or save manual effort on complex logic. The Decision table, the complex calculation and the widely called When rule all fit that profile.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"do-007","objective":1,"type":"multi","stem":"A tester converts a unit test run into a test case. It must confirm that the case urgency `pxUrgencyWork` equals 10 and that the rule finishes within 3 seconds. Which two assertion types should be added? (Choose Two)","options":["Property assertion","Expected run time assertion","Decision result assertion","Page assertion"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"A Property assertion checks a specific property value, such as pxUrgencyWork = 10. An Expected run time assertion checks that the rule finishes within a limit, such as 3 seconds. A Decision result assertion checks the result returned by a decision rule, not the value of a case property. A Page assertion checks a page as a whole, for example that D_CoursesList has no errors.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"do-008","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"A developer selects the Disable checkbox on a test case that has become unreliable. What is the effect?","options":["The test case is removed from suites and REST runs, but can still be run manually.","The test case still runs in suites, but its failures are ignored.","The test case cannot be run in any way until it is enabled again.","The test case is deleted at the next Branch merge."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A disabled test case is left out of test suites and REST API runs, but a developer can still run it manually. It does not stay in suites with its failures ignored. Manual runs remain possible, so it is not completely blocked. Disabling a test case does not delete it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"do-009","objective":1,"type":"multi","stem":"A QA analyst records test cases for the Onboarding flow. Which two statements are accurate? (Choose Two)","options":["The captured inputs are stored in a Data Transform.","Properties listed in the pxCapturePropertyIgnore Data Transform are not captured.","From version 8.5, a new Case Type's process that includes the Create stage can be recorded.","A test case identifier can be renamed later by editing its Label."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Recording a test case with Actions > Record test case stores the captured inputs in a Data Transform. Properties such as changing timestamps can be excluded through the pxCapturePropertyIgnore Data Transform. Since 8.5, a process that includes the Create stage for new Case Types cannot be recorded, which is the reverse of the statement offered. The Label sets the identifier when the test case is created, and the identifier cannot be changed afterward.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"do-010","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"A team has 400 end-to-end UI tests and 20 unit tests. Builds take hours, and failures are hard to trace. What does the test pyramid recommend?","options":["Shift toward many unit tests at the base and keep only a few end-to-end tests at the top.","Add more end-to-end tests, because they cover the most functionality for each test.","Keep the same balance but run the end-to-end tests less often.","Replace the unit tests with load tests, which find failures faster."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The test pyramid puts cheap, fast and numerous unit tests at the base and a few expensive end-to-end tests at the top. This team's pyramid is upside down. Unit tests isolate errors at the level of a single rule, so failures are easier to trace. Adding end-to-end tests makes the imbalance worse. Running them less often does not fix how hard failures are to trace. Load testing is a different testing cycle, not a replacement for unit tests.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"do-011","objective":2,"type":"multi","stem":"A quality manager wants one view of rule coverage across testing done by six developers. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Application-level coverage combines data from several users and is shown in the Application Quality Dashboard.","Starting application-level coverage requires the pzStartOrStopMasterAppRuleCoverage privilege.","User-level coverage reports can be merged to combine results from several users.","Any operator, including one with WorkMgr4, contributes coverage data while testing."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Application-level coverage collects data from several users, is viewed in the Application Quality Dashboard, and needs the pzStartOrStopMasterAppRuleCoverage privilege to start. User-level reports cover one user and show that user's coverage history. Merging reports is a feature of application-level coverage. Only operators with SysAdm4 or User4 contribute coverage data, so WorkMgr4 does not.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"do-012","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"A QA analyst has the User4 role. She can contribute coverage data, but cannot run the team's test suites. What explains this?","options":["Running test suites requires the SysAdm4 role.","Test suites can run only from a Deployment Manager pipeline.","User4 operators cannot run any unit tests.","The suites contain test cases marked as uncovered rules."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Running test suites requires SysAdm4. User4 can contribute coverage data, which matches what the analyst sees. Test suites are not limited to Deployment Manager pipelines. Nothing in the role prevents unit testing in general. An uncovered rule is one that no test exercised. The term describes coverage results, not test cases, and it does not affect who can run a suite.","difficulty":"medium"}]},{"id":"user-experience","title":"User Experience","weight":10,"intro":"User Experience covers how Constellation turns your data model and rules into screens: the four view types, form layouts, field configuration, dynamic behavior, portals and channels, and the styling and localization that finish the job. Expect scenario questions where two answers both seem to work and only one matches Pega's prescribed default or best practice.","objectives":[{"objective":"Views and templates; Full Page View customization (summary panel, promoted action, utilities)","modules":["Application user interface design"],"summary":["In Constellation, a view is a reusable arrangement of UI elements, and a form is the kind of view that collects input. Every view is rendered through a template, and the template handles layout, responsiveness, breakpoints, whitespace, wrapping and accessibility, so you pick a template and fill its regions instead of placing controls by hand. There are four view types: the Full Page View, Partial Views, List Views and Form Views. Pega creates form views for you, one per user step, and each one takes the step's name. Automation steps do not get a form view.","Pega adds the Full Page View automatically to every case type and data object, and each one has exactly one. It uses three panes: the summary panel on the left, tabs in the center, and the utilities pane on the right. You edit it on the UX tab, but the preview there only shows placeholders, so you need to run a case to see real data. The summary panel sits at the upper left at roughly mobile width, and when collapsed it switches to responsive mode. It holds the Details view, which shows the primary fields by default, and the Pulse view for posting and replying in the context of the case.","The promoted action is a single button in the case header. You never add a button component for it, because Pega picks it: the first stage-wide action other than Edit details, or if there is none, the first case-wide action. Only user actions can be promoted, never optional processes. The utilities pane holds widgets such as Attachments, Case operator, Tags, GenAI Coach, Followers, Prediction, Stakeholders and Related Cases, and it is hidden when no widgets are configured."],"keyFacts":["Four Constellation view types: Full Page View, Partial View, List View and Form View. Only Form Views can be editable; the other three are read-only.","Each case type or data object has exactly **one** Full Page View, which Pega adds automatically. You edit it on the UX tab.","Full Page View panes: summary (left), center tabs, and utilities (right). The UX tab preview shows placeholders, so run a case to see data.","The summary panel contains the Details view (primary fields by default) and the Pulse view.","Promoted action: the first stage-wide action other than Edit details, otherwise the first case-wide action. Only user actions qualify, and no button component is needed.","Default templates: Form view uses Default form, List view uses Table, and Partial view uses Details (One/Two/Three column, Sub tabs, Narrow-wide, Wide-narrow) or Dynamic tabs for lists.","List views can use table, timeline or gallery layouts, but data objects do not support timeline.","A form view created on the UX tab under Other Views cannot be linked to a step directly. Embed it in the step's top-level form instead.","Best practice: build views from the lowest embedded level upward, and embed views rather than individual fields, which avoids similarly named views being auto-generated in the data class.","Constellation view changes save automatically in real time. There is no save, cancel or revert."],"table":{"headers":["View type","Purpose","Editable?","Notes"],"rows":[["Full Page View","Main interface for a case or data object","Read-only","One per case type/data object; 3 panes (summary, center tabs, utilities)"],["Partial View","Reusable read-only fragment; defaults are Details and Preview","Read-only","Confirmation view when enabled; Details and Dynamic tabs templates"],["List View","Shows records from a data source","Read-only","Table, timeline or gallery; default lists open cases with primary fields; no timeline for data objects"],["Form View","Collects input at a step; default Create and Edit views","Editable and read-only","Auto-created per user step (not automation steps); Default form template"]]},"examTraps":["A second Full Page View or a separate full page for each stage is never right. There is exactly one per case type or data object.","Adding a button component to the header to make a promoted action is wrong, because the platform derives it. An optional **process** can never be the promoted action.","Blank data in the UX tab preview is not a defect. The preview only shows placeholders.","Partial, List and Full Page views are read-only. If a question needs data entry, the answer is a Form View."]},{"objective":"Default forms vs hierarchical forms (and multi-step forms)","modules":["Application user interface design","Defining a Case Lifecycle"],"summary":["A form view normally uses the Default form template: one layout with one to three columns. Two columns is the recommended layout. Wide inputs such as Rich text, Text area, Radio group, Checkbox, Boolean and Attachment span both columns, and tab order runs left to right. Step instructions show on the form by default, and you can replace them with custom text or hide them.","A hierarchical form takes one form view and splits it into tabs called form groups. Use it when users can fill in the information in any order and you want less scrolling. Form groups are not independent or reusable views; they exist only inside that form. Do not use hierarchical forms inside a multi-step form, because tabs combined with Next and Previous buttons confuse users.","A multi-step form is still one assignment for one user, but it shows several views in sequence with Next and Previous, and Submit appears only on the last view. Navigation can be horizontal, vertical or standard. The default Create process is a multi-step form. The process option 'Allow users to go back to previous step' adds the Previous button."],"keyFacts":["Default form: a single layout with 1 to 3 columns. Two columns is the best practice.","Wide controls (Rich text, Text area, Radio group, Checkbox, Boolean, Attachment) span two columns, and tab order runs left to right.","Hierarchical form: one form view split into tabs (form groups) for information that can be completed in any order, which reduces scrolling.","Form groups are **not** independent or reusable views.","Do not put a hierarchical form inside a multi-step form.","Multi-step form: one assignment, one user, several views. Next and Previous move between views, and Submit appears only on the last view.","Multi-step navigation styles are horizontal, vertical and standard. The default Create process is a multi-step form.","Step instructions display by default and can be overridden with custom text or hidden.","Forms are views that collect input and carry action controls (submit, cancel, navigate). Not every view is a form."],"table":{"headers":["Layout","Structure","Use when","Watch out"],"rows":[["Default form","Single layout, 1-3 columns","Most steps; a short to medium number of fields","2 columns recommended; wide inputs span 2 columns"],["Hierarchical form","One form view split into tabs (form groups)","Fields can be completed in any order; long forms with a lot of scrolling","Form groups are not reusable views; not inside multi-step forms"],["Multi-step form","One assignment showing several views in sequence","One user enters data in a guided order (for example, Create)","Submit only on the last view; still a single assignment"]]},"examTraps":["A multi-step form does **not** create several assignments and cannot route views to different users.","Making form groups reusable in other case types is not possible. Reuse needs real views, such as embedded views.","Choosing a hierarchical form for an ordered, guided entry is a mistake, because tabs allow any order. Choose a multi-step form when the order matters."]},{"objective":"Field settings, primary fields and field groups (model-driven controls)","modules":["Application user interface design"],"summary":["When you select a field in a view, its settings cover the field type, label settings, editing options, additional helper text, form messages (warnings) and field behavior such as Required. Required can be always on or conditional. Because the UI is model-driven, the field type decides the control. Pega pairs each field with the best control automatically, such as autocomplete, checkbox, dropdown, link, rich text editor or text input.","Primary fields are the key fields of a case type or data object. Pega adds them automatically to the Create, Edit, Details and List views, so they appear in the Details view of the summary panel and in default lists. In a case type data model, Label and Description are primary fields by default, and you can also set primary fields on data objects. A field group puts related fields under a header with optional instructions. It can be collapsed, shows a vertical line at runtime, and can display an embedded list.","Picking the right field type is a UX decision. Use Currency for money, not Decimal. Use Text (single line) for numeric identifiers that never take part in arithmetic, because Integer strips leading zeros. A Picklist can show as a drop-down or radio buttons when the list is short, or as a search box when there are many records or you need several properties. In Constellation, a cascading dropdown is two Picklist fields where the second is sourced from a data page that takes a parameter. Traditional UI used a section with On Change and an action set instead."],"keyFacts":["Field settings: field type, label, editing options, helper text, form messages (warnings), and behavior such as Required (always or conditional).","Primary fields are added automatically to the Create, Edit, Details and List views.","Label and Description are primary fields by default in a case type data model. Data objects can have primary fields too.","Field group: a header, optional instructions, collapsible, a vertical line at runtime, and it can display an embedded list.","Picklist display: Drop-down or Radio buttons for short lists, Search box for many records or when several properties are needed.","Constellation cascading dropdown: two Picklist fields plus a parameterized data page.","Use Currency (currency codes and separators) for money. Use Text (single line) for IDs and zip codes, because Integer strips leading zeros.","Embedded data lists show as a Table (editable or read-only) or a Field Group (for complex records). Case reference can show as Autocomplete, Dropdown or Table.","Data reference modes are Single, List and Many to Many. List displays are Table, Simple table, Combobox and Checkbox group.","Location shows a Google Map. Email and URL include built-in validation and render as action links."],"table":{"headers":["Field type","UI display options"],"rows":[["Picklist","Drop-down (short list), Radio buttons (short list), Search box (many records or several properties)"],["Embedded data (list)","Table (editable or read-only), Field Group (complex records)"],["Case reference","Autocomplete, Dropdown, Table"],["Data reference (list)","Table, Simple table, Combobox, Checkbox group (modes: Single, List, Many to Many)"],["Query","Read-only data from a parameterized data page outside the case"]]},"examTraps":["Integer for an employee ID or zip code that keeps leading zeros is wrong. Use Text (single line).","Decimal for monetary amounts is wrong. Currency handles codes and separators.","A Constellation cascading dropdown does not use a section rule with an On Change action set. That is the Traditional UI approach.","Primary fields appear in views automatically. Adding them by hand to every Create and Details view is unnecessary."]},{"objective":"Patterns, conditional expressions and when rules for dynamic UI behavior (visible, disabled, required)","modules":["Configuring UI elements","Application user interface design"],"summary":["Constellation UI is model-driven. The data model and rules produce the UI through patterns, which are reusable templates for forms, lists, dashboards and case management screens. When the rules change, the UI adapts automatically and stays responsive. This follows the separation-of-concerns principle and the center-out approach: logic sits at the center and channels present it. Because UI generation is centralized, accessibility (WCAG 2.1 and Section 508) is built in by default. A fix to a central component reaches every application without editing each screen.","Dynamic behavior is set per field with three properties. Visible defaults to Always and can be set to Custom condition, When rule, If not blank or If not zero. Disabled defaults to Never and can be Always, Custom condition or When rule. Required defaults to Never and can be Always, Custom condition or When rule. A custom condition is an expression for one-off logic, while a When rule suits complex or reusable logic. Dynamic UI responds in real time, reduces clutter and avoids full-page refreshes.","A When rule built in Dev Studio can be used by App Studio authors only after it is marked as a relevant record. When rules are one of the rule types that can be marked relevant."],"keyFacts":["Patterns are reusable UI templates (forms, lists, dashboards, case management screens) generated from the data model and rules.","The model-driven UI adapts automatically when rules change and is responsive by default.","Accessibility (WCAG 2.1, Section 508) is built in. Fix a central component once and every app inherits the fix.","Visible options: Always (default), Custom condition, When rule, If not blank, If not zero.","Disabled options: Never (default), Always, Custom condition, When rule.","Required options: Never (default), Always, Custom condition, When rule.","Custom condition: a one-off expression. When rule: complex or reusable logic.","Benefits: real-time response, less clutter, fewer full-page refreshes.","Dev Studio When rules must be marked as relevant records before they appear in App Studio pickers."],"table":{"headers":["Property","Default","Other options"],"rows":[["Visible","Always","Custom condition, When rule, If not blank, If not zero"],["Disabled","Never","Always, Custom condition, When rule"],["Required","Never","Always, Custom condition, When rule"]]},"examTraps":["'If not blank' and 'If not zero' exist only for **Visible**, not for Disabled or Required.","Defaults differ: Visible starts as Always, while Disabled and Required start as Never.","Building a When rule for logic used on a single field is overkill. A custom condition is intended for one-off logic, and a When rule for reuse or complexity.","A Dev Studio When rule that does not appear in App Studio is usually missing its relevant-record flag. It is not a bug in the When rule."]},{"objective":"Channels vs personas; portal templates","modules":["Configuring Portal content"],"summary":["A persona is the business representation of a type of user. A channel is the interface those users work through: web portals, mobile, messaging and voice. Conversational channels include Facebook Messenger, WhatsApp, Apple Messages for Business, SMS/MMS through Twilio, embedded chat, Amazon Alexa and Email, supported by Intelligent Virtual Assistants (IVA) and Email Bots. Personas can share channels, and persona and channel access can be configured per stage. On the Channels landing page, a lock icon means the required component has not been added from Marketplace.","Drafting personas and channels in the case lifecycle, for example assigning them to a release such as MLP1, is a planning step. It does **not** configure channel access. By default a persona can reach every page. To limit a portal to certain users, assign the persona when you configure that portal, for example a Manager Portal. Creating a persona also creates a default permission set, a work queue and an access group. Creating an access group does not create a persona.","A portal is the UI of an application, and it is built from landing pages. Constellation has two portal families. Standard work portals serve professionals doing complex, recurring work. Self-service portals provide a minimal, guided experience for occasional tasks done by customers or employees. The default portal is the Web Portal. You can run one portal with visibility based on persona or several portals. Portal templates are responsive and include a mobile-only layout."],"keyFacts":["Persona = who (a business user type). Channel = how they interact (web, mobile, messaging, voice).","The default persona is 'Users'. A new persona creates default permissions, a work queue and an access group.","Personas can share channels. Persona and channel access can be set per stage.","Drafting personas and channels in the lifecycle for a release does **not** restrict access. By default a persona can reach all pages.","To restrict a portal, assign the persona while configuring that portal.","A lock icon on the Channels landing page means the component has not been added (Marketplace).","'Add channel process' on a stage lists only channels that have already been added. It is used for channel-specific processes such as IVA.","Standard work portal: professional, complex and recurring work. Self-service portal: minimal, guided and occasional tasks.","The default portal is the Web Portal. Templates are responsive and include a mobile-only layout.","Every role has a default channel interface, such as Work Portal or a custom portal."],"table":{"headers":["","Standard work portal","Self-service portal"],"rows":[["Audience","Professional, internal case workers","Customers or employees doing occasional tasks"],["Workflow style","Complex, recurring work","Minimal, guided, occasional"],["Built from","Landing pages (Home, My Work, Dashboard, Explore Data)","Landing pages (Self-service Main page)"]]},"examTraps":["Treating channels and personas as the same thing is a mistake. A persona is a user type, and a channel is an interface that several personas can share.","Assigning personas to channels in the lifecycle for a release is planning only. It is not access control.","A self-service portal is not the right choice for case workers who handle complex daily work.","Creating an access group does not create a persona. The reverse is true: a persona creates an access group."]},{"objective":"Landing page templates, themes, widgets and localization best practices","modules":["Configuring Portal content","Localizing application content"],"summary":["The ready-made landing pages are Dashboard, Home, Explore Data, My Work and the Self-service Main page. Custom landing pages use the Default page, Tabbed page, Dashboard page or Inline Dashboard page template. A default landing page can use a column layout, with or without a banner, or a dynamic layout where you drag, drop and resize. A dashboard is a landing page of widgets showing summaries and KPIs for each persona, organized as template, then regions, then widgets. New dashboards are Private by default and can be Shared (with all users or with your access group) or Public. A copy keeps the visibility of its source. Portal widgets include Pulse, To do (my worklist, team members' worklists, or a work queue) and App announcement (a message plus a link button). A developer must add the Pulse widget to the portal navigation.","The available themes are Pegasus (the default), Aries, Self-Service and Bootes. To customize one, save it under a new name. The Themes landing page flags colors that fail WCAG contrast. Themes do not support inheritance, so to specialize a theme you save a copy into a higher ruleset. Keep the design system (themes, fonts, icons, templates) in the enterprise or COE layer. Design tokens store styling centrally for consistency across platforms and can be changed per component.","For localization, Pega uses the browser locale first, and the locale on the operator profile overrides it. If a locale ruleset is missing, users get English. A language pack must match both the platform version and the UI architecture. A translation package bundles the application's text as JSON or Excel, can be pre-translated with GenAI, and is imported on the Localization landing page under App Studio Settings. Constellation cannot localize decision tables or trees, case statuses, or radio buttons and dropdowns inside a table. Picklist values are left out of the package, so you add them by editing the localization file in Dev Studio. Dynamic values need the pyMessages rule. Radio buttons and dropdowns sourced from data pages need values entered on the Additional text tab. Test by changing the locale on the operator record in Dev Studio; you may need to log in again."],"keyFacts":["Ready-made landing pages: Dashboard, Home, Explore Data, My Work, Self-service Main page.","Custom landing page templates: Default page, Tabbed page, Dashboard page, Inline Dashboard page.","Dashboard visibility: Private (default for new dashboards), Shared (all users or your access group), Public. A copy keeps its source's visibility.","Portal widgets: Pulse, To do, App announcement. A developer must configure Pulse in the portal navigation.","Themes: Pegasus (default), Aries, Self-Service, Bootes. Customize with save-as. Themes have **no inheritance**, so save a copy into a higher ruleset.","The Themes landing page flags colors that fail WCAG contrast. Design tokens centralize styling.","Locale order: browser locale first, then the operator profile overrides it. A missing locale ruleset falls back to English.","A language pack must match the platform version **and** the UI architecture.","Constellation cannot localize decision tables or trees, case statuses, or radios and dropdowns inside a table.","Character encoding cannot be switched from UTF-8 to UTF-16 after installation."],"table":{"headers":["Localization gap","Fix"],"rows":[["Picklist values missing from the translation package","Add them manually by editing the localization file in Dev Studio"],["Messages with dynamic values (for example {@P .userName})","Localize the pyMessages rule"],["Paragraph rules such as step instructions","Translate manually; edit the paragraph files (base and translated folders) in the package"],["Radio buttons or dropdowns sourced from a data page","Localization landing page, Additional text tab: enter values per row, rebuild and import"],["Decision tables or trees, case statuses, radios or dropdowns in a table","Cannot be localized in Constellation"]]},"examTraps":["Expecting a division theme to pick up enterprise theme changes through inheritance is wrong, because themes do not inherit.","A language pack that matches only the platform version is not enough. It must also match the UI architecture.","If the operator profile locale is set, it overrides the browser locale, not the other way around.","Dashboards are Private by default, not Public."]}],"questions":[{"id":"ux-001","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"In the Vendor Onboarding case type, the Verification stage has two stage-only optional actions, in this order: the optional process 'Escalate review' and the user action 'Request documents'. Case-wide actions are Edit details, Change Stage and the user action 'Add note'. Which action is the promoted action while a case is in Verification?","options":["Request documents","Escalate review","Change Stage","Add note"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The promoted action is the first stage-wide action other than Edit details, and only user actions can be promoted, so Request documents wins. Escalate review is listed first but is an optional process, which can never be promoted. Change Stage and Add note are case-wide actions, which are only used when no stage-wide user action qualifies.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-002","objective":0,"type":"multi","stem":"A team is designing the Constellation views for a Supplier data object and a Contract case type. Which two statements about Constellation view types are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["A Form View is the only view type that can contain editable fields","The Supplier data object has exactly one Full Page View, added automatically","A List View of Supplier records can use a timeline layout","A Partial View becomes editable when it is embedded in a step's form"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Form Views are the only editable view type, and each case type or data object gets exactly one Full Page View that Pega adds automatically. List Views support table, timeline and gallery layouts, but data objects do not support timeline. Partial Views are read-only wherever they are used, so embedding one in a form does not make its fields editable.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-003","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"A business architect built a form view named 'Bank details' on the UX tab under Other Views. It must appear when users complete the 'Collect payment' step, which already has an auto-generated form view. What should the system architect do?","options":["Embed Bank details in the Collect payment step's top-level form view","Select Bank details as the step's view in the step contextual pane","Add Bank details to the utilities pane of the Full Page View","Rebuild Bank details as a Partial View and attach it to the step"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A form view created from Other Views on the UX tab cannot be associated directly with a step, so it is embedded in the step's top-level form, which is the view Pega generated for the step. Selecting it directly in the step pane is the limitation itself. The utilities pane holds widgets such as Attachments and Tags, not step forms. A Partial View is read-only, so it could not collect bank details.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ux-004","objective":0,"type":"multi","stem":"The Policy case type has a Policyholder field group, and Policyholder contains an embedded Dependents list. The team is about to build the case's views. Which two practices are recommended? (Choose Two)","options":["Build the Dependent view first and work upward to the Policyholder view","Embed the Dependent view in the Policyholder view instead of adding its fields one by one","Add Dependent fields individually so Pega generates matching views in the data class","Save view changes manually before leaving, so they can be reverted if needed"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Best practice is to build views from the lowest embedded level upward and to embed views rather than individual fields. Adding fields one by one is exactly what causes Pega to auto-generate similarly named views in the data class, which is the clutter the practice avoids. Constellation view changes save automatically in real time, and there is no save, cancel or revert.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-005","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"The 'Collect property details' step is a single assignment, not part of a multi-step form. It has 36 fields in three sections, Structure, Occupancy and Financials. Users fill the sections in whatever order suits them and complain about scrolling. Which design fits best?","options":["Convert the form to a hierarchical form with a form group for each section","Turn the step into a multi-step form with one view per section","Keep the default form and increase it to three columns","Split the step into three assignments routed to the same user"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A hierarchical form splits one form view into tabs, which suits information that can be completed in any order and reduces scrolling. A multi-step form imposes an order through Next and Previous. Three columns is possible but goes against the two-column best practice, and the form is still one long layout. Three assignments add work items without solving the order or scrolling problem.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ux-006","objective":1,"type":"multi","stem":"The Create stage of a Loan Application uses the default multi-step form with four views. Which two statements about this form are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["All four views are completed by one user within a single assignment","The Submit button appears only on the last view","Each view can be routed to a different work queue","The navigation style must be horizontal because it is in the Create stage"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"A multi-step form is one assignment for one user spread across several views. Next and Previous move between views, and Submit appears only on the last one. Because it is a single assignment, its views cannot be routed to different queues. Horizontal, vertical and standard navigation styles are all available, and nothing forces horizontal in the Create stage.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-007","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"A form view uses the Default form template with two columns. It contains a Text area field among several Text (single line) fields. Which statement describes how this form lays out and navigates?","options":["The Text area spans two columns, and tab order runs left to right","The Text area stays in one column, and tab order runs down each column","The Text area spans two columns, and tab order runs down each column","The Text area stays in one column, and tab order follows field creation order"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Wide inputs, including Text area, Rich text, Radio group, Checkbox, Boolean and Attachment, span two columns, and tab order on a default form runs left to right. The other options each get at least one of those two behaviors wrong, either keeping the wide field in one column or describing a column-by-column or creation-order tab sequence.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-008","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"A Policy case needs 'Premium amount', charged in US dollars, and 'Postal code', with values such as 02134 that are never used in arithmetic. Which field types should be used?","options":["Currency for Premium amount and Text (single line) for Postal code","Decimal for Premium amount and Integer for Postal code","Currency for Premium amount and Integer for Postal code","Decimal for Premium amount and Text (single line) for Postal code"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Currency handles currency codes and separators, so it is the right type for money. Integer strips leading zeros, so 02134 would display as 2134, and Text (single line) is the recommended type for codes and IDs that are not used in calculations. Every other option either uses Decimal for money or Integer for a value with leading zeros.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-009","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"On a Constellation form, the 'Model' dropdown must list only the models for the 'Make' the user picked in the dropdown above it. Which configuration achieves this?","options":["Two Picklist fields, with Model sourced from a data page parameterized by Make","A section with an On Change action set that refreshes the Model list","One Picklist field shown as a Search box that lists makes and models","Two Picklist fields, with Model shown as Radio buttons listing every model"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"In Constellation a cascading dropdown uses two Picklist fields, and the second takes its values from a data page with a parameter, here the selected Make. A section with an On Change action set is the Traditional UI technique. A single Search box does not create a dependent second list. Radio buttons listing every model are meant for short lists and do not filter by Make.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-010","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"A developer marks 'Account number' as a primary field in the Customer case type data model. No views have been edited. Where does Account number appear without further configuration?","options":["In the Create, Edit, Details and List views","Only in the Details view of the summary panel","Only in List Views and Insights of the case type","In every Form View of every step in the lifecycle"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Primary fields are added automatically to the Create, Edit, Details and List views. The Details view in the summary panel does show primary fields by default, but it is not the only place. Insights are created from Explore Data queries, not from the primary field setting. Ordinary step Form Views do not receive primary fields automatically.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ux-011","objective":2,"type":"multi","stem":"An Order case has 'Ordered items', a list where users enter a quantity and choose a product for each line and the data is stored in the case. It also has 'Ordering customer', chosen from existing customer records maintained outside the case. Which two field configurations fit? (Choose Two)","options":["Ordered items as an Embedded data field, list of records","Ordering customer as a Data reference field, single record","Ordering customer as an Embedded data field, single record","Ordered items as a Query field sourced from a data page"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Embedded data stores user input inside the case, and a list of records suits the order lines. A Data reference reuses a data object from outside the case, which suits existing customer records, as one record. Embedded data for the customer is partly plausible because it is a single record, but it would copy user-entered data into the case instead of referencing the existing records. A Query field is read-only external data, so users could not enter quantities.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-012","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"A senior system architect created the When rule 'HasCoApplicant' in Dev Studio in the loan case type's class. A business architect wants to use it for the Visible setting of six fields in App Studio, but it is not offered in the When rule list. What should be done?","options":["Mark HasCoApplicant as a relevant record in Dev Studio","Rebuild the logic as a custom condition on each of the six fields","Set the six fields to Visible: If not blank on Has co-applicant","Move HasCoApplicant into the Pega Platform base ruleset"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Dev Studio rules such as When rules appear in App Studio pickers only when they are marked as relevant records. Custom conditions would work but are meant for one-off logic, and duplicating reused logic six times is poor practice. If not blank tests whether a value exists, which is not the same as the When rule's logic. Moving rules into the Pega base layer is never the approach, and you develop only in your own top layer.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-013","objective":3,"type":"multi","stem":"A business architect is setting dynamic behavior on fields in a Constellation form view. Which two statements about the Visible, Disabled and Required settings are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Visible defaults to Always, while Required defaults to Never","If not zero is offered for Visible but not for Disabled","A custom condition is preferred over a When rule for logic reused across views","Disabled offers If not blank in addition to Custom condition and When rule"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Visible starts as Always, while Disabled and Required both start as Never. If not blank and If not zero exist only for Visible. Custom conditions are meant for one-off expressions, and a When rule is the choice for complex or reused logic. Disabled offers Never, Always, Custom condition and When rule, but not If not blank.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-014","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"An accessibility audit finds a keyboard navigation defect in a Constellation table pattern used by twelve applications. The organization must meet WCAG 2.1. Which approach reflects how model-driven UI handles this?","options":["Fix the central component once, and every application using it inherits the fix","Correct the table configuration in each view of each of the twelve applications","Enable an accessibility setting separately on each application's portal","Rebuild the affected screens as Traditional UI sections with custom markup"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Constellation generates screens from centralized patterns, so an accessibility fix made once in the central component propagates to every application, with no per-screen changes. Editing every view repeats work that the pattern approach exists to avoid. Accessibility is built in by default, not switched on per portal. Moving to Traditional UI sections would abandon the centralized patterns and add custom work.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-015","objective":4,"type":"single","stem":"In the lifecycle's Personas and Channels section, a team drafted the Manager persona with the Manager Portal for release MLP1. In testing, Manager users can still open every portal page. Only the Manager Portal should be available to them. What should the team do?","options":["Assign the Manager persona when configuring the Manager Portal","Remove the Manager persona from the other stages of the lifecycle","Move the MLP1 release assignment to the current release","Lock the other portals on the Channels landing page"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Drafting personas and channels in the lifecycle is planning only and does not configure access, and by default a persona can reach all pages. To restrict a portal, assign the persona while configuring that portal. Changing stages or release labels changes the plan, not the access. A lock icon on the Channels landing page means a component has not been added from Marketplace. It is not an access control.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ux-016","objective":4,"type":"single","stem":"An insurer needs one portal for policyholders, who file a claim perhaps once a year with step-by-step help, and one for adjusters, who process complex claims every day. Which portal assignment is correct?","options":["Self-service portal for policyholders, standard work portal for adjusters","Standard work portal for policyholders, self-service portal for adjusters","Self-service portal for both, because templates include a mobile-only layout","Standard work portal for both, with Private dashboards hiding adjuster work"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Self-service portals are minimal and guided for occasional workflows, which matches policyholders. Standard work portals suit professionals doing complex, recurring work, which matches adjusters. Swapping them reverses the purposes. Responsive templates with a mobile-only layout exist, but they do not make a self-service portal right for daily professional work. Dashboard visibility controls who sees a dashboard, not which portal type fits a user group.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ux-017","objective":4,"type":"multi","stem":"A lead system architect is setting up user types and interfaces for a new Constellation application. Which two statements about personas, access groups and channels are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Creating a persona in App Studio automatically creates an access group","Several personas can share the same channel interface","Creating an access group in Dev Studio automatically creates a persona","Add channel process on a stage lists every channel available in Marketplace"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Creating a persona generates default permissions, a work queue and an access group, and personas can share channels. The reverse is not true: creating an access group does not create a persona. Add channel process lists only channels that have already been added to the application. Components still showing a lock icon are not offered.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-018","objective":5,"type":"single","stem":"A Constellation application imported a Spanish translation package. Spanish-locale users see translated labels and buttons, but case statuses and the results returned by a decision table still appear in English. What is the best explanation?","options":["Constellation cannot localize case statuses or decision table and tree values","The operator profile locale is overriding the browser locale","The language pack matches the platform version but not the UI architecture","The translation package must be regenerated with GenAI pre-translation"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Constellation cannot localize decision tables and trees, case statuses, or radio buttons and dropdowns inside a table, so these values stay in the base language. A locale override would affect all text, not just these items. A language pack that does not match the UI architecture would cause broader problems than two element types. GenAI pre-translation helps produce translations but does not remove the platform limitation.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ux-019","objective":5,"type":"multi","stem":"An enterprise Center of Excellence is building a corporate theme from Pegasus, and two divisions need small variations. Which two statements reflect correct practice? (Choose Two)","options":["Customize by saving the theme under a new name rather than editing Pegasus","The Themes landing page flags colors that fail WCAG contrast","Division themes automatically inherit later changes to the enterprise theme","Design tokens must be redefined in each application that uses the theme"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Themes are customized by saving a copy under a new name, and the Themes landing page flags color combinations that fail WCAG contrast. Themes do not support inheritance, so a division copy saved into a higher ruleset does not pick up later enterprise changes. Design tokens exist to store styling centrally for consistency, not to be redefined in every application.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-020","objective":5,"type":"multi","stem":"A team is localizing a Constellation application into French for the first time. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["The language pack must match both the platform version and the UI architecture","Picklist values left out of the translation package are added by editing the localization file in Dev Studio","Dropdown values sourced from a data page are included automatically in the translation package","Character encoding can be switched from UTF-8 to UTF-16 after installation to support French"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"A language pack must match the platform version and the UI architecture, and multi-value local properties such as picklists are omitted from the translation package, so you add them by editing the localization file in Dev Studio. Data-page-sourced radio buttons and dropdowns are not included automatically, because the data pages are empty when the package is generated, so you enter their values on the Additional text tab. Encoding cannot be switched from UTF-8 to UTF-16 after installation.","difficulty":"hard"}]},{"id":"app-development","title":"Application Development","weight":15,"intro":"This domain tests how Pega applications are structured and built: the studios and low-code approach, layered reuse, center-out design, team roles and collaboration tools, the rule/ruleset/class model behind every application, and how to debug what you build. Expect many questions that hinge on precise vocabulary (pattern vs directed, workspace vs branch, major vs patch).","objectives":[{"objective":"Evaluate the benefits of using Pega Platform for creating digital solutions","modules":["Low-code defined"],"summary":["Pega is a low-code platform: instead of writing code, teams model applications visually and declaratively, so the platform effectively generates the software from the model. Business people and developers share one visual language, which shortens feedback loops, supports agile delivery, and lets one design serve many channels (web, mobile, chatbots, email, robots). Integrations are mapped visually and anything built once can be reused. Both citizen developers and professional developers benefit.","Work is split across studios aimed at different audiences. **App Studio** is the low-code workspace for case design, data and UX, used by business architects, citizen developers and system architects; best practice is to build there unless a feature is only available elsewhere. **Dev Studio** exposes the underlying rule forms for security, reuse, integration, class mapping and advanced SLA settings. **Prediction Studio** serves data scientists building adaptive, predictive and text models. **Admin Studio** is for IT system administrators monitoring the runtime. Every studio shares the same layout: header, navigation pane (explorers) and authoring space; the studio switcher is in the upper-left and a default studio opens at login.","Because App Studio uses business-friendly names, the same concept has two names. Rules are generated automatically behind App Studio configuration using these names."],"keyFacts":["Low-code means visual and declarative modeling instead of hand-written programming.","Best practice: build in App Studio; move to Dev Studio only when App Studio does not expose the feature.","Dev Studio is needed for advanced SLA options such as initial urgency/delay timers, programmatic interval length, business-day calendars, and escalation actions beyond notifications (reassign, resolve).","Prediction Studio requires the `pxPredictionStudio` portal in the operator's access group.","Admin Studio is for runtime monitoring by IT system administrators.","App Studio navigation: Overview, Case Types, Data, Channels, Library (Reuse Library), Users.","The Application profile tracks features, user stories, bugs and feedback for traceability.","Name mapping: Field = Property, Goal & deadline = SLA, User = Operator, Team = Work Group.","Co-development pattern: a business architect designs views in App Studio, a system architect adds functionality and tests in Dev Studio."],"table":{"headers":["Studio","Primary audience","Typical work"],"rows":[["App Studio","Business architects, citizen developers, system architects","Case types, data, UX, personas, low-code configuration"],["Dev Studio","System architects, QA","Rule forms, security, integration, class mapping, advanced SLA, reuse"],["Prediction Studio","Data scientists","Adaptive, predictive and text ML models"],["Admin Studio","IT system administrators","Runtime monitoring (also hosts Deployment Manager)"]]},"examTraps":["Dev Studio is not the default best-practice place to build just because it is more powerful; start in App Studio.","Runtime monitoring belongs to Admin Studio, not Dev Studio.","A User in App Studio is an Operator in Dev Studio, not an access group; a Team is a Work Group.","Not being able to open Prediction Studio is an access group/portal issue, not a licensing of App Studio issue."]},{"objective":"Explain how the Situational Layer Cake, prescriptive design, studio interoperability, and the Reuse Library support modular enterprise reuse","modules":["Modular architecture and enterprise reuse","Promoting rule reuse with relevant records"],"summary":["The **Situational Layer Cake** lets an organization reuse common logic while still supporting variations, avoiding both tangled if-then-else logic and many near-duplicate applications. Rules are organized in layers: Enterprise (platform-wide and company-wide assets such as authentication), Division (assets reusable within a division, for example a claims flow), Application (app-specific), and Implementation (deployment-specific customization such as regional regulation or localization). At runtime Pega picks the most specific applicable rule, so upper layers can specialize without disturbing the shared layers beneath them. Reusable features can be packaged as module applications stacked as built-on applications. A well-known example is an insurer consolidating 52 claim systems into one claims platform.","Modular enterprise reuse pays off in every phase (design, development, testing, deployment, maintenance) and follows five principles: Interoperable, Updateable, Configurable, Modular and Governed (typically through a Center of Excellence). Prescriptive design means App Studio guides builders toward proven patterns (prescriptive, model-driven UI) instead of free-form coding.","**Studio interoperability**: Dev Studio is where advanced applications, reusable components and modules, traditional UI, and automated unit testing are built, and where rules are marked as relevant records. App Studio consumes that work without code through AI-assisted development, prescriptive UI, relevant records and Configuration Sets. The **Reuse Library** (App Studio Library) is a central repository of reusable assets, backed by governance: lifecycle management (centralized or federated), a reuse catalog with tagging and search, deployment and maintenance tracking, value measurement (usage/ROI dashboards), and education and advocacy."],"keyFacts":["Layer cake layers, from shared to specific: Enterprise, Division, Application, Implementation.","Runtime selects the most specific rule available for the situation.","Regional regulation or localization variations belong in the Implementation layer.","Reusable features can be delivered as module applications used as built-on applications.","Five reuse principles: Interoperable, Updateable, Configurable, Modular, Governed.","Dev Studio: reusable components/modules, traditional UI, automated unit tests, marking relevant records.","App Studio: AI-assisted development, prescriptive UI, consuming relevant records, Configuration Sets.","Dev Studio can widen a rule's scope from a case type to the application, division or organization for reuse.","Reuse Library governance includes usage/ROI dashboards (value measurement) and a tagged, searchable reuse catalog."],"table":{"headers":["Layer","Contains","Example"],"rows":[["Enterprise","Platform and organization-wide assets","Authentication"],["Division","Assets reusable across a division","A shared claims flow"],["Application","Application-specific rules","One line-of-business app"],["Implementation","Deployment-specific customization","Regional regulation, localization"]]},"examTraps":["Handling variation with if-then-else conditions inside one rule is exactly what the layer cake avoids.","Copying an application per region is the other anti-pattern; specialize in an upper layer instead.","Configuration Sets and AI-assisted development are App Studio capabilities; building reusable modules and automated unit testing are Dev Studio work.","Know the Reuse Library governance aspects by purpose: catalog = find assets, value measurement = usage/ROI."]},{"objective":"Describe the benefits of using the Center-out architecture","modules":["Pega Center-out architecture"],"summary":["Traditional architectures fail in two ways. Top-down designs start at the channel, so logic gets duplicated in every channel. Bottom-up designs start at the data layer, producing siloed and duplicated data with no single view of the customer. **Center-out** puts the business logic layer at the center: the presentation layer responds dynamically to whichever channel is used, and the data access layer is insulated so that a change of system of record (for example moving from on-premises to cloud) requires no change to the business logic.","Five principles drive center-out: manage intelligence centrally; focus on outcomes and align the process (case management); connect up to the channels/experiences; connect down to the data while keeping logic nimble; and manage variations so the solution is scale-ready (the Situational Layer Cake). The benefits are efficiency (define once), agility (one change applies to all channels), governance (auditing and policy) and scalability (reuse logic for new channels). Model-driven UI completes the picture: the data model and rules generate the UI through reusable patterns, so the UI adapts automatically when rules change."],"keyFacts":["Top-down = channel-first, logic duplicated per channel.","Bottom-up = data-first, siloed duplicated data, no single view.","Center-out = business logic in the middle, channels above, data access below.","Changing the system of record does not require changing business logic.","Benefits: efficiency, agility, governance, scalability.","The fifth principle (manage variations to be scale-ready) is delivered by the Situational Layer Cake.","Model-driven UI uses patterns (forms, lists, dashboards, case UIs) generated from the data model and rules; it is responsive."],"table":{"headers":["Approach","Starts from","Main weakness / strength"],"rows":[["Top-down","Channel / UI","Logic duplicated in each channel"],["Bottom-up","Data layer","Siloed, duplicated data; no single view"],["Center-out","Business logic","Define once, reuse across channels; data layer insulated"]]},"examTraps":["Center-out does not mean the UI drives design; channels are connected up to central logic.","Agility in center-out means one change applies to every channel, not that each channel is changed independently.","A system-of-record move affects the data access layer only, not lifecycles or UI."]},{"objective":"Describe the accessibility features included in Pega Platform","modules":["Pega Center-out architecture"],"summary":["Because Pega generates UI centrally from patterns and templates, accessibility is implemented once and propagated everywhere. Pega supports WCAG 2.1 and Section 508. When a central component is updated, every application that uses it becomes compliant without per-screen edits. In Constellation, accessibility is built in by default, and templates handle concerns such as layout, responsiveness and accessibility.","Supporting tools reinforce this: the Themes landing page flags WCAG color-contrast failures, and UX designers on the project team focus on accessibility and consistency."],"keyFacts":["Supported standards: WCAG 2.1 and Section 508.","Implement once in central UI components/patterns; changes propagate to all apps.","Accessibility is built in by default in Constellation.","Templates handle accessibility along with layout, breakpoints and responsiveness.","The Themes landing page highlights WCAG color-contrast failures.","UX designers are the project role responsible for accessibility and consistency."],"examTraps":["Fixing accessibility screen by screen is the wrong answer; update the central component.","Accessibility is not an optional add-on to enable per view in Constellation; it is default."]},{"objective":"Define the responsibilities of key roles involved in Pega project delivery","modules":["Team application development"],"summary":["A Pega delivery team mixes client and implementation roles. The **Product Owner** (client business) owns scope and prioritizes requirements for sprints. The **Project Manager** leads the delivery team, handling governance, stakeholder liaison, schedule, budget and scope. The **Scrum Master** runs the ceremonies (sprint planning, sizing, backlog refinement, retrospectives) and removes impediments. **System Architects** (SA, SSA, LSA) configure the application; the LSA imports Blueprint files and configures functional and technical requirements and designs the class hierarchy with project leaders.","**Quality Analysts** build test scripts and scenario tests from user stories. **SMEs** supply operational knowledge and walkthroughs. The **Business Architect** translates needs into functional requirements, workflows, data models and UX. **UX Designers** cover user needs, accessibility and consistency. The **Solutions Consultant** works early in the sales cycle, co-designs a Blueprint with the client and hands it to delivery. Most roles work in App Studio; SAs and QAs use Dev Studio, data scientists use Prediction Studio and IT uses Admin Studio."],"keyFacts":["Product Owner: client-side, defines scope and prioritizes requirements per sprint.","Project Manager: governance, liaison, schedule, budget, scope.","Scrum Master: ceremonies and removing impediments.","LSA: imports Blueprint files, configures functional and technical requirements, designs class hierarchy.","Quality Analyst: test scripts and scenario tests from user stories.","Business Architect: translates business needs into functional requirements, workflows, data models, UX.","Solutions Consultant: pre-sales, co-designs the Blueprint, then hands off.","SMEs provide client operational knowledge."],"table":{"headers":["Role","Key responsibility","Typical studio"],"rows":[["Product Owner","Scope and requirement priority","App Studio"],["Project Manager","Governance, schedule, budget","App Studio (documentation)"],["Scrum Master","Ceremonies, remove impediments","App Studio (user stories)"],["System Architect / LSA","Configure app; LSA imports Blueprint","App Studio and Dev Studio"],["Quality Analyst","Test scripts from user stories","Dev Studio"],["Business Architect","Functional requirements, workflows","App Studio"],["Solutions Consultant","Pre-sales Blueprint co-design","Blueprint"]]},"examTraps":["Prioritizing requirements is the Product Owner, not the Scrum Master or Project Manager.","The Solutions Consultant co-designs the Blueprint but the LSA imports it.","Business Architect (requirements) is not the same as System Architect (configuration)."]},{"objective":"Explain the roles of Workspaces and Branches in development","modules":["Team application development"],"summary":["A **workspace** is a private development space tied to one operator (with developer access) and one application. Each operator gets one default workspace per application and can create more. Changes stay private until they are shared (pushed) into a branch. Workspaces are available in App Studio and Dev Studio, are enabled by the EnableWorkspace setting, and let you pick the application layer for new rules. The flow is Workspace, then Branch, then merge into the Application.","A **branch** is a container for rulesets that are changing rapidly; branch rulesets are based on another ruleset and hold rules in active development. Best practice is one branch per feature, merged only when the feature is complete, conflicts are resolved and approval is given. Before merge, the system checks for updated or missing rules. Branch quality metrics give a merge-readiness picture: guardrail score (weighted), total merge conflicts, test coverage (percentage of rules covered and test pass rate), and uncovered rules.","Collaboration is supported by Pulse (posts to users, teams and case stakeholders; developers open it through the developer collaboration toggle in the upper-right of App Studio; users through a Pulse widget the developer adds to the portal or case) with email notifications for mentions, likes and comments, and by hashtags that a Tags widget can display."],"keyFacts":["Workspace = one operator + one application; private until pushed to a branch.","Default one workspace per operator per application; more can be created.","Workspaces are switched on with the EnableWorkspace setting.","Promotion path: Workspace to Branch to Application merge.","One branch per feature; merge after completion, conflict resolution and approval.","Branch quality metrics: guardrail score, merge conflicts, rules covered % and test pass rate, uncovered rules.","Branch rulesets are based on an existing ruleset.","Replying to a Pulse email notification posts the reply as a comment."],"table":{"headers":["Branch quality metric","What it shows"],"rows":[["Guardrails","Weighted guardrail compliance score"],["Merge conflicts","Total conflicts to resolve before merge"],["Test coverage & unit tests","Percentage of rules covered and test pass rate"],["Uncovered rules","Rules not exercised by any test"]]},"examTraps":["Workspace changes are not visible to teammates until shared to a branch.","A workspace is not shared by a team; it belongs to one operator and one application.","Long-lived per-developer or per-release branches violate the one-branch-per-feature practice."]},{"objective":"Manage application development with Agile Workbench: user stories, feedback, bugs","modules":["Application development management","Collaboration with users"],"summary":["Agile Workbench lives in App Studio (Application profile and the Developer Assistant pane, whose tabs are Guidance, Knowledge, Work and Checklists). It organizes the application into a feature map of features and subfeatures; creating a case type automatically creates a feature with the same name.","Work items are Stories (user stories, which can be bulk-uploaded from Excel), Bugs and Feedback. Items move between To do, Doing and Done by dragging. During playback sessions with stakeholders, the team records bugs and feedback directly. Agile Workbench integrates with Agile Studio, Jira and CA Agile Central, and is recommended after a Blueprint import to generate the backlog."],"keyFacts":["Developer Assistant tabs: Guidance, Knowledge, Work, Checklists.","Creating a case type auto-creates a feature of the same name.","Work item types: Stories, Bugs, Feedback.","Statuses: To do, Doing, Done (drag to change).","User stories can be bulk-uploaded from Excel.","Integrates with Agile Studio, Jira and CA Agile Central.","Use playback sessions to capture bugs and feedback from stakeholders."],"examTraps":["Agile Workbench is an App Studio tool, not Admin Studio.","A defect found in playback is a Bug; a suggestion is Feedback; new scope is a Story.","It integrates with more than just Agile Studio (also Jira, CA Agile Central)."]},{"objective":"Create and maintain rules and rulesets","modules":["Creating a Rule"],"summary":["A rule is an instance of a rule type (a Flow rule models a process). App Studio creates rules automatically: a case type produces a work class, a pyDefault case type rule, a data page and properties; a process becomes a Flow rule; a view becomes a Section rule; a data object produces list, lookup and savable data pages. Modular rules provide versioning (history and revert), delegation (business users change values such as an approval threshold) and reuse; parameterization increases reuse further.","A **ruleset** groups rules (like an album groups songs) and can be shared. Its version is Name:MM-mm-pp (major, minor, patch, each 01–99, starting at 01-01-01). Major versions add substantial functionality (and are created for major platform upgrades), minor versions add enhancements, patch versions fix bugs; older versions should be locked. The ruleset stack is the order Pega searches; each listed version also includes lower minor and patch versions within the same major version, and every application version has its own stack. Names should be meaningful and start with the company plus purpose, must not use a Pega prefix, contain no spaces or special characters, and are at most 32 characters. Develop only in the topmost layer and keep built-on applications locked.","In Dev Studio, rules are created via the Create menu, the App Explorer, a rule reference, Save As or the Records explorer. Save As pre-fills everything but you must change the identifier, the class or the ruleset, otherwise saving fails. Every rule's `.pzInsKey` is built from the rule type's internal name, applies-to class, identifier and creation timestamp in GMT; rule resolution then chooses the right rule at runtime."],"keyFacts":["Ruleset version format: MM-mm-pp, each segment 01–99, first version 01-01-01.","Patch = bug fix, minor = enhancement, major = substantial new functionality or major upgrade.","Lock older ruleset versions; develop in the topmost layer only.","Ruleset names: company + purpose, no Pega prefix, no spaces or special characters, max 32 characters.","A new ruleset's top-level class inherits from @baseclass.","`.pzInsKey` = rule type + applies-to class + identifier + GMT create timestamp (some types add keys, e.g., Correspondence type).","Save As errors unless identifier, class or ruleset is changed.","The Label entered on a new record generates the identifier.","App Studio view = Section rule; process = Flow rule; case type = work class + pyDefault + data page.","Delegation lets business users change rule values (e.g., an auto-approve threshold) in production."],"table":{"headers":["Version segment","Increment when","Example from 02-03-04"],"rows":[["Patch (pp)","Bug fixes","02-03-05"],["Minor (mm)","Enhancements / interim releases","02-04-01"],["Major (MM)","Substantial new functionality, major upgrade","03-01-01"]]},"examTraps":["Abbreviated names such as UPTC are discouraged; use descriptive names like UPlusTelcoContracts.","Listing a version in the stack includes lower minor/patch versions of the same major only, never a different major version.","The .pzInsKey does not include the ruleset version or the creating operator.","Unlocking a locked version to fix a bug is wrong; create the next patch version."]},{"objective":"Classes, inheritance, and relevant records","modules":["Creating a Rule","Promoting rule reuse with relevant records"],"summary":["Classes group rules by purpose: Work classes for case processing, Integration (Int) classes for connectors, and Data classes for data types. A child class inherits its parent's rules; case type classes sit under Work, and a child case type gets a class under its parent's. The hierarchy runs from most to least specific: case type classes, Work, Application, Division/Unit (optional, only when necessary), Organization, other (framework) applications, and Pega base classes. A hyphen separates levels, so TGB-IT-Work is a child of TGB-IT, which is a child of TGB.","**Pattern inheritance** is automatic and based on the class-name prefix; it reuses rules by business suitability. **Directed inheritance** is set explicitly as the parent on the class rule form and is used to reuse Pega standard classes (Work-, Data-) or other applications by functional suitability. Rule resolution searches pattern parents first, then the directed parent (and its pattern parents), repeating up to @baseclass; if nothing is found, an error results. Place reusable rules as high as sensible and parameterize instead of duplicating.","**Relevant records** make Dev Studio rules available to App Studio builders. Fields, views, processes and user actions created in App Studio are marked automatically; others are marked in Dev Studio via Actions > Mark as relevant record, after confirming the rule is guardrail-compliant. Markable types include properties, flows, flow actions, SLAs, data transforms, decision tables and trees, when rules, validate rules, correspondence, paragraphs, notifications, Pulse feed rules, sections, harnesses, views, strategies and proposition filters. The inventory is at Configure > Application > Inventory > Relevant Records, with Show inactive and Show inherited options; relevant records inherit down the class hierarchy."],"keyFacts":["Three class groups: Work (cases), Integration/Int (connectors), Data (data types).","The Division/Unit class layer is optional and used only when necessary.","Pattern = name prefix, automatic; Directed = explicit parent on the class form.","Search order: pattern parents first, then directed parent, repeat to @baseclass.","Example: .pxCreateOpName in Work-Cover- is reached through directed inheritance.","App Studio-created fields, views, processes and user actions are relevant records automatically.","Manual marking: Actions > Mark as relevant record; verify guardrail compliance first.","An existing Dev Studio SLA appears in App Studio's dropdown only if marked relevant.","Relevant records inherit downward (Insurance-Claims to Insurance-Claims-Auto and -Home).","A relevant proposition filter needs its properties, strategies and when rules relevant for the primary context class."],"table":{"headers":["","Pattern inheritance","Directed inheritance"],"rows":[["How set","Automatically from the class name prefix","Explicitly on the class rule form"],["Reuse basis","Business suitability (org/division/app)","Functional suitability (Pega Work-, Data-, other apps)"],["Search order","Searched first","Searched after pattern parents"],["End point","Both continue up to @baseclass","Both continue up to @baseclass"]]},"examTraps":["Directed is not searched first; pattern parents come first.","App Studio-created fields do not need manual marking as relevant.","Marking as relevant should follow a guardrail check, not precede it blindly.","Division layer is optional, Organization is not."]},{"objective":"Debug application errors","modules":["Debugging application errors"],"summary":["**Tracer** captures runtime events for troubleshooting; it is not for performance analysis because it has heavy impact. Open it from the App Studio runtime toolbar (lower-right toggle) or the Dev Studio developer toolbar. Event colors: gray for activities, orange for flows, decisions and declaratives, light blue for database and cache. Each event shows Good or Fail, and the real root cause may sit before the final failed step. In Constellation, events are grouped by request ID because the DX API is stateless. One Tracer can run per operator per node.","The toolbar offers Pause, Clear and Save, plus Settings (event types, rulesets, break conditions, number of lines, filters such as removing View rules), Breakpoints (pause the thread on an activity; resume with Play or automatically after one hour), Watch (track property value changes, shown in the Property Inspector with old and current values and a yellow highlight) and Remote Tracer. Breakpoints and Watch are not supported for Service rules or Constellation applications.","For Constellation front ends, other tools help: view rule metadata (JSON when the pxEnableC11nDev when rule is true), browser F12 tools (Console, Network for DX API payloads, Application storage), React DevTools, Redux DevTools (actions, state, time travel), XRay toggled with `PCore.getDebugger().toggleXRay(true)`, the DebugPegaAPI setting for verbose DX API logs, `PCore.getDataPageUtils()` for client-side data pages, and UI Inspector. PCore/PConnect are not available inside the App Studio shell, so launch the portal from Dev Studio."],"keyFacts":["Tracer is for troubleshooting, not performance analysis.","Colors: gray = activity, orange = flow/decision/declarative, light blue = database and cache.","Root cause can occur before the last failed event.","Constellation events are grouped by request ID.","One Tracer per operator per node.","Breakpoints pause the thread; resume on Play or after one hour.","Watch shows old vs current values in the Property Inspector with yellow highlight.","Breakpoints and Watch are unsupported for Service rules and Constellation apps.","XRay: `PCore.getDebugger().toggleXRay(true/false)`; DebugPegaAPI = true gives verbose DX API logs.","PCore/PConnect are unavailable in the App Studio shell; launch the portal from Dev Studio."],"table":{"headers":["Tracer color","Event types"],"rows":[["Gray","Activities"],["Orange","Flows, decisions, declaratives"],["Light blue","Database and cache"]]},"examTraps":["Using Tracer to measure performance is wrong; it adds heavy overhead.","Breakpoints do not wait forever; they resume after one hour.","Do not expect Breakpoints/Watch to work for a Constellation app.","The final red Fail line is not necessarily the root cause."]},{"objective":"Configuration Sets (supporting topic)","modules":["Creating and setting application variables"],"summary":["Configuration sets and settings provide a unified framework for parameters that control application behavior, reducing the need for rule overrides and replacing Dynamic System Settings. Typical uses: toggling features (disable outbound email until the integration is finished), steering process paths (an approval monetary limit) and UI behavior (show instructions for a new process). They are managed in App Studio on the Settings tab, Configurations landing page, and referenced via the Condition Builder.","Settings are instances of Data-Configuration-Setting; sets live in the Pega-Configuration class. A set does not appear until it holds at least one setting, and its name (pyLabel) can be up to 64 characters. Access: SysAdm4 can read, write and delete both sets and settings; WorkMgr4 can read/write/delete sets but only read settings; User4 can only read both. They travel in the product file when Include associated data is selected."],"keyFacts":["Configuration settings replace Dynamic System Settings for app behavior parameters.","Location: App Studio Settings tab > Configurations landing page.","Settings = Data-Configuration-Setting; sets = Pega-Configuration class.","A set is hidden until it contains at least one setting.","Set name (pyLabel) max 64 characters.","Roles: SysAdm4 full on both; WorkMgr4 full on sets, read settings; User4 read only.","Packaged when Include associated data is checked on the product file.","Referenced in logic through the Condition Builder."],"examTraps":["A newly created empty set not showing is expected behavior, not a bug.","Do not reach for Dynamic System Settings; configuration settings replace them.","WorkMgr4 cannot edit settings, only sets."]}],"questions":[{"id":"ad-001","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"A citizen developer builds a Claims Case Type in App Studio. The Assess Damage step needs an SLA that uses a business-days calendar and holds the assignment with a delay timer before it is routed. Which approach follows Pega best practice?","options":["Configure the goal and deadline in App Studio, then add the advanced options to the generated SLA rule in Dev Studio.","Configure the business-days calendar and delay timer in the App Studio Goal & deadline settings.","Rebuild the whole Case Type in Dev Studio because one requirement needs it.","Configure the SLA in Admin Studio, where runtime timing is managed."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Best practice is to build in App Studio and move to Dev Studio only for what App Studio does not expose. App Studio creates an SLA rule behind its Goal & deadline settings, and the business-days calendar and the initialization delay timer are Dev Studio options on that rule. App Studio does not offer either option. Rebuilding the whole Case Type in Dev Studio ignores the App Studio-first practice. Admin Studio is for runtime monitoring by IT system administrators, not for designing SLAs.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-002","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"A business architect asks a Dev Studio developer to add a new adjuster to the Claims team and to tighten the Goal & deadline on the Review step. Which two Dev Studio records hold these settings?","options":["An Access Group and a Service Level Agreement rule","A Work Group and a Service Level Agreement rule","A Work Group and a Decision table","An Access Role and a Flow rule"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"In Dev Studio terms, a Team is a Work Group and a Goal & deadline is an SLA (Service Level Agreement) rule, and the adjuster is an Operator (User). An Access Group controls authorization, not team membership. A Decision table holds conditional results, not service levels. An Access Role describes a job function's permissions, and a Flow rule models a Process, so neither one holds team membership or the step's timing.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ad-003","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"A global insurer has three layers of shared logic: authentication used by every business line, a claims intake flow reused by its property and auto claims applications, and Germany-only regulatory checks for auto claims. In the Situational Layer Cake, where does the claims intake flow belong?","options":["Enterprise layer","Division layer","Application layer","Implementation layer"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The claims intake flow is reused by several applications within one division (claims), so it belongs in the Division layer, which holds division-specific reusable assets. The Enterprise layer is for platform and company-wide assets such as authentication. The Application layer holds rules specific to one application, but this flow is shared by two applications. The Implementation layer holds deployment-specific variations such as the German regulatory checks.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-004","objective":1,"type":"multi","stem":"A Center of Excellence is splitting work between professional developers and citizen developers. Under Pega's studio interoperability model, which two tasks belong in Dev Studio? (Choose Two)","options":["Marking rules as relevant records for App Studio users","Building reusable modules and automated unit tests","Adjusting application behavior through Configuration Sets","Using relevant records while configuring prescriptive UI"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Dev Studio is where developers build advanced applications, reusable components and modules, automated unit tests and traditional UI, and where they mark rules as relevant records. Configuration Sets are configured in App Studio (Settings tab > Configurations). Using relevant records while building with prescriptive UI is the App Studio side of the model: Dev Studio makes the records available, and App Studio uses them.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-005","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"Three applications at a bank each need the same meeting-scheduling feature. A developer proposes copying the rules into each application and adding if-then-else conditions for small differences. What is the better modular reuse approach?","options":["Package the feature as a module application and add it as a built-on application to each app.","Copy the rules into each application's Implementation layer so each team can edit them.","Mark the rules as relevant records in one application so the other two can see them.","Keep one copy in the first application and branch the logic with When rules for the others."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Reusable features are delivered as module applications that are stacked as built-on applications, as with the Meeting module example. The Situational Layer Cake exists to avoid both duplicate applications and if-then-else spaghetti. Copying rules into each application duplicates logic. Relevant records make rules visible to App Studio users in an application's own class hierarchy, so they do not share rules across applications. Branching one copy with When rules is the condition-heavy design the Layer Cake avoids.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-006","objective":2,"type":"multi","stem":"A bank built its loan application using center-out architecture. This year it moves the customer system of record from on-premises to the cloud and adds a chat channel. Which two outcomes does center-out architecture predict? (Choose Two)","options":["The data access layer changes while the loan business logic stays the same.","The chat channel reuses the loan logic that is already defined once at the center.","The chat channel needs its own copy of the eligibility rules to respond correctly.","The business logic is rebuilt to fit the new cloud data structures."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"In center-out architecture, business logic sits in the middle. The data access layer is insulated, so a change of system of record, such as on-premises to cloud, needs no change to the logic. Channels connect from above and reuse the same logic, so it is defined once and the change applies everywhere. A separate copy of the rules per channel describes the top-down approach. Rebuilding logic around data structures reflects the data-centric bottom-up approach.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ad-007","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"An organization runs 30 Constellation applications that must meet WCAG 2.1 and Section 508. A new accessibility requirement changes how a common input pattern behaves. Which approach requires the least effort?","options":["Update each View that uses the input in all 30 applications.","Update the central UI component so that the change reaches every application.","Save a new theme in each application's Implementation layer with accessible styles.","Run the Themes landing page check in each application and fix the screens it flags."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Pega generates UI centrally from patterns, so accessibility is implemented once in the central components and propagates to all applications without per-screen changes. Editing each View in 30 applications is the per-screen work that model-driven UI avoids. A theme controls styling, and themes do not support inheritance, so a theme per application does not change how an input pattern behaves. The Themes landing page flags WCAG color-contrast failures only, not input behavior.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-008","objective":4,"type":"single","stem":"During pre-sales, a team co-designed a Blueprint with the client. The deal is signed and a delivery team is formed. Who imports the Blueprint file and configures the functional and technical requirements?","options":["Solutions Consultant","Lead System Architect","Business Architect","Product Owner"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The Lead System Architect imports Blueprint files and configures the functional and technical requirements. The Solutions Consultant works in early sales and co-designs the Blueprint, then hands it to the delivery team. The Business Architect turns business needs into functional requirements, workflows, data models and UX, but does not import the Blueprint. The Product Owner is on the client side, defines scope and prioritizes requirements for each sprint.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ad-009","objective":4,"type":"multi","stem":"A new delivery team is writing its RACI chart. Which two role assignments are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Product Owner: prioritizes the requirements for each sprint","Quality Analyst: writes test scripts and scenario tests from user stories","Scrum Master: manages the project budget, schedule and scope","Business Architect: designs the class hierarchy and imports the Blueprint"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"The Product Owner represents the client business, defines scope and prioritizes requirements for each sprint. Quality Analysts build test scripts and scenario tests from user stories. Budget, schedule and scope belong to the Project Manager, while the Scrum Master runs the Scrum ceremonies and removes impediments. Designing the class hierarchy and importing Blueprint files is Lead System Architect work. The Business Architect translates business needs into functional requirements.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-010","objective":5,"type":"single","stem":"Developer Maya configured a new View in her workspace for the Onboarding application. A teammate working on the same application cannot see the View. Which statement about Maya's change is correct?","options":["It stays private to Maya until she shares it to a Branch.","It is visible to every developer on the application as soon as it is saved.","It is merged into the application rulesets as soon as it is saved.","It is visible to other developers in the same Access Group."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A workspace is a private development space for one operator and one application. Changes stay private until they are shared (pushed) to a Branch, and the Branch is later merged into the application. Saving does not publish the change to other developers, and it does not skip the Branch and go straight into the application. Membership of an Access Group does not affect what can be seen in another operator's workspace.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ad-011","objective":5,"type":"multi","stem":"A team lead is writing workspace guidance for new developers. Which two statements are accurate? (Choose Two)","options":["A workspace is tied to one operator and one application.","An operator can create more than one workspace for the same application.","Each operator is limited to one workspace for each application.","Workspaces are available only in Dev Studio."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Each workspace belongs to exactly one operator with developer access and to one application. By default each operator has one workspace per application, and more can be created. The statement about a limit of one workspace per application confuses the default with a limit. Workspaces are available in both App Studio and Dev Studio, and they are switched on with the EnableWorkspace setting.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-012","objective":5,"type":"multi","stem":"Before approving a merge, a team lead opens the quality metrics for the ClaimsIntake Branch. Which two metrics are reported? (Choose Two)","options":["The total number of merge conflicts","The percentage of rules covered and the unit test pass rate","An unweighted count of guardrail warnings","The number of locked ruleset versions in the stack"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Branch quality metrics include the weighted guardrail score, the total number of merge conflicts, test coverage and unit test results (rules covered percentage and test pass rate), and the uncovered rules. The guardrail metric is a weighted score, not an unweighted count of warnings. Locked ruleset versions are a ruleset management practice, not a Branch quality metric.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-013","objective":5,"type":"single","stem":"A team is building the Claims Intake feature and the Payment Calculation feature at the same time. Payment Calculation will take two more sprints. Which Branch strategy follows Pega guidance?","options":["Use one Branch for the sprint and merge everything at the end of each sprint.","Use one Branch per developer and merge each Branch daily.","Use one Branch per feature and merge each when it is complete, conflict-free and approved.","Use one Branch per feature and merge each as soon as any rule passes its unit tests."],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Pega recommends one Branch per feature, merged only when the feature is complete, merge conflicts are resolved and the merge is approved. The system also checks for updated or missing rules before merging. A single sprint Branch would push the unfinished Payment Calculation work into the application. A Branch per developer does not separate work by feature. Merging when one rule passes its tests ignores the completion and approval criteria.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-014","objective":6,"type":"single","stem":"During a playback session, one stakeholder says the worklist would be easier to use if it were sorted by due date. Another reports that Submit on the address form saves the wrong postal code. How should the team record these in Agile Workbench?","options":["As Feedback for the sorting suggestion and a Bug for the postal code","As Bugs for both, because both came from a playback session","As a user story for the postal code and a Bug for the sorting suggestion","As Feedback for both, because neither was reported in production"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Agile Workbench has three work item types: Stories, Bugs and Feedback. Playbacks are used to capture bugs and feedback from stakeholders. A suggestion about how the worklist should behave is Feedback, and a form that saves an incorrect value is a defect, so it is a Bug. Where an item was found does not decide its type. The postal code problem is not a new user story, and the sorting suggestion is not a defect.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-015","objective":6,"type":"single","stem":"A Product Owner has 80 user stories in a spreadsheet and wants the team to track them in Agile Workbench. Which statement is correct?","options":["The stories can be bulk-uploaded from Excel and then tracked through To do, Doing and Done.","Agile Workbench stores only Bugs and Feedback, so the stories must stay in Jira.","Each story must be created by hand because Agile Workbench has no bulk upload.","The stories are imported through the Dev Studio Records explorer."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Agile Workbench, opened from the App Studio Application profile, supports bulk upload of user stories from Excel. Work items move through To do, Doing and Done, and you drag an item to change its status. Stories are one of its three work item types, and it integrates with Jira rather than relying on it. Bulk upload is supported. The Dev Studio Records explorer is not where Agile Workbench items are managed.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ad-016","objective":7,"type":"single","stem":"The highest version of ruleset ClaimsMain is 02-03-04, and it is locked. A defect in a calculation must be fixed, with no new functionality. Where should the fix go?","options":["ClaimsMain:02-03-05","ClaimsMain:02-04-01","ClaimsMain:03-01-01","Unlock ClaimsMain:02-03-04 and fix it in place"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Ruleset versions use the format major-minor-patch, and bug fixes increment the patch number, so the fix goes in 02-03-05. A minor increment such as 02-04-01 is for enhancements. A major increment such as 03-01-01 is for substantial new functionality or a major platform upgrade. Older versions should stay locked, so unlocking 02-03-04 to change it goes against best practice.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ad-017","objective":7,"type":"single","stem":"A developer uses Save As on a Decision table, changes only the short description and clicks Create and open. The system shows an error. Which single change allows the copy to be created?","options":["Make the short description longer so the label is unique.","Change the Apply to class to a child class.","Check out the original rule before using Save As.","Enable a delegation option on the Results tab."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Save As pre-fills every key value, and it fails unless the identifier, the Apply to class or the ruleset is changed. Changing the class to a child class meets that requirement and specializes the rule. The short description is not part of the rule's key, so editing it does not help. Checking out the original and setting delegation options do not change the key, so the error remains.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-018","objective":7,"type":"multi","stem":"While troubleshooting, a developer reads the `.pzInsKey` of an SLA rule. Which two values make up part of this key? (Choose Two)","options":["The internal name of the rule type","The creation timestamp in GMT","The ruleset version that holds the rule","The operator ID of the rule's creator"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"`.pzInsKey` combines the internal name of the rule type (such as RULE-OBJ-SERVICELEVEL), the applies-to class, the identifier and the creation timestamp in GMT. Some rule types add more keys, for example the Correspondence type. The ruleset version and the creator's operator ID are not part of the key.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-019","objective":7,"type":"multi","stem":"An application's ruleset stack includes the entry ClaimsMain:02-03. Which two ruleset versions does Pega search through this entry? (Choose Two)","options":["ClaimsMain:02-01-05","ClaimsMain:02-03-02","ClaimsMain:01-09-09","ClaimsMain:02-04-01"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"A ruleset stack entry includes the listed version and every lower minor and patch version within the same major version. That covers 02-03-02 and 02-01-05. Version 01-09-09 belongs to a different major version, so it is outside the entry even though its numbers look higher. Version 02-04-01 is higher than the listed 02-03, so it is not included.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-020","objective":7,"type":"multi","stem":"In App Studio, a business architect creates the Employee Relocation Case Type, adds a Process named Pricing and configures a View for one of its steps. Which two Dev Studio rules does this create? (Choose Two)","options":["A Flow rule for the Pricing Process","A pyDefault Case Type rule in the Case Type's work class","A Section rule in the Data-Portal class for the View","An Activity rule for each step in the Process"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"A new Case Type creates a work class (such as TGB-HRApps-Work-EmployeeRelocation) with a pyDefault Case Type rule, a data page and properties, and each Process becomes a Flow rule. A View does become a Section rule, but its class context is the case class. UI rules in the Data-Portal class come from adding a channel such as Mobile. Steps are not converted into Activity rules.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-021","objective":8,"type":"single","stem":"Class TGB-HR-Work-Relocation uses pattern inheritance and has Work-Cover- as its directed parent. A When rule with the same name is defined in both TGB-HR-Work and Work-Cover-. Which version does rule resolution find first?","options":["The TGB-HR-Work version, because pattern parents are searched before the directed parent","The Work-Cover- version, because the directed parent is searched before pattern parents","The version created most recently, regardless of class","Neither, because the same name in two parent classes causes an error"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Rule resolution searches the pattern parents first (by class name prefix, so TGB-HR-Work), then the directed parent and its pattern parents, repeating up to @baseclass. The TGB-HR-Work version is found first. The directed parent comes after the pattern parents, not before. Creation date does not decide which class wins. A rule with the same name at two levels is normal specialization, not an error. An error happens only when no rule is found.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-022","objective":8,"type":"multi","stem":"An LSA is explaining class inheritance to new developers. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Pattern inheritance follows the class name prefix automatically.","Directed inheritance is set explicitly on the class rule form.","Directed inheritance reuses rules based on business suitability.","The pattern search stops at the organization class."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Pattern inheritance is automatic and based on the class name prefix, with hyphens separating levels. Directed inheritance names a parent explicitly on the class rule form, for example to reuse Pega's Work- or Data- classes or rules from other applications. Business suitability describes pattern inheritance, while directed inheritance is about functional suitability. The search does not stop at the organization class: it continues through directed parents up to @baseclass.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-023","objective":8,"type":"multi","stem":"A senior developer created an SLA rule in Dev Studio. A citizen developer configuring a step in App Studio cannot find it in the list of existing SLAs. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["The SLA must be marked as a relevant record before App Studio lists it.","The developer should confirm the SLA is guardrail-compliant before marking it.","Only SLAs created in App Studio can appear in the App Studio list.","The SLA is marked as relevant from the SLA list in App Studio."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"An existing Dev Studio SLA appears in App Studio only when it is marked as a relevant record. A developer does this in Dev Studio with Actions > Mark as relevant record, after checking that the rule is guardrail-compliant. SLAs are one of the rule types that can be marked manually, so they are not limited to App Studio-created rules. Marking happens in Dev Studio, not from the App Studio list.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-024","objective":8,"type":"single","stem":"A developer marks a Decision table as a relevant record for class Insurance-Claims. Where can App Studio users see it as relevant?","options":["In Insurance-Claims and its child classes, such as Insurance-Claims-Auto and Insurance-Claims-Home","Only in Insurance-Claims, because each child class must mark it separately","In Insurance-Claims and its parent class Insurance","In every class of the application, whatever the hierarchy"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Relevant records inherit down the class hierarchy, so a record marked for Insurance-Claims is relevant in Insurance-Claims-Auto and Insurance-Claims-Home too. Child classes do not need to mark it again. Inheritance runs down the hierarchy, not up to the parent class Insurance. It does not cover classes outside that branch of the hierarchy.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-025","objective":8,"type":"single","stem":"Application TGB-IT has the Case Types TGB-IT-Work-ITTicket and TGB-IT-Work-LaptopRequest. A developer needs a Data Transform that every Case Type in the application can reuse. In which class should it be created?","options":["TGB-IT-Work-ITTicket","TGB-IT-Work","TGB-IT-Data-Asset","@baseclass"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Reusable rules should sit as high in the hierarchy as their use allows. TGB-IT-Work is the parent of every Case Type class in the application, so all of them inherit it through pattern inheritance. TGB-IT-Work-ITTicket is a single Case Type, and LaptopRequest is a separate Case Type that does not inherit from it. A Data class holds data types, not case processing. @baseclass is Pega's ultimate base class, which is far too general for application logic.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ad-026","objective":9,"type":"single","stem":"A developer is debugging a Constellation application. She wants Tracer to pause on a specific activity and to show how a property value changes. What should she expect?","options":["Breakpoints and Watch are not supported, and events are grouped by request ID.","A Breakpoint pauses the thread until she clicks Play or one hour passes.","Watch highlights the old and current values in yellow in the Property Inspector.","Tracer cannot be used with Constellation applications."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Breakpoints and Watch are not supported for Constellation applications or Service rules. Tracer still works, and because the DX API is stateless it groups Constellation events by request ID. The pause-until-Play-or-one-hour behavior and the yellow Old/Current highlighting in the Property Inspector are real features, but they apply only where Breakpoints and Watch are supported. Tracer can still trace Constellation applications.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-027","objective":9,"type":"multi","stem":"A developer is reviewing a Tracer session after a case fails to advance. Which two statements are accurate? (Choose Two)","options":["Light blue events show database and cache operations.","The root cause can appear in an event before the last failed step.","Orange events show activities.","Tracer is the recommended tool for analyzing performance problems."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"In Tracer, light blue marks database and cache events. The root cause of a failure often appears earlier than the final event marked Fail. Gray marks activities, while orange marks flows, decisions and declaratives. Tracer is for troubleshooting runtime events and has a heavy performance impact, so it is not suited to performance analysis.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-028","objective":9,"type":"single","stem":"A developer opens the browser console while the application is running in the App Studio shell and runs `PCore.getDebugger().toggleXRay(true)`. The command fails because PCore is undefined. What should she do?","options":["Set the DebugPegaAPI setting to true and run the command again.","Launch the portal from Dev Studio and run the command there.","Set the pxEnableC11nDev When rule to true.","Turn on Tracer from the Runtime toolbar first."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"PCore and PConnect are not available in the App Studio shell, so the portal must be launched from Dev Studio before running XRay. Setting DebugPegaAPI to true gives verbose DX API logs, but PCore is still undefined. pxEnableC11nDev shows View rule metadata as JSON. Tracer is a separate tool and does not load PCore.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-029","objective":9,"type":"multi","stem":"A developer is troubleshooting a Constellation screen with browser and extension tools. Which two tool and purpose pairs are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Browser Network tab: inspect DX API requests and payloads","Redux DevTools: inspect actions and state with time travel","Redux DevTools: inspect client-side data pages","React DevTools Network tab: inspect DX API payloads"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"The browser Network tab (Fetch/XHR) shows DX API calls and their payloads. Redux DevTools shows actions and state and supports time travel. Client-side data pages are held in a separate datastore, not in Redux, and are read with `PCore.getDataPageUtils()`. React DevTools is for inspecting components, for example through the Components tab, and has no Network tab for DX API traffic.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-030","objective":10,"type":"single","stem":"A manager with the WorkMgr4 role creates a new configuration set, but cannot change the value of an existing setting. What explains this?","options":["WorkMgr4 has full access to configuration sets but read-only access to settings.","Configuration settings can be changed only as Dynamic System Settings in Dev Studio.","A configuration set stays hidden until it contains at least one setting.","Only the User4 role can change configuration settings."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"WorkMgr4 can read, write and delete configuration sets, but can only read settings. SysAdm4 has full access to both, and User4 can only read both. Configuration settings replace Dynamic System Settings and are managed on the Configurations landing page. A set is hidden until it contains a setting, which is true but does not explain why a value cannot be changed. User4 is read-only.","difficulty":"medium"}]},{"id":"insights","title":"Insights","weight":5,"intro":"Insights are Pega's reporting building blocks in Constellation: queries built in Explore Data and saved as tables or charts that can be shared and placed on dashboards, portal landing pages and Full Page case views. Expect questions about where an Insight can and cannot go, simple value charts, and common troubleshooting fixes.","objectives":[{"objective":"Describe the benefits of using Insights","modules":["Exploring application data with Insights"],"summary":["An **Insight** is a rule that turns a data query into a table or chart. Because it is a saved rule rather than a one-off query, it can be shared with other users and reused in several places, such as dashboards, portal landing pages and case pages. Insights come in table-based and chart-based forms.","Insights are built in the Explore Data landing page, which is the preferred reporting experience for Constellation applications. Pega GenAI Autopilot can also help create an Insight from a natural-language request."],"keyFacts":["Insight = a rule that turns a query into a table or chart.","Insights are shareable and reusable across dashboards, portal landing pages and Full Page case views.","Two forms: table-based and chart-based.","Explore Data is the preferred place to build them for Constellation.","Autopilot can create an Insight from natural language."],"examTraps":["An Insight is a saved rule, not a temporary query that disappears after the session.","Insights are a Constellation-oriented feature; Traditional UI apps need extra enablement (see Explore Data)."]},{"objective":"Use the Explore Data landing page to create Insights","modules":["Exploring application data with Insights"],"summary":["The **Explore Data** landing page lets users query, sort, filter and group application data and then save the result as an Insight. It is the preferred option for Constellation applications. Traditional UI applications must enable Constellation UI for landing pages before they can use it. Object names shown in Explore Data come from each class's short description.","A **simple value chart** displays a single headline number. It is produced when only Measures fields are used (no grouping dimensions). Layouts are Highlight, Highlight + summary, and Custom highlight + summary, and conditional formatting can color the value based on thresholds."],"keyFacts":["Explore Data supports query, sort, filter and group, then save as Insight.","Traditional UI apps must enable Constellation UI for landing pages to use Explore Data.","Object names in Explore Data come from the class short description.","Simple value chart appears when only Measures fields are selected.","Simple value layouts: Highlight; Highlight + summary; Custom highlight + summary.","Simple value charts support conditional formatting."],"table":{"headers":["Simple value layout","Shows"],"rows":[["Highlight","The single measure value"],["Highlight + summary","The value plus a summary"],["Custom highlight + summary","A customized highlight plus a summary"]]},"examTraps":["Adding a grouping/dimension field means it is no longer a simple value chart; only measures qualify.","Explore Data is not automatically available to Traditional UI apps.","Odd object names in Explore Data are fixed through the class short description, not the Insight."]},{"objective":"Describe the relationship between Insights, Dashboards, the Dashboards landing page, Portal landing pages, and Full Page Case Views","modules":["Exploring application data with Insights","Configuring Portal content"],"summary":["Insights can be placed in three kinds of location. On a **Full Page case view**, an Insight can only be added as a tab, not directly inside a View. On **portal landing pages**, public Insights can be added to a region. On **dashboards**, Insights are arranged in a template; dashboards have private, shared (users with access, either all users or your access group) or public visibility, and a promoted filter applies across all Insights on the dashboard.","The **Dashboards landing page** is available by default in App Studio and in work portals and is not configured through portal authoring; a default Work metrics dashboard is provided. Dashboards are landing pages made of widgets that summarize KPIs per persona, built from a template with regions (for example a two-column template with Region A and Region B). New dashboards are private by default, and a copy keeps the source dashboard's visibility. Out-of-the-box portal landing pages include Dashboard, Home, Explore Data, My Work and Self-service Main page."],"keyFacts":["Full Page case view: Insights only as tabs, never directly inside a View.","Portal landing pages: only public Insights can be added to a region.","Dashboard visibility: private (default for new), shared, public.","A copied dashboard keeps the source's visibility.","A promoted filter applies to every Insight on a dashboard.","Dashboards landing page is available by default in App Studio and work portals; not set up through portal authoring.","A default Work metrics dashboard exists.","Explore Data and Dashboard are among the out-of-the-box portal landing pages."],"table":{"headers":["Location","How Insights appear","Key constraint"],"rows":[["Full Page case view","As a tab","Not directly inside a View"],["Portal landing page","In a region","Public Insights only"],["Dashboard","In a dashboard template","Visibility private/shared/public; promoted filter spans all Insights"],["Dashboards landing page","Lists dashboards","Available by default in App Studio and work portals"]]},"examTraps":["Insights cannot be embedded in a partial or form View; use a tab on the Full Page case view.","A private Insight cannot be placed on a portal landing page.","New dashboards default to private, not public.","Do not look for the Dashboards landing page in portal authoring."]},{"objective":"Troubleshoot common Insights issues (supporting topic)","modules":["Exploring application data with Insights"],"summary":["Common Insights problems have specific fixes. Repeated login prompts are addressed by adjusting the access token lifetime. Unwanted number separators in table columns are fixed by modifying the UI controls. Strange object names in Explore Data come from class short descriptions. A missing column usually means the backing property must be optimized for reporting (in the parent classes as well as the specific class) or marked as a relevant record.","Stale display issues can come from the browser cache: close pages, use an incognito window or clear session storage. If the Constellation portal or Explore Data fails to load, verify the application settings, alias and authentication types, and the OAuth 2.0 Client Registration rule."],"keyFacts":["Repeated login prompts: adjust the access token lifetime.","Number separators in tables: modify the UI controls.","Explore Data object names: class short description.","Missing column: optimize the property for reporting in parent classes and the specific class, or mark it relevant.","Browser cache issues: close pages, use incognito, clear session storage.","Portal/Explore Data load failures: check application settings, alias, authentication types and the OAuth 2.0 Client Registration rule."],"examTraps":["Optimizing a property only in the specific class may not be enough; parent classes matter too.","Login prompts are a token lifetime issue, not an access role issue.","Load failures point to app settings/auth/OAuth client registration, not to the Insight definition."]}],"questions":[{"id":"in-001","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"Every Monday a regional manager rebuilds the same filtered, grouped list of overdue claims and emails screenshots to colleagues. What removes this repeated work?","options":["Save the Explore Data query as an Insight and share it.","Ask a developer to add the list to the claim's Create Form View.","Mark the claim properties as relevant records.","Turn the list into a Decision table that colleagues can open."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Insights are rules that turn Explore Data queries into tables or charts that can be saved and shared, so the manager builds the list once and colleagues open it directly. A Create Form View collects input when a case is created and does not present a reusable query. Marking properties as relevant records makes them available in App Studio but does not save the query. A Decision table returns results from conditions and is not a reporting tool.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"in-002","objective":0,"type":"multi","stem":"A business lead is evaluating Insights for her operations team. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Insights are rules that turn queries into tables or charts.","Insights can be shared with other users.","Insights can only be charts, so tables must stay in Explore Data.","Insights can be embedded directly in any Form View."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Insights are rules that turn queries into visualizations, and they can be shared. Both table-based and chart-based Insights exist, so tables are not limited to Explore Data. On a case, Insights are placed in tabs of the Full Page View, not directly inside a View such as a Form View.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"in-003","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"A company has an older application built with Traditional UI. Business users want to use Explore Data to build their own Insights. What must happen first?","options":["Enable Constellation UI for landing pages in the application.","Rebuild every Case Type as a Constellation Case Type.","Create a Report Definition for each Insight in Dev Studio.","Add the Explore Data page to the portal through portal authoring."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Explore Data is the preferred approach for Constellation. Traditional UI applications must enable Constellation UI for landing pages before they can use it. Rebuilding every Case Type goes far beyond what is needed. Insights are created from Explore Data queries, not one Report Definition at a time. Adding a page through portal authoring does not meet the Constellation landing page requirement.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"in-004","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"In Explore Data, an analyst wants a tile that shows the total number of open claims as one large number. She selects the Count measure and also adds Region as a column, but the simple value chart is not offered. Why?","options":["A simple value chart needs Measures fields only, so Region must be removed.","The Count measure is not supported, so she must switch to Sum.","Simple value charts are available only after the Insight is added to a Dashboard.","Conditional formatting must be turned on before simple value charts appear."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The Simple Value visualization is available only when the query contains just Measures fields. Adding a grouping column such as Region removes the option. Count is a valid measure. Simple value charts are built in Explore Data and do not have to be placed on a Dashboard first. Conditional formatting is an optional feature of a simple value chart, not a condition for offering one.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"in-005","objective":1,"type":"multi","stem":"A dashboard designer wants a simple value chart that shows the open-claims count as a large number with a supporting summary. Which two layouts provide this? (Choose Two)","options":["Highlight + summary","Custom highlight + summary","Highlight","Summary + trend line"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"The simple value chart has three layouts: Highlight, Highlight + summary, and Custom highlight + summary. Only the two that include a summary meet the requirement. Highlight on its own is a real layout but has no summary. Summary + trend line is not a layout offered for simple value charts.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"in-006","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"Case workers want to see an Insight of related open orders while they work on an order case. Where should the developer add it?","options":["To a tab of the order Case Type's Full Page View","Inside the order's Details Partial View","Inside the Create Form View for the order","To the Pulse view in the summary panel"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"On a case, Insights can be placed only in tabs of the Full Page Case View. They cannot be added directly inside a View, so the Details Partial View and the Create Form View are both wrong. Pulse in the summary panel is for posting and replying to messages in the context of the case, and it does not host Insights.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"in-007","objective":2,"type":"multi","stem":"A team lead is setting up Dashboards for her team. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["A new Dashboard is private by default.","A promoted filter applies to every Insight on the Dashboard.","A copied Dashboard is always private, whatever the visibility of the source.","The Dashboards landing page must be added to each Work Portal through portal authoring."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"New Dashboards are private by default and can be shared with an Access Group or made public. A promoted filter applies across all Insights on the Dashboard. A copy keeps the visibility of its source Dashboard, so it is not always private. The Dashboards landing page is available by default in App Studio and in Work Portals, and it is not configured through portal authoring.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"in-008","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"A developer edits a portal landing page and wants to add an Insight she shared with her Access Group yesterday. It does not appear in the list for the region, but other colleagues' Insights do. What is the most likely reason?","options":["Portal landing pages accept only public Insights, and hers is shared with an Access Group only.","The Insight must be placed on a Dashboard before it can be added to a landing page.","Landing page regions accept only table-based Insights.","The Insight must first be added to a tab of a Full Page View."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Portal landing page regions take public Insights only. Sharing an Insight with an Access Group is a real visibility level, but it is not enough for a landing page. An Insight does not need to be placed on a Dashboard or in a Full Page View tab first. Both table-based and chart-based Insights exist, and the region does not limit them by type.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"in-009","objective":2,"type":"multi","stem":"A new application team is planning where users will see Insights. Which two statements are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["Public Insights can be added to a region on a portal landing page.","The Dashboards landing page, with a default Work metrics Dashboard, is available in Work Portals by default.","An Insight can be embedded directly in a case's Partial View.","A Dashboard is made of Insights placed straight onto the page, without templates or regions."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Public Insights can be placed in a region of a portal landing page. The Dashboards landing page is available by default in App Studio and in Work Portals, and it includes a default Work metrics Dashboard. On a case, Insights appear only in tabs of the Full Page View, not directly inside a View such as a Partial View. Dashboards are built from a template divided into regions that hold widgets, such as the two-column Work metrics layout.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"in-010","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"A developer adds the property RepairCost to a claim Case Type, but it does not appear as a column option in Explore Data. Which action is most likely to fix this?","options":["Optimize the property for reporting in the claim class and in its parent classes.","Optimize the property for reporting only in the specific claim class.","Clear the browser session storage and reopen Explore Data.","Change the claim class's short description."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"When a column is missing, optimize its backing property for reporting in both the parent classes and the specific class, or mark the property as a relevant record. Optimizing it only in the specific class is incomplete. Clearing session storage fixes cached page problems, not a missing property. The class short description sets the object name shown in Explore Data, not which columns are available.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"in-011","objective":3,"type":"multi","stem":"After a configuration change, the Constellation portal and Explore Data fail to load for every user. Which two checks are appropriate? (Choose Two)","options":["Verify the application settings, alias and authentication types.","Verify the OAuth 2.0 Client Registration rule.","Adjust the number separators in the table UI controls.","Update the class short descriptions."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"When the Constellation portal or Explore Data fails to load, check the application settings, alias and authentication types, and the OAuth 2.0 Client Registration rule. Number separators are a table display issue that is fixed by changing UI controls. Short descriptions affect the object names shown in Explore Data and do not stop pages from loading.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"in-012","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"Users browsing Explore Data see a data object listed under an unclear technical name instead of a business-friendly one. What should the developer change?","options":["The class short description","The property's reporting optimization","The access token lifetime","The table's UI controls"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Explore Data takes object names from the class short description, so updating it fixes the label. Optimizing a property for reporting fixes missing columns. The access token lifetime fixes repeated login prompts. UI controls fix number separators in tables.","difficulty":"medium"}]},{"id":"genai","title":"Pega GenAI","weight":10,"intro":"Pega GenAI is a family of tools, and the exam mostly tests whether you can match a need to the right tool: Blueprint for designing a new app, Autopilot for design-time suggestions, Connect for custom GenAI automation steps, Coach for runtime guidance, Knowledge Buddy for answers from your own content, and Agents with Tool rules for autonomous case processing. Blueprint import options and Connect response structures are the most detail-heavy areas.","objectives":[{"objective":"Describe how to leverage Pega GenAI Knowledge Buddy to provide client-specific contextual knowledge and support","modules":["Pega GenAI in Pega Platform"],"summary":["Knowledge Buddy gives users instant answers drawn from an organization's own procedural documents and knowledge bases. Its value is knowledge that is specific to the client and to the context, rather than general internet knowledge. It can be enabled for Intelligent Virtual Assistants (IVA), so conversational channels can serve the same grounded answers. Pega uses it for its own Search Assistant on Pega Community, and GenAI agents can list Knowledge Buddies as context sources.","On the exam, Knowledge Buddy is mainly a distractor or a correct choice in a 'which tool' scenario. Choose it when the need is answering questions from company content. Choose GenAI Coach when the need is case-specific advice on the task in front of the user. Choose Autopilot when the need is design-time help building the application."],"keyFacts":["Knowledge Buddy gives instant answers grounded in the client's own procedures, documents and knowledge bases.","Its distinctive value is knowledge specific to the client and to the context.","Knowledge Buddy can be enabled for IVA (Intelligent Virtual Assistant) channels.","Pega Community's Search Assistant is powered by Knowledge Buddy.","GenAI agents can list Knowledge Buddies (and data pages) as context.","It is a runtime, end-user tool, unlike Blueprint and Autopilot, which are design-time tools."],"table":{"headers":["GenAI tool","When it is used","Purpose"],"rows":[["Blueprint","Before or at the start of the build (SaaS)","Turn an idea into an app design (case types, lifecycles, data objects, personas) and export or import it"],["Autopilot","Design time in App Studio (plus Fill form with AI while testing)","Suggestions for case type names, lifecycles, data models, personas, sample records and Insights"],["Connect Generative AI","Runtime automation step designed in App Studio","Custom GenAI capability through the GenAI gateway, driven by a prompt with a response structure"],["GenAI Coach","Runtime","Expert, case-specific advice on the task the end user is doing"],["Knowledge Buddy","Runtime (including IVA)","Answers from the organization's own documents and knowledge bases"],["Agents and Tool rules","Runtime","Conversational, autonomous case processing; Tool rules add governance, security and compliance"]]},"examTraps":["When the source of truth is the company's own manuals or knowledge base, the answer is Knowledge Buddy, not Coach.","Knowledge Buddy does not design or generate application rules. That is Blueprint or Autopilot."]},{"objective":"Describe the role of Pega Blueprint in transforming application design; create, import, and modify a Blueprint","modules":["Accelerating application building with Pega Blueprint"],"summary":["Blueprint is a free SaaS tool at pega.com/blueprint, and you do not need a Pega instance or login to use it. You start from the My Blueprint dashboard and pick an industry, a sub-industry and a department or function. If yours is not listed, choose Other and describe it. You then choose an application purpose: either pre-built industry templates or Pega GenAI solutions. Next you write a functional description (help is available under 'What should I write?'), enter the organization name and language, and optionally upload supporting documents such as requirements. Over about seven steps you refine case types, case lifecycles (primary and alternate stages, processes and steps), the case data model (fields, field types, descriptions, primary fields, single or multiple records, user input or calculated), data objects and personas (access, channels, release planning and effort), and then review a summary. 'Generate more' asks GenAI for additional case types. Blueprint supports App Studio rather than replacing it.","Best practices: use Notes to record routing, SLA and condition details, use 'Preview my app' to validate the design (this includes a center-out, multichannel preview with chat and dashboards), and after import use Agile Workbench to build the backlog. You can export a PDF summary, a .blueprint file and the revision history, or spin up a Pega instance with the app already imported.","To import, create a new application in App Studio, choose 'Build from a Blueprint', and select the file. You do not need PremBridge. The app is built on Pega Platform with Constellation UI. The wizard has a Blueprint Details tab and then steps for Case Types (build, inherit from an existing one, or don't build), Data Objects (build, inherit, reuse an existing one with no class of its own, or skip), Personas (build or not), and Configurations (class layers, case and data classes). Every generated asset except access roles is created in a branch, and the base rulesets are locked with a password that you must store securely. Download the PDF to keep beside you during the import. To use an existing application in the New Application wizard, save it as a template first."],"keyFacts":["Blueprint is free SaaS at pega.com/blueprint. It needs no Pega instance or login.","Application purpose: pre-built industry templates OR Pega GenAI solutions. Supporting assets such as requirements documents can be uploaded.","Blueprint designs case types, lifecycles (primary and alternate stages), the data model (including primary fields, single or multiple records, user input or calculated), data objects and personas.","Exports: PDF summary, .blueprint file, revision history. You can also spin up a Pega instance with the app imported.","Import path: App Studio, New application, Build from a Blueprint. PremBridge is not required, and the result uses Constellation UI.","Import options. Case types: build, inherit from existing, don't build. Data objects: build, inherit, reuse existing (no class of its own), skip. Personas: build or not.","Everything except **access roles** is created in a branch. Base rulesets are locked with a password you must store securely.","Checklist: Pre-import, Import, Post-import, Review, and Common challenges.","Best practices: Notes for routing, SLAs and conditions; 'Preview my app'; Agile Workbench backlog after import; keep the PDF handy during import.","After import, add users and assign them personas."],"table":{"headers":["Checklist phase / import step","What to do or decide"],"rows":[["Pre-import","Confirm app name, case types, data objects, personas and organization details"],["Import","New app vs. extend an existing one; watch for overlaps and conflicts in rulesets and class inheritance"],["Post-import","Merge branches, update rulesets, set up the developer application"],["Review","Decision shapes, flow actions, locking strategies; data model DB mapping, data pages, property settings"],["Common challenges","Alternate stages, approval steps, automation steps, child case management"],["Import: Case Types","Build / Inherit from existing / Don't build"],["Import: Data Objects","Build / Inherit / Reuse existing (no own class) / Skip"],["Import: Personas","Build / Don't build"],["Import: Configurations","Class layers, case and data classes"]]},"examTraps":["Saying Blueprint needs a Pega instance, a login or PremBridge to import is wrong.","Access roles are the one asset type **not** created in the branch during import.","'Reuse existing' for a data object creates no class of its own. 'Inherit' creates a class that inherits from the existing one.","Blueprint is not limited to templates. It can also generate solutions with GenAI, and 'Generate more' adds case types."]},{"objective":"Analyze the role of Pega GenAI Autopilot in developing applications faster","modules":["Pega GenAI in Pega Platform"],"summary":["Autopilot is the umbrella name for GenAI help at design time in App Studio. It suggests case type names based on the application name and metadata, lifecycle stages and steps, data model fields (including picklist choices), personas with descriptions, and sample data records. It also offers 'Fill form with AI' while you test at runtime and builds Insights from natural-language requests. In '23 and '24 it was inactive by default. You must review every suggestion, because Pega is not responsible for the generated content.","To create a case type, go to the Case Types tab and accept a suggested name. Select 'Use Autopilot to suggest a Life Cycle and Data Model', review the suggested stages and steps, click Next, and review the fields (name, type, ID). Field types can include Text, Date only, Picklist, Currency, Integer, Embedded data and Data reference. For a Data reference field, Autopilot also creates the data object. After you submit, the results appear on the Workflow and Data model tabs. On a data object's Records tab, 'Generate sample records with Autopilot' creates the number of records you choose, with values that match each field type (an Email field gets email-like values). This lets testing continue before the real system of record is integrated. You find persona suggestions under Users > User management, and you add a persona to a case type in the Personas and Channels section of the lifecycle."],"keyFacts":["Autopilot covers GenAI design-time features in App Studio.","It suggests case type names, lifecycle stages and steps, data model fields (including picklist choices), personas, sample records and natural-language Insights.","'Fill form with AI' fills a form while you test at runtime.","A Data reference field suggested by Autopilot causes Autopilot to create that data object.","Sample records come from the data object's Records tab: you choose how many, and values match the field types.","Sample records stand in for a system of record that is not yet integrated, during testing.","Persona suggestions are under Users > User management. Personas are then added to a case type in the lifecycle's Personas and Channels section.","Inactive by default in '23 and '24. Always review suggestions, because Pega is not responsible for generated content."],"table":{"headers":["Need","Where Autopilot helps"],"rows":[["New case type with stages, steps and fields","Case Types tab: suggested name, then 'Use Autopilot to suggest a Life Cycle and Data Model'"],["Realistic test data without a system of record","Data object Records tab: 'Generate sample records with Autopilot'"],["User types for the app","Users > User management: persona suggestions with descriptions"],["Faster manual testing","Fill form with AI at runtime"],["Reports from a plain-language question","Insight from natural language"]]},"examTraps":["Autopilot output is a suggestion. Developers must review it rather than trust it as validated.","Runtime, case-specific expert advice for end users is GenAI Coach, not Autopilot.","Autopilot sample records are for testing. They do not replace configuring the real data source before production."]},{"objective":"Explain how to integrate generative AI features into your application by using Pega GenAI Connect Rules","modules":["Pega GenAI in Pega Platform","Automation shapes in the Case Life Cycle"],"summary":["Connect Generative AI lets you build your own GenAI capabilities, and calls go through the GenAI gateway. In App Studio ('24 and later) it appears as the 'Connect Generative AI' automation step, and you build it in AI Designer. Every Connect starts with a prompt. You can write a custom prompt or reuse an existing prompt from Dev Studio.","The response structure decides how the output lands in the case. Structured-Single maps the response to one field. Structured-List maps it to an embedded data list. Unstructured maps free-form output to a Text (paragraph) field. In every case you must pick a target field and add that field to the UI, or users will not see the result. Best practices: mask sensitive data in prompts, and note that the response comes back in the language of the user's locale."],"keyFacts":["Connect builds custom GenAI capabilities through the GenAI gateway.","In App Studio it is the 'Connect Generative AI' automation step, built in AI Designer.","Each Connect starts from a prompt, either custom or an existing prompt from Dev Studio.","Structured-Single maps to one field.","Structured-List maps to an embedded data list.","Unstructured maps to a Text (paragraph) field.","You need a target field, and that field must be added to a view for the result to show.","Mask sensitive data. Responses come back in the language of the user's locale."],"table":{"headers":["Response structure","Maps to","Typical use"],"rows":[["Structured-Single","One field","Extract one discrete value, such as a category or sentiment"],["Structured-List","An embedded data list","Extract repeating items, such as line items from a document"],["Unstructured","A Text (paragraph) field","Free-form output such as a summary or draft text"]]},"examTraps":["A Structured-List response does not go into a single text field. It needs an embedded data list.","Configuring the step is not enough. If the target field is not on a view, users never see the output.","Connect calls go through the GenAI gateway; they do not call a model directly. Sensitive data should be masked."]},{"objective":"Explain how to guide users through complex processes with custom AI-powered capabilities using Pega GenAI Coach","modules":["Pega GenAI in Pega Platform"],"summary":["GenAI Coach is a runtime tool that gives end users expert advice on the task they are working on, with guidance specific to the current case. It is available as a widget in the utilities pane of a case's Full Page View, beside Attachments, Tags, Followers and the other case utilities.","Separate it from Knowledge Buddy, which answers questions from the organization's own documents and knowledge bases, and from Autopilot, which helps developers at design time. When a question says a worker needs advice in the context of the case they are handling, the answer is Coach."],"keyFacts":["GenAI Coach provides runtime, expert advice on end-user tasks.","Its guidance is specific to the current case.","It is surfaced as the GenAI Coach widget in the Full Page View utilities pane.","It is aimed at end users working cases, not at developers designing the app.","Contrast: Knowledge Buddy grounds answers in the client's own documents, and Autopilot works at design time."],"examTraps":["Coach is not a design-time tool, so do not choose it for generating lifecycles or data models.","Coach is not added as an automation step. Connect Generative AI is the automation step, while Coach is a utilities widget."]},{"objective":"Describe how Pega GenAI Agents and Tool Rules benefit application users","modules":["Pega GenAI in Pega Platform"],"summary":["Agents automate case processing and work conversationally. An agent can run an automation, start a case, call another agent or launch a user action. You give it context by listing data pages and Knowledge Buddies, and it can process uploaded documents. Agents can talk to other agents and use the MCP protocol. Each agent needs a clear description and guardrails so its behavior stays within bounds.","Tool rules supply the governance, security and compliance layer for what agents can use and do. On the exam, link Tool rules to governance and control and link agents to autonomous, conversational case work. Neither one shapes GenAI output into fields; that is the job of a Connect response structure."],"keyFacts":["Agents automate case processing and are conversational.","Agent actions: run an automation, start a case, call another agent, launch a user action.","Context comes from listed data pages and Knowledge Buddies.","Supports agent-to-agent communication and MCP protocols.","Agents need descriptions and guardrails, and they can process uploaded documents.","Tool rules provide governance, security and compliance."],"table":{"headers":["Element","Role"],"rows":[["Agent actions","Run automation, start a case, call another agent, launch a user action"],["Context sources","Data pages and Knowledge Buddies"],["Protocols","Agent-to-agent and MCP"],["Descriptions and guardrails","Define the agent's purpose and keep its behavior within bounds"],["Tool rules","Governance, security and compliance"]]},"examTraps":["Tool rules are about governance, security and compliance. They are not prompts or response structures.","Agents get context from data pages and Knowledge Buddies, not by importing Blueprint files."]}],"questions":[{"id":"ai-001","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"A bank's service representatives, and later its IVA chatbot, need instant answers such as which documents close a deceased customer's account. Answers must come from the bank's own procedure manuals. Which Pega GenAI capability fits best?","options":["Knowledge Buddy","GenAI Coach","Autopilot","A Connect Generative AI step with an Unstructured response"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Knowledge Buddy answers from the client's own procedures, documents and knowledge bases, and it can be enabled for IVA. GenAI Coach gives case-specific advice on the task a user is working, not answers grounded in the bank's manuals across channels. Autopilot is a design-time assistant for developers. A Connect step runs a custom prompt inside a case process and is not a knowledge service for representatives and a chatbot.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ai-002","objective":0,"type":"multi","stem":"An insurer is evaluating Knowledge Buddy for its service operations. Which two statements about Knowledge Buddy are correct? (Choose Two)","options":["It can be enabled for an Intelligent Virtual Assistant channel","Pega Community's Search Assistant is powered by it","It generates case lifecycles from uploaded process documents","It appears as a widget in the Full Page View utilities pane"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Knowledge Buddy can be enabled for IVA, and Pega Community's Search Assistant is built on it. Uploading requirements documents to generate lifecycles is partly real, but that is Blueprint, which takes supporting assets during design. The GenAI Coach widget, not Knowledge Buddy, is the GenAI widget in the Full Page View utilities pane.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-003","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"A team is importing a Blueprint. Its Customer data object duplicates a Customer data object already maintained in an enterprise application the new app is built on. The team wants to use that object as is, without creating a Customer class in the new app. Which import option fits?","options":["Reuse existing","Inherit from existing","Build","Skip"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Reuse existing uses the existing data object and creates no class of its own. Inherit from existing does create a new class, which inherits from the existing one, so it fails the no-new-class requirement. Build creates a new, separate Customer data object. Skip leaves the object out, so the case types would have no Customer data from the import.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-004","objective":1,"type":"multi","stem":"A lead system architect has just finished importing a Blueprint through App Studio's 'Build from a Blueprint' option. Which two statements describe the result? (Choose Two)","options":["The application is built on Pega Platform and uses Constellation UI","Case types, data objects and personas are created in a branch","Access roles are created in the branch together with the other assets","Base rulesets are left unlocked so the team can develop in them directly"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"A Blueprint import builds on Pega Platform with Constellation UI, and the generated assets are created in a branch. Access roles are the one exception, because they are not created in the branch. Base rulesets are locked, and the password Pega provides must be stored securely, so the statement about unlocked rulesets is wrong.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-005","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"Following the Blueprint checklist, a team is merging branches, updating rulesets and setting up a developer application. Which checklist phase are they in?","options":["Post-import","Review","Import","Common challenges"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Merging branches, updating rulesets and setting up the developer application are Post-import tasks. Review covers decision shapes, flow actions, locking strategies and data model details such as database mapping and data pages. Import is where you choose a new application or extend an existing one and watch for overlaps. Common challenges lists areas such as alternate stages, approval steps, automation steps and child case management.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-006","objective":1,"type":"multi","stem":"A solutions consultant wants to co-design an application with a prospect who has no Pega environment, and to reuse the prospect's requirements documents. Which two statements about Pega Blueprint support this? (Choose Two)","options":["It runs as SaaS at pega.com/blueprint without a Pega instance or login","Requirements documents can be uploaded as supporting assets","The exported file must go through PremBridge before it can be imported","It generates designs with GenAI only and offers no industry templates"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Blueprint is free SaaS that needs no Pega instance or login, and it accepts supporting assets such as requirements documents. PremBridge is not required to import a Blueprint file. For application purpose, Blueprint offers pre-built industry templates as well as Pega GenAI solutions, so it is not GenAI only.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-007","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"While designing in Blueprint, an analyst wants to record that the Approval step should route to finance managers and resolve within two days. What is the recommended way to capture this?","options":["Add it to Notes so the delivery team configures it after import","Model it as a Calculated field in the Case Data Model","Give the finance persona a higher access level in the Personas step","Add an alternate stage named Finance Approval to the lifecycle"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Blueprint best practice is to use Notes for routing, SLAs and conditions, so the details travel with the design for the delivery team. A Calculated field captures a derived value, not routing or a deadline. Persona access levels describe what a persona can do, not where a step goes or when it is due. An alternate stage changes the lifecycle structure without recording the routing rule or the two-day target.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ai-008","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"While creating a Purchase Request case type with Autopilot, a developer accepts a suggested field 'Vendor' of type Data reference. No Vendor data object exists yet. What happens when the developer submits?","options":["Autopilot creates the Vendor data object","Submission fails until a Vendor data object is created manually","Vendor is converted to an Embedded data field","Vendor is created as a Text (single line) field"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"When Autopilot suggests a Data reference field, Autopilot creates the referenced data object. Submission does not fail, and the field is not converted. Embedded data and Text (single line) are different field types with different behavior, and Autopilot keeps the type the developer accepted, Data reference.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-009","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"The external customer system is not integrated yet, but testers need 50 Customer records with realistic email addresses to test case flows. What is the fastest App Studio approach?","options":["Generate sample records with Autopilot on the Customer data object's Records tab","Use Fill form with AI fifty times on the Customer data object","Accept Autopilot persona suggestions under Users > User management","Configure a Connect Generative AI step that writes Customer records"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"On the data object's Records tab, Autopilot can generate a chosen number of sample records, and values match the field types, so Email fields get email-style values. These records stand in for the system of record that is not yet integrated during testing. Fill form with AI fills in a form while you test at runtime and does not create a set of records. Persona suggestions are about user types, not data. A Connect step adds runtime GenAI behavior to a case, which is far more work than generating test data.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ai-010","objective":2,"type":"multi","stem":"A product owner asks which features the team will get by turning on Pega GenAI Autopilot in App Studio. Which two capabilities are part of Autopilot? (Choose Two)","options":["Suggesting picklist choices for a data model field","Creating an Insight from a natural-language request","Giving case workers case-specific expert advice during assignments","Extracting invoice line items into an embedded data list during processing"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Autopilot covers design-time GenAI features, including data model suggestions with picklist choices and Insights created from natural language. Case-specific expert advice for end users is GenAI Coach. Extracting line items into an embedded data list while a case runs is a Connect Generative AI step with a Structured-List response.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-011","objective":2,"type":"multi","stem":"A governance board is writing guidelines for Autopilot use on a Pega '24 project. Which two statements should the guidelines include? (Choose Two)","options":["Autopilot suggestions must be reviewed, because Pega is not responsible for generated content","Autopilot sample records replace an unintegrated source for testing, not for production","Autopilot is active by default, so no setup is needed on Pega '24","Accepted persona suggestions are added automatically to every case type's lifecycle"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Autopilot output is a suggestion that developers must review, and Pega is not responsible for the content. Sample records stand in for a system of record that is not yet integrated, so the real data source must still be configured. Autopilot is inactive by default in '23 and '24. Accepted personas still have to be added to a case type in the lifecycle's Personas and Channels section.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-012","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"A developer accepts Autopilot's suggested 'Field Inspector' persona. The persona now needs to be part of the Inspection case type. Where does the developer add it?","options":["The Personas and Channels section of the Inspection lifecycle","Users > User management, where the suggestion was accepted","The Data model tab of the Inspection case type","The Channels landing page, by adding a channel interface"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Autopilot suggests personas under Users > User management, and a persona is then added to a case type in the Personas and Channels section of its lifecycle. User management is where the suggestion was accepted, but it does not attach the persona to a case type. The data model holds fields and data objects. The Channels landing page manages interfaces such as portals and conversational channels, not a case type's personas.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ai-013","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"An accounts payable case receives supplier invoices. A Connect Generative AI step must extract every line item, with description, quantity and unit price, into the case. Which response structure and target fit?","options":["Structured-List mapped to an embedded data list","Structured-Single mapped to a Text (paragraph) field","Unstructured mapped to an embedded data list","Structured-List mapped to a Text (paragraph) field"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Many line items with the same fields form a list, and Structured-List maps to an embedded data list. Structured-Single maps to one field, which cannot hold many rows. Unstructured output is free text that maps to a Text (paragraph) field, not a list. A Structured-List response does not go into a single text field.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-014","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"In a Complaints case, a Connect Generative AI step with an Unstructured response writes a summary of the customer's emails to a Text (paragraph) field. The step runs without errors, but handlers never see the summary. What is the most likely fix?","options":["Add the target field to a view that handlers see","Change the response structure to Structured-Single","Add the GenAI Coach widget to the utilities pane","Rewrite the prompt in Dev Studio instead of AI Designer"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A Connect step needs a target field, and that field must be on a view for users to see the result. Here the step works, so the missing piece is the view. Structured-Single maps to one field but does not change visibility, and a free-form summary is Unstructured output. GenAI Coach is a separate runtime advice widget. Prompts can be custom or come from Dev Studio, and where the prompt lives has nothing to do with whether the output is displayed.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-015","objective":3,"type":"multi","stem":"A system architect is designing a Connect Generative AI step that summarizes medical claim notes for staff in several countries. Which two statements reflect how Connect works and its best practices? (Choose Two)","options":["Responses are returned in the language of the user's locale","Sensitive data such as member IDs should be masked","The step calls the model directly, bypassing the GenAI gateway","Existing Dev Studio prompts cannot be used, so every prompt must be new"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Connect responses come back in the language of the user's locale, and sensitive data should be masked. Connect calls go through the GenAI gateway, not directly to a model. A Connect step starts from a prompt that can be custom or an existing one from Dev Studio, so reuse is allowed.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-016","objective":3,"type":"multi","stem":"A team is mapping the outputs of several Connect Generative AI steps to case fields. Which two pairings of response structure and target are valid? (Choose Two)","options":["Structured-Single mapped to one field, such as Claim category","Unstructured mapped to a Text (paragraph) field","Structured-List mapped to a Text (paragraph) field","Structured-Single mapped to an embedded data list"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Structured-Single maps to a single field, and Unstructured maps to a Text (paragraph) field. Structured-List needs an embedded data list, not a text field. Structured-Single returns one value and does not populate a list.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-017","objective":4,"type":"single","stem":"New claims adjusters want advice on what to check next for the specific claim they have open, while they work their assignments. Which capability fits best?","options":["GenAI Coach","Knowledge Buddy","Autopilot","Blueprint"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"GenAI Coach gives runtime expert advice on end-user tasks, specific to the current case. Knowledge Buddy also works at runtime, but it answers from the client's own documents and knowledge bases rather than giving guidance on the open claim. Autopilot and Blueprint are design-time tools for building the application.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ai-018","objective":4,"type":"single","stem":"A product owner wants GenAI Coach available to case workers on every Service Request case. Where should the system architect make it available?","options":["Add the GenAI Coach widget to the utilities pane of the Full Page View","Add a Connect Generative AI automation step to each stage","Add GenAI Coach to the Details view in the summary panel","Add GenAI Coach to the To do widget in the portal"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"GenAI Coach is one of the widgets available in the utilities pane of the Full Page View, so adding it there makes it available on every case of the type. Connect Generative AI is an automation step for custom GenAI tasks, and Coach is not added as a step. The Details view shows primary fields, and To do is a portal widget that lists worklists and work queues.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-019","objective":5,"type":"multi","stem":"A team is designing a GenAI agent that handles incoming service requests conversationally. Which two actions can the agent take? (Choose Two)","options":["Launch a user action","Call another agent","Import a Blueprint file to gain context","Merge branches into the application ruleset"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Agent actions include running an automation, starting a case, calling another agent and launching a user action. Agents get context from the data pages and Knowledge Buddies listed for them, not from Blueprint files, which are imported at design time to build applications. Merging branches is a development and DevOps task, not something an agent does.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-020","objective":5,"type":"single","stem":"A compliance officer asks how the organization will govern what its GenAI agents may use and do when processing cases, for security and compliance. Which Pega element is designed for this?","options":["Tool rules","The data pages listed as agent context","A Structured-List response structure","A Knowledge Buddy grounded in compliance manuals"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Tool rules provide governance, security and compliance for agents. Data pages listed for an agent give it context, not control. Structured-List is a Connect response structure that maps output to an embedded data list. A Knowledge Buddy could answer questions about compliance policy, but it gives information and does not govern what an agent may do.","difficulty":"hard"}]}]};
